.\" SPDX-License-Identifier: BSD-2-Clause
.Dd October 30, 2026
.Dt BUILD-BSD 8
.Os
.Sh NAME
.Nm build-bsd
.Nd build, stage, and package 5BSD through maintained system targets
.Sh SYNOPSIS
.Nm
.Op Cm plan
.Cm build | world | kernel | stage | packages | sign | verify | inventory | inventory-audit | reconcile | construct | boot-observe | snapshot | deploy | bsdinstall | media | secureboot-test | bazel-test
.Op Ar options
.Nm
.Cm status
.Ar run-directory
.Sh DESCRIPTION
The command is installed as
.Nm build-bsd ;
.Nm bsd-build
is retained as a compatibility alias.
Both names invoke the same compiled frontend and use the same configuration
and build lock.
Backend commands receive separate arguments, without shell
expansion of configuration values.
.Pp
.Nm
builds the world and selected kernel from
.Pa /usr/src
using every logical CPU by default and the standard
.Pa /usr/obj
object root.
It invokes maintained make targets, records per-step logs, stops on failure,
and serializes its own executions with a build lock.
Independent manual make invocations must not overlap the same object tree.
Build, stage, packages and verify do not install an upgrade on the host.
Deploy requires an explicit alternate destination.
No command activates a
boot environment or reboots the machine.
The bsdinstall command explicitly opens the interactive disk installer; its
disk selections can replace an existing system.
.Pp
The normal make buildworld, buildkernel and packages targets remain supported.
A world intended for package publication must be built with
.Va WITH_MALLOC_PRODUCTION=yes ;
development worlds retain debug malloc by default.
The packages make target produces base packages; system-packages completes the
matching base and hardware publication workflow.
.Nm
orchestrates these targets and the explicit signing and verification actions.
Veriexec support is already compiled into GENERIC; signatures and trust policy
are separate inputs, and using this frontend alone does not enable enforcement.
Secure Boot additionally requires signing keys and firmware trust enrollment.
BIP development is dropped. The experimental GENERIC-INTEGRITY profile and
kernel enforcement implementation are archived and are not supported by the
current source tree. Retained image and storage tooling does not establish a
root-resistant immutable installation. Signed package publication, veriexec
support, and Secure Boot each require separate qualification; none alone
establishes BIP. Historical BIP fixture commands require the archived source.
Later BIP-specific descriptions in this manual are retained design context,
not supported enforcement in the active tree.
.Pp
World and kernel compile only the selected component.
Sign finalizes an existing alternate root with an explicit signing key.
The build command is an alias for packages: it compiles world and kernel
and completes the pkgbase publication pipeline.
Stage is a separate private VM/development operation; it compiles and installs
into a new private NO_ROOT directory, runs
distribution and kernel installation there, and finalizes signing.
Packages defaults to a production world/kernel build and invokes
system-packages,
which checks production malloc and kernel/hardware identity, stages base and
hardware separately, and publishes one combined repository.
World package creation rejects conflicting installation metadata before
sorting METALOG and generating package lists.
Each invocation selects fresh world, kernel and source package stages beneath
its run directory in
.Pa /usr/obj/usr/src/build-runs .
Incremental compilation reuses objects, but never reuses a previously signed
installation stage. Failed stages are retained with their run logs.
The repository remains
.Pa /usr/obj/usr/src/repo ;
base-only output does not advance latest.
.Pp
Plan prints the actions without building, creating directories, generating
keys,
or publishing anything.
Status prints a run's current or final state.
Snapshot verifies and copies a completed signed repository using the maintained
consumer verifier.  It requires
.Fl repository ,
.Fl pkg-public-key ,
.Fl package-tool ,
.Fl snapshot-abi ,
and
.Fl snapshot-osversion .
The copied catalogue retains its signature, and every package archive is checked
against it.  The output must be new under
.Pa /usr/obj ;
by default it is the repository subdirectory of the new build run.
The output parent must already exist when an explicit output is supplied.
Snapshot does not select packages, construct a filesystem, authorize a generation,
or change the published latest pointer.  Consumer trust is an explicit public key;
no private signing key is needed.
.Pp
Verify checks all system bundles in an existing signed root,
using native capsign tools and an explicitly trusted public key.
It does not compile world or kernel, install packages, or execute target code.
Bsdinstall verifies and snapshots a completed signed pkgbase repository before
launching the native interactive installer.
It requires both public trust keys,
a terminal, root privileges, and an installed bsdinstall supporting explicit
repository selection and code verification.
It refuses a foreign architecture
and requires the repository to include pkg, the base set and selected kernel.
The installer verifies signed bundles before boot setup.
Preparation, installer logs and recovery files remain beneath build-runs,
including after failure.
Installer creation and VM validation remain separate
from this launch operation.
Media builds disc1.iso, memstick.img, or both from an existing built
world/kernel
and a completed signed publication.
The maintained release targets check
production malloc and exact live-kernel identity, preserve the complete signed
catalogue, and embed only public trust keys.
A new output directory isolates
release staging from the standard world/kernel objects.
The frontend checks
requested artifacts and writes SHA256/SHA512 checksum files.
It does not boot
an image or launch the installer.
Full image boot/install validation is still
required before this new path is considered release-ready.
Logs reside under
.Pa /usr/obj/usr/src/build-runs .
A failed stage is incomplete and must not be installed or published.
.Pp
The separate maintained
.Pa tools/test/daemon-runtime/run-vm.py
runner accepts explicit world and kernel inputs for confined ZFS mechanism tests
with
.Fl -zfs .
The optional
.Fl -scratch-disk-mib
input creates a new private virtual disk for explicit-device import tests.
It never selects or attaches a host disk or pool.
It records test timeouts and cleanup requirements and rejects missing cleanup
evidence.
These tests do not qualify an installed protected generation and are
not a replacement for the Secure Boot or packaged-system upgrade gates.
.Pp
The tool does not silently resume or reuse an incomplete stage.
.Ss Manifest trust selection
The normal world and loader build accepts
.Va WITHOUT_LOADER_EFI_TRUST_DB=yes
and explicit public X.509
.Va TRUST_ANCHORS
through the source configuration selected with
.Fl -src-conf .
This embeds generation trust in the loader and excludes firmware db certificates
from manifest trust, while retaining firmware revocations.
Supply public certificates only; trust-anchor inputs become loader contents.
The build rejects private-key or mixed PEM framing before embedding.
Certificate decoding and trust verification remain separate checks.
The default continues to import firmware db trust.
Changing trust inputs requires rebuilding the loader and its libraries;
the generated trust header is regenerated to prevent stale anchor selection.
.Pp
This selection does not authorize a generation, implement anti-rollback,
or prevent firmware from selecting another trusted loader.
The default disposable-key secureboot-test fixtures exercise firmware-backed
manifest trust. Explicit generation test credentials select compiled-only trust
for a selected case; actual firmware execution is required to qualify it.
Do not count a loader build or signature check as such qualification.
.Ss Historical BIP and Secure Boot qualification
BIP has been dropped.
The descriptions in this subsection describe the former combined fixture,
not supported BIP options or qualification of the current tree.
The runner cleanup and independent Secure Boot qualification remain incomplete.
.Pp
Packaged extension and device permissions are configuration inputs.
BSDExtension requires its packaged allow-list at startup and has no compiled
module grants; BSDDevice ships an empty device grant list.
These files follow normal package inventory, staging and bundle verification.
Parsing and signing them do not qualify protected runtime storage or policy updates.
.Pp
The staged BSDFilesystem provider requires a bounded storage-root bootstrap grant
under BIP; publication does not establish that the authenticated import and grant
path works end to end or that persistent storage has been qualified.
Capsule's packaged configuration grants no persistent storage by default.
Installations must supply an authenticated
.Va storage
policy with explicit devices, pool/dataset GUIDs, recipient, operation rights
and a stable
.Va hostuuid ;
see
.Xr capsule.conf 5 .
Staging does not discover host pools or invent these deployment inputs.
.Pp
BSD Integrity Protection (BIP) protects the operating system\(aqs integrity
by enforcing restrictions that apply even to root.
Secure Boot and code signing are supporting mechanisms.
The internal kernel policy name remains MAC_SYSTEM_INTEGRITY.
.Pp
Secureboot-test cleans and rebuilds the EFI loader in a private object subtree
below its build-run directory, with firmware trust and
mandatory boot-file verification, rebuilds Capsule and SwitchBoard using the
matching world build environment, builds a static guest fixture, then runs a
networkless QEMU Q35/SMM suite against an existing staged root and matching
kernel objects.
It uses all logical CPUs for compilation.
The bootstrap step also builds the runtime linker, used by both the baseline
probe and Capsule/native-service fixtures. Private stage replacement checks
reject symlink parents/leaves and non-file destinations.
With a BIP kernel, a dynamic
setuid probe checks authenticated-root libmap inputs and rejection of writable
and unrelated read-only replacements, including include files, include
directories and a FIFO. Signals and timeouts do not count as policy rejection.
Library hints are checked through dlinfo, and identical writable or foreign
read-only hints replacements and FIFO substitution must be rejected.
A shared test library must load and execute through name lookup, absolute
paths and fdlopen from the authenticated root; identical writable and foreign
read-only copies, and FIFO substitutions, must be rejected on every route.
An identical non-setuid control must load and execute the same library from
ordinary writable and read-only storage using all three APIs, with real and
effective UID 65534 and no credential taint.
These mechanism checks do not qualify separate service-generation mounts,
production hints provisioning or installed-service startup.
The required signed /boot/lua/secureboot.lua policy reads explicit generation
configuration and boots without an interactive menu or command fallback.
Missing required policy or configuration fails closed.
Unsigned ESP settings, ZFS nvstore settings and optional nextboot/local
overrides cannot supply policy in this mode.
It generates disposable PK, KEK and signing keys outside guest images,
enrolls a private OVMF variable store, and checks approved boot, rejection,
revocation and unauthorized variable changes by guest root.
It never changes host firmware or publishes these test credentials.
.Pp
This action requires native amd64, QEMU with Secure Boot OVMF, OpenSSL,
uefisign, makefs, mkimg, Python 3.12 and virt-fw-vars (tested with virt-firmware
26.10).
Dependencies must already be installed; the command does not install packages.
Use
.Fl -root
for an existing stage under /usr/obj and
.Fl -output
for a new result directory under /usr/obj.
The result.json report records each test and its evidence.
A final retained-evidence step requires complete case coverage and rehashes the
recorded inputs, logs, firmware variables, disk and root images.
For paired provider boots it also checks the storage and firmware handoff.
It writes evidence-verification.json without replacing an existing receipt.
This is a consistency check of trusted-runner evidence, not a signed attestation
or installed-system qualification.
Supplying both storage inputs adds the storage-anchor case to the normal matrix.
Adding
.Fl -filesystem-policy
selects the actual Capsule/SwitchBoard/BSDFilesystem integration case instead.
The protected kernel must include BIP and have a matching built ZFS module.
The bootstrap build also builds the storage fixture with matching world
libraries.
See
.Pa tools/test/secureboot/README.md
for disposable disk preparation and the remaining integration boundaries.
The selected loader is an explicit, hashed runner input.
Firmware-verification compiler options do not alter the shared world objects
or the BIOS loader used by a subsequent incremental publication.
An incomplete run is not a passing qualification.
The fixture boots PID 1 from a whole-image authenticated UFS preload and checks
read-only access plus denial of forced detach and resize.
It also requires root process-debug isolation between separate authority-bearing
executions of the same approved image, while preserving ordinary fork behavior.
The same kernel fixture requires immutable invocation-bound channel checks:
owner use, fork and successful-exec reuse denial, credential independence,
failed-exec preservation and software revocation.
With BIP, the static fixture also requires system-operation authority checks:
ordinary root cannot claim gates, inherit PID 1 owner authority, mint or release
its claims, or bypass delegation through raw clock-write syscalls.
An explicitly delegated clock token must work and lose authority when closed.
The Capsule fixture repeats the root denial checks from its rc child and
requires denial of its unauthorized raw poweroff attempt.
The host then stops that VM after all assertions and manager READY; this cleanup
is reported separately and does not qualify authorized guest shutdown.
These tests do not qualify production time-service or user authorization policy.
The bootstrap backend rebuilds libpam and stages that exact shared library.
Its native fixture also requires PAM module-resolution checks: rejected
candidates stop lookup, absent candidates permit fallback, and explicit numeric
versions select the named module.
It also requires PAM include-cycle, nesting and expansion rejection, while
preserving valid repeated includes, combined-file services and recovery after
failed configuration.
The bootstrap backend also rebuilds pwd_mkdb and runs its maintained account
database tests as UID 65534 in isolated guest scratch directories.
They check incremental UID records, duplicate-UID ownership and rejection of
malformed updates against actual pwd.db and spwd.db contents.
The retired session fixture no longer requires a host account database generator.
This does not qualify complete PAM/NSS/account dependency integrity or a
protected credential-update transaction.
.Pp
The populated native fixture also exercises actual SwitchBoard status channels
and administrative denial for an unprivileged software grant running as UID 0.
The native fixture additionally enrolls a test-only Core issuer to exercise
independent management and lifecycle roles, read-only principals, lease closure,
expiry, cached channels, and software eligibility independently of session rights.
Signed images containing duplicate or embedded-NUL session-policy keys,
unknown session rights, or include directives must reject startup.
Signed images with missing Capsule policy, duplicate or embedded-NUL keys,
unknown fields, invalid field types or include directives must reject before
service startup without entering a recovery shell.
The bootstrap build rebuilds libcapsulert and the image consumes that rebuilt
library so claim-parser changes are included even when using an older stage.
The ordinary world tests include Capsule policy and shared claim-parser checks
for selected inputs, atomic rejection and bounded descriptor parsing.
The Capsule fixture also loads the shipped storage policy through the actual
BSDFilesystem parser from the authenticated read-only root, rejects a mutable
policy descriptor, and requires BIP status reads to work in capability mode
while policy writes remain denied.
The ordinary world test build includes BSDFilesystem config_test for schema,
duplicate, include, type, range, descriptor and selected-input checks.
These checks do not qualify persistent dataset delegation or normal system startup.
The networkless fixture also does not qualify SSH or network-administration
mediation; legacy routing/interface privileges remain an explicit installed-system
acceptance gate.
The maintained bootstrap step rebuilds libservice, pam_unix, and the session
personal-service and principal-provider fixtures.
Two unsigned personal bundles with the same label exercise explicit start,
owner scope, cached management denial after logout, and confinement of requests
for root credentials and Core status. Root and same-UID processes must fail
ambient signal and debugger access to these protected services.
Guest ownership comes from the fixture METALOG; no host account changes occur.
Both personal services must report readiness after checking their delegated
volatile containers: ownership, create/read/fsync/rename/delete and denied
parent/global traversal in capability mode.
Persistent personal storage remains a separate acceptance gate.
The native fixture's rc software grants come from the explicit
policy/boot-authority.ucl test input, whose digest is recorded with the run.
These fixture grants are not installed as production authorization policy.
The protected discovery test also grants the rc probe software eligibility for
the personal endpoint, then requires denial after root changes to each owner's
UNIX UID without a session. The authenticated issuer, still UID 0, must reach
the correct same-named provider for each session, including on-demand activation.
New lookup requests after logout must fail. Already-delivered provider channels
and their duplicates must become unusable immediately after logout and on expiry.
The static kernel fixture also requires independent software and lifetime checks,
queued-message refusal, blocked-receive wakeups, kqueue EOF, immutable binding,
last-duplicate semantics, issuer/control closure, inheritance restrictions,
and repeated lease teardown. These checks run under actual UEFI Secure Boot.
The native fixture loads the shipped 5bsd-authenticate PAM policy and requires
it to deny authentication and account management until an owner supplies
an authenticated protected-account policy.
A separate explicit vm-ordinary-password test policy loads staged PAM modules
against synthetic ordinary account records.
It checks passwords (including hashes of empty passwords), account expiration,
required password changes, rejection of
UID impersonation, and broker denial of authenticated but unenrolled principals.
The fixture supplies its own password conversation and is not a trusted login UI.
.Pp
A separate test policy selects pam_unix's
.Cm protected_passwd
option with an explicit immutable secret credential file.
The fixture checks correct and incorrect passwords, missing protected users,
locked accounts, empty passwords, expiry, and independent session enrollment.
Protected remote-account cases accept numeric IPv4/IPv6 peers and reject
hostnames and invalid addresses; absent or empty peers retain local semantics.
These supplied peer values do not establish a trusted connection origin.
A paired login-class case checks ordinary default fallback and protected
rejection of the same missing class.
A root-owned copy of valid credentials on writable storage must be rejected by
both authentication and account management; protected positive controls follow.
These are synthetic credential tests, not production enrollment or proof that
ordinary account databases may safely become writable.
.Pp
The shipped 5bsd-authenticate policy denies all operations by default.
An installation enabling protected authentication must supply its protected PAM
policy and credential file as explicit,
hash-bound installation-policy inputs, keep login-class policy immutable, and
qualify the resulting complete generation.
The existing installation-policy file creation and replacement mechanism
applies;
using build-bsd alone does not select a credential store or enroll a user.
.Pp
An independent provider fixture checks broker-attested principal metadata,
denied anonymous root access, distinct permissions for principals sharing a UID,
object ownership and denial of software-only provider ADMIN bypass.
The native result requires PROVIDER_PRINCIPAL_PASS.
This qualifies the metadata mechanism and a test operation policy;
production providers still require their explicit operation and resource policies.
The assembler uses the freshly rebuilt libservice for its new session API;
other dependencies come from the matching world stage.
These checks do not establish human authentication, protected login input,
full user-service authorization or production authenticator enrollment.
A kernel built with MAC_SYSTEM_INTEGRITY, such as the experimental
GENERIC-INTEGRITY profile, additionally requires BIP bootstrap root-attack
checks, rejects a mutable preload or disabled Secure Boot, and ignores alternate
root selection. The runner checks the embedded kernel configuration, not just
the configuration name. Plans report bootstrap_integrity_verification as
kernel-dependent; the result records which policy actually ran.
The protected-profile fixture also checks protection at process creation,
before initialization and across exec/fork, explicit process-descriptor lifecycle
control, and core-dump suppression with an ordinary core-dump control.
An additional protected-profile case boots the actual dynamically linked
Capsule and SwitchBoard, with their runtime loader and shared-library closure
copied from the staged world into a whole-image authenticated UFS preload.
A bounded rc probe checks denied root interference and immutable code, then
allows rc to complete. Success requires Capsule's authenticated READY receipt
and EFI shutdown. These baseline cases use an empty catalogue.
A populated case builds and signs the actual BSDAudit and BSDNotify bundles,
using a native host signer and a disposable code key outside the guest.
The maintained stage signing and verification helpers enforce the exact code
trust catalogue. A scoped rc client performs a notification round trip and
checks that UID 0 cannot publish system notifications or acquire the gated
endpoint without a grant. This is boot software authority, not user login
authorization. The probe also checks provider capability mode, root interference
denials, and process-local OOM exclusion.
A companion case authenticates a root image containing a tampered signed
provider; the manager must reject its catalogue before rc or READY.
General installed-world services and other writable data mounts
remain separate qualification work.
PID 1 delegates only console output before protected process creation.
The descriptor permits write and stat operations; input and terminal ioctls
are denied even through duplicates held by UID 0.
Protected rc reads from /dev/null and writes to this diagnostic stream.
The VM probe checks these restrictions and attempted rights widening.
This is not a trusted login display: diagnostic output is still shared.
Protected interactive maintenance requires a separate authenticated path.
Capsule prepares /Capabilities/Run as a private bounded tmpfs and retains its
kernel directory claim across manager lifetimes. SwitchBoard receives a scoped
descriptor grant, locks inheritance and transfer, and prepares runtime metadata.
Root descendants must fail namespace attacks and must not inherit the grant.
Capsule also prepares an empty /tmp as a 64 MiB sticky nosuid/noexec tmpfs.
Missing and nonempty mountpoints must be rejected before manager startup.
The guest checks root alias/replacement attacks, denied executable mappings,
and real shell, cat, cp, mv, rm, mkdir, mktemp and sort operations as UID 65534.
The result.json and programs.txt reports distinguish exercised operations,
READY-only services, test fixtures and programs without execution evidence.
Image inclusion alone is not a program compatibility pass.
Persistent /var/tmp, third-party package installation and user storage remain
unqualified. Package manifests are inventory inputs; production writable
exceptions require authenticated system policy, not a root-writable pkg database.
An additional signed-configuration case requests isolation off; the mandatory
kernel must keep enforcement enabled. File-handle APIs and NFS server
administration are restricted to bootstrap to prevent traversal bypasses.
PID 1 re-exec and RB_REROOT are denied after the first image starts, preserving
its confined claims until a new authenticated boot. The guest tests both paths
and retains ordinary child exec and EFI shutdown as positive controls.
The profile reserves device acquisition, mount changes and sysctl writes for
trusted bootstrap and is not yet suitable for a general installed world.
These tests cover firmware, loader, root-image authentication and this bootstrap floor; they do not
qualify complete runtime integrity, user authorization, recovery or rollback resistance.
.Ss Package manager qualification
The selected bootstrap package and its SHA256 remain explicit publication inputs.
After changing the package manager, rebuild and qualify that archive before
selecting it; update any generation database engine contract to match.
A previously selected archive is not replaced implicitly by a newer ports recipe.
.Ss Hardware firmware layout
Raw Bluetooth firmware in the custom hardware ports uses the base loader
locations under /usr/share/firmware. Rebuild base devd configuration and
hardware packages together when changing this layout. Locally modified devd
configuration needs explicit upgrade review; package configuration preservation
does not establish migration to the new defaults.
.Ss Package-derived generation inputs
Deploy inventories the exact archives fetched through its authenticated package
catalogue before creating a destination or boot environment.
It first snapshots the publication and explicit public keys into its private
run directory; inventory and installation use those same inputs.
This requires a trusted builder and does not provide a protected live updater.
Fetched packages must be reachable from the selected roots, with no missing
named dependencies, duplicate identities, origin substitutions or missing
advertised shared-library providers.
A dependency's recorded build version is not an exact version pin.
This closure check does not authorize a downgrade or replace generation approval.
It records package identities, archive and payload hashes, file types, numeric
and named ownership, modes, flags, timestamps, links and declared directories
in package-inventory.json beneath its retained deployment run directory.
It rejects duplicate payload paths, manifest/payload disagreement and conflicting
ownership or directory metadata.
Every directory declaration is validated against archive headers and other
packages, including declarations for an already known directory.
Supported declaration attributes are uname, gname, octal perm and native fflags;
unknown or malformed attributes fail.
Equivalent permission spellings agree by value; package order cannot resolve
conflicts.
Reconciliation requires complete directory ownership and permissions and
rejects independently asserted named and numeric owners as ambiguous.
Missing selected packages, missing dependencies and pkg fetch diagnostics are
fatal before destination creation.
Unsupported ACLs, extended attributes and special objects fail explicitly.
Configuration-file membership is recorded without granting a writable exception.
After generated replacements are composed, a declared root must be a directory
and no declared object may have a declared file or symlink as an ancestor.
Deployment rejects these conflicts before destination creation or target code
execution.
Valid leaf symlinks remain supported.
The composed hardlink graph must be acyclic and terminate at regular files.
A generated replacement cannot leave an existing hardlink targeting a symlink,
directory or missing object.
Boot-environment deployment derives private traversal directories from validated
package and generated objects, together with directories used by the installer.
Source mounts covering those directories are rejected.
Before running package scripts, the candidate must have no nested mounts;
a shared mount outside the payload inventory could still be reached by a script.
Existing traversal components must be physical directories on the candidate
filesystem.
This conservative staging restriction does not implement a production mutable
storage layout or establish resistance to a hostile builder.
Archive owner names must be resolved against the completed generation, not the host.
.Pp
This inventory is an input to the unfinished complete filesystem reconciliation
and authenticated BIP policy-generation path.
It does not authenticate an installed OS, apply runtime BIP classes, or qualify
normal startup, SSH or a protected boot-environment upgrade.
Repository verification and deployment explicitly set
.Va PKG_ENABLE_PLUGINS
to false for the selected pkg executable.
This is required even when an ambient configuration enables plugins.
Static pkg disables plugin loading independently; testing only that executable
cannot establish that the dynamic executable receives the correct option.
.Pp
Before destination creation, deploy requires every advertised shared-library
requirement to have a provider in the fetched package set.
The combined publication backend applies the same check before advancing latest.
Only selected package manifests supply providers; libraries on the builder do not.
This complements package-name dependency closure and does not prove ELF or dynamic
loading closure, compatible search paths, or runtime authorization.
.Pp
World package creation checks every staged plist against the package targets
selected by the current make configuration before creating or staging archives.
An old archive does not satisfy this check.
Kernel-owned and DTB payloads remain in their separate packaging paths; kernel
modules explicitly assigned to world packages enter the world metadata stream.
This coverage check does not establish payload authentication or runtime BIP.
.Pp
.Ss Package construction declarations
Deploy validates the optional 5bsd.construction JSON annotation in fetched
package manifests before creating its destination.
Format 1 describes named actions with explicit argv, regular-file inputs,
and finite regular-file outputs with numeric ownership, mode, flags and
explicit replacement intent.
Commands and inputs must bind to package payloads or declared input producers.
Conflicting outputs, dependency cycles, unsafe paths, foreign replacements,
and replacements with hardlink aliases fail.
The inventory records the declarations and payload hashes as declared-not-executed,
with outputs_approved false.
.Pp
Declarations neither execute code nor grant runtime authority, and they do not
replace the required generated-input manifest.
The construct action supplies bounded trusted-builder execution and measured
receipts; complete generator coverage, authenticated policy and generation
sealing remain required.
PAM, account databases, login classes and referenced security policy have the
same coverage requirements as executable payloads.
.Pp
World bootstrapping builds cap_mkdb with its matching text-only reader instead
of inheriting the host's compiled-database behavior.
Secure Boot qualification exercises the target generator and libc, including
stale input caches and oversized name rejection.
These generator checks do not supply complete construction receipts or qualify
a protected pkg upgrade.
The runtime package declares password and service database generation from
explicit master.passwd and services inputs.
It produces passwd and pwd.db with mode 0644, spwd.db with mode 0600, and
services.db with mode 0644.
These declarations cover fresh-generation derivation; account migrations,
protected credential writers, kernel hints and package
registration still require their own construction contracts.
.Pp
.Ss Collected boot transport check
The boot-check action checks collected image and boot archive bytes against their
common unsigned image receipt without extracting archive paths:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
build-bsd boot-check --boot-image /usr/obj/collected/image.ufs \e
    --boot-archive /usr/obj/collected/boot.tar \e
    --image-receipt /usr/obj/collected/image-receipt.json
.Ed
It checks the complete boot namespace, contents, transport metadata, source and
read-back observation binding, hardlink consistency, and archive end records.
Unsupported extensions, duplicate receipt keys and invalid metadata reject.
Success does not authenticate the receipt, reconstruct filesystem metadata,
approve a release or establish that the system boots.
.Ss Boot transport staging
The boot-stage action accepts the same three inputs as boot-check, plus a new
.Fl -output
folder. It writes a makefs mtree manifest, private regular payload files and a
stage receipt containing the expected filesystem observations.
The finalized stage.json matches the returned report, including any startup
contract and stable generated-policy payload path. It is published by rename
only after staging completes; it is not a durable boot-selection journal.
Recorded modes,
ownership, flags and nanosecond timestamps enter the manifest; host ownership is
not used as image metadata. Archive symlinks are represented as manifest entries
and are never extracted on the host. Internal regular-file hardlinks are restored
in the payload store. Aliases outside /boot remain in the separate installed
root image; the outer transport contains only their /boot projection.
Hardlinked symlinks currently reject rather than losing their identity.
The outer filesystem root is a format directory (root:wheel, mode 0755, epoch
zero); it is not a reconstruction of the installed root directory.
The resulting makefs image still requires independent read-back against stage.json,
explicit boot policy and signing before boot qualification.
.Pp
The request summary labels the general signing option as
.Dq bundle-signing-mode .
In JSON request records,
.Dq signing_scope
is
.Dq system-bundles
and qualifies the
.Dq signing
field.
This mode does not describe generation-manifest or EFI signing, which use their
separate explicit key inputs. A request or plan is not evidence of completed
signing, firmware acceptance or root protection; inspect the operation receipts
and boot qualification results.
.Ss Generation filesystem image
The image action requires an alternate root, package inventory, final generated
manifest and a completed construction receipt:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
build-bsd image --root /usr/obj/candidate \e
    --inventory /usr/obj/inputs/inventory.json \e
    --generated /usr/obj/constructed/generated.json \e
    --construction-receipt /usr/obj/constructed/construction.json \e
    --image-makefs /path/to/qualified/makefs \e
    --image-mdconfig /sbin/mdconfig --image-mount /sbin/mount \e
    --image-umount /sbin/umount --output /usr/obj/generation-image
.Ed
All four tools are explicit trusted-builder inputs and must reside outside the
candidate. Execution requires root for a newly allocated memory disk; use a
trusted disposable builder VM. The action re-reads package archives, checks the
construction receipt and reconciles the complete source filesystem. It builds
UFS2 without metadata overrides, checks that the source remains unchanged,
mounts the image read-only and compares all filesystem observations before
unmounting and detaching the memory disk. A failed check leaves diagnostic output
without a successful image receipt.
.Pp
With
.Fl -image-boot-archive ,
the action also writes boot.tar and boot.json for the complete verified /boot
namespace. It checks the source before and after export and verifies serialized
contents and transport metadata. The image receipt binds the archive digest and
complete observations, including flags and hardlink aliases outside /boot.
This is a transport artifact: boot-media construction must restore and reconcile
those observations. Ordinary tar extraction does not establish equivalent
metadata or authenticated boot media. This action takes no signing-key input.
.Pp
.Fl -image-verity-policy
selects an explicit JSON policy for the immutable base projection and requires
.Fl -storage-layout-policy .
The policy has exactly format (integer 1), block_size (a power of two from
512 through 65536), and salt_hex (zero through 256 bytes in lowercase hex).
The selected block size aligns the complete image using makefs size rounding;
it does not change the UFS sector size.
The builder emits system.verity (filesystem followed by hash tree) and
root.descriptor beside the base system.ufs, binds their hashes and policy in the
unsigned receipt, and checks retained bytes.
Retained validation recomputes every hash-tree block, compares the descriptor
root and checks the filesystem prefix against the selected source digest.
It uses block-bounded memory and reuses the data pass for the combined-file hash;
this is construction consistency, not release authentication.
Writable seed images retain their current format.
These outputs are not signed or enabled for boot by this option.
The optional kernel GEOM_VERITY configuration and mount.conf .verity directive
provide construction and final root-identity checks; they do not complete
authenticated staged BIP boot or runtime topology protection. The boot backend
requires explicit artifacts and a matching disk startup policy for staging;
this constructs a candidate and does not qualify protected boot.
Root-descriptor loading requires restricted compiled generation anchors; a loader
using general firmware manifest trust rejects it before reading descriptor bytes.
The existing secureboot-test generation-certificate input selects restricted
trust, but does not by itself qualify disk-backed boot.
The loader checks opened-device identity for selected-image ELF and raw-preload
inputs. The normal boot command checks the loaded root descriptor against the
retained selected descriptor before invoking the exec handler. Complete boot
provenance, release-key scope and handoff qualification remain required.
Compressed-loader source admission follows the retained backing handle to the
selected UFS image. Native gzip/bzip read, rewind and descriptor-lifetime tests
exist; compressed ELF execution and authenticated boot still require qualification.
Verified-root staging requires direct directories on the selected root for
/.mount and /dev; placement failures stop boot instead of using /mnt or
continuing with an incomplete mount topology.
Image construction and retained-layout verification require both slots to be
empty root-owned directories without group or other write permission in the
reconciled base inventory, with no writable-state boundary at or beneath them.
Supply these through package contents or declared generated-file policy; the
builder does not silently create them.
.Pp
The unsigned receipt records source and read-back observation digests, image
hash and size, tool hashes and input bindings. This action does not authenticate
inputs, sign a generation, verify boot, authorize activation or install an update.
Its plan performs no image construction or mounts.
.Ss Explicit package inventory
Create a construction inventory from an explicit selection of package archives:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
build-bsd inventory --package-selection /usr/obj/selection.json \e
    --output /usr/obj/selected-inventory
.Ed
The selection is a JSON object with format set to 1 and a nonempty archives array.
Each entry has exactly path (an absolute archive filename) and sha256 (the
expected lowercase SHA-256 digest):
.Bd -literal -offset indent
{"format":1,"archives":[{"path":"/usr/obj/snapshot/example.pkg",
 "sha256":"<64 lowercase hexadecimal characters>"}]}
.Ed
The backend rereads payloads without extraction or script execution, checks each
expected hash, and requires declared dependencies and advertised shared-library
providers in the selected set.
Explicit conflicts between selected packages are rejected.
It writes inventory.json in a new output directory and binds the original
selection bytes by digest.
Existing output is never overwritten.
Format 1 requires the full selected dependency set.
Alternatively, format 2 selects roots from a quiescent repository directory:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
{"format":2,"repository":"/usr/obj/verified-snapshot",
 "roots":["5BSD-set-base","5BSD-kernel-generic-integrity"]}
.Ed
Select hardware and application package roots explicitly as needed.
The backend derives transitive dependencies from archive manifests, rejects
missing roots/dependencies and duplicate package names, then fully inventories
only the selected archives.
Selected manifests must remain unchanged between selection and full inventory.
Cycles are visited once.
The resulting inventory records the selected roots and measured archive hashes.
No host package database or hardcoded program inventory supplies the selection.
Neither format approves versions or authenticates the repository.
Format 2 does not replace a package solver for alternative/version constraints;
this repository must contain one already selected version per package name.
.Pp
Hashes bind bytes but do not authenticate a publisher.
Verify the repository with snapshot before selecting its archives.
Inventory does not apply BIP policy, construct a filesystem, sign a release or
authorize activation.
.Ss Trusted-builder construction
Construct executes the package-declared generators in a private alternate root:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
build-bsd construct --root /usr/obj/candidate \e
    --inventory /usr/obj/package-inventory.json \e
    --generated /usr/obj/initial-inputs.json \e
    --output /usr/obj/construction-result \e
    --construction-timeout 300
.Ed
The builder and its kernel must be trusted; execution requires a root shell
for temporary jail creation.
The command re-reads the referenced package archives and checks their hashes.
The selected set must include declared package dependencies with matching
origins and providers for advertised required shared libraries.
Dependency versions record build-time metadata; they are not exact solver pins.
These checks also apply when image creation revalidates construction inputs.
They do not authenticate the publisher, approve versions, prove runtime library
loading, or authenticate the initial input manifest.
The complete candidate must match these explicit expectations before execution.
Declared outputs cannot be preapproved by the initial manifest.
.Pp
Each generator runs in a temporary jail with a fixed environment and umask,
no IP networking, no mount permission, no child jails, and no supplied device
filesystem or inherited host directory descriptors.
The per-action timeout defaults to 300 seconds and permits 1 through 86400.
The builder removes the jail and waits for its descendants to exit before
measuring output; cleanup failure prevents acceptance.
.Pp
Only declared regular-file outputs may change.
The builder applies their declared ownership, mode and flags, and rejects
symlink or hardlink output aliases and unexplained filesystem changes.
Output-parent directory timestamps may change; the ZFS archive bookkeeping
flag is restored to its prior value.
Other metadata changes are errors.
The result includes construction.json with measured before/after hashes,
explicit inputs and output receipts, and generated.json for reconciliation.
These unsigned records do not prove all internal reads of an arbitrary program,
authenticate site policy, or authorize activation.
A failed candidate is retained for diagnosis and must not be deployed.
.Pp
This command is not yet a complete package installation transaction or a
root-resistant live updater.
It does not automatically replace deploy's package hooks.
Complete generator coverage, authenticated inputs and generation sealing,
normal startup, SSH, recovery and new-boot-environment qualification remain
required.
.Ss Package coverage review
Inventory-audit reviews an explicit package inventory:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
build-bsd inventory-audit --inventory /usr/obj/package-inventory.json \e
    --output /usr/obj/package-review
.Ed
The new output directory contains inventory-review.json, listing package objects,
executable candidates, state declarations and outstanding review gaps.
This report does not authenticate inputs, assign authority, discover every
runtime path or prove program compatibility.
Use generation reconciliation and functional qualification separately.
.Ss Offline generation reconciliation
Reconcile checks a quiescent alternate root against an explicit package
inventory and generated-input manifest:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
build-bsd reconcile --root /usr/obj/candidate \e
    --inventory /usr/obj/package-inventory.json \e
    --generated /usr/obj/generated-inputs.json \e
    --output /usr/obj/reconciliation-result
.Ed
The output directory must be new and outside the generation.
The command records reconciliation.json and exits unsuccessfully for unexplained
objects, missing objects or mismatched content, ownership, modes, flags
or links.
It never repairs the candidate or automatically accepts observed differences.
Unsupported special objects, ACLs, extended attributes and nested filesystems
are refused.
Archive ownership names resolve against declared, content-checked account files
inside the generation, without consulting the host account database.
Extraction preflight reads bounded, hash-checked account payloads from selected
packages and uses the same account parser before checking library ownership.
Unknown or ambiguous names are rejected; numeric archive ownership is not
substituted for a named candidate identity.
Compatibility aliases must have matching declared and observed lookup chains.
Their targets are resolved within the generation, opened without filesystem
symlink traversal, and content-checked again before deriving numeric ownership.
Configuration files receive the same checks as other package payloads.
.Pp
The generated-input JSON document has format 1 and an entries object keyed by
canonical absolute generation paths.
Each entry requires type, uid, gid, mode, flags_set, source and replaces.
Files additionally require size and sha256; symbolic links require target.
The source string identifies the declared generator or site input.
The replaces array must name exactly the SHA256 hashes of all package archives
owning a replaced path; new paths use an empty array.
Unknown fields and incomplete metadata are errors.
This declaration is not an attestation that a generator ran.
.Pp
Reconciliation does not authenticate its input files, classify storage or
writers, authorize symbolic-link targets, seal a generation, or verify BIP at
runtime.
The caller must supply trusted expectations and a quiescent staging filesystem;
this is not a verifier for a hostile live filesystem.
Directory timestamps are measured but not compared to package timestamps,
because installing children changes them.
Generated timestamps are also measured without a prescribed value.
All measured timestamps remain in the report for subsequent generation sealing.
.Sh OPTIONS
.Bl -tag -width "--development-key file"
.It Fl -package-selection Ar file
Explicit archive bindings or repository package roots for inventory; see above.
.It Fl -inventory Ar file
Package payload inventory for inventory-audit, reconcile or construct.
.It Fl -generated Ar file
Explicit generated-input manifest, required for reconcile, construct and deploy.
Construct accepts initial inputs only; declared outputs must be produced by
execution.
Deploy derives its package inventory from authenticated downloads and does not
accept an external inventory.
.It Fl -storage-layout-policy Ar file
Optional explicit JSON storage partition policy for construct, reconcile or image.
Construct and reconcile also inspect declared protected consumers and record
ELF interpreter, library and search-path metadata bound to package and file
hashes.
Changed consumer bytes and malformed ELF are rejected; non-ELF consumers are
reported without claiming dependency coverage.
This metadata does not establish a resolved dependency set, runtime enforcement,
or authenticated input policy.
With construct, validate the completed filesystem after generated outputs and
the package database have been reconciled, before reporting success.
Retain storage-layout.json and bind its digest, the policy digest and final
filesystem digest in the construction receipt.
A planning invocation cannot validate this final state.
Format 1 has a boundaries array of objects containing path and content,
where content is ordinary or protected.
Format 2 additionally requires a protected_consumers array.
Format 3 also requires an explicit target_elf object with elf_class (32 or 64),
byte_order (little or big), machine (ELF machine number) and page_size.
A format-3 consumer may specify code_loading equal to bip.
For that consumer, reconciliation additionally requires matching native ELF
identification and exactly one ABI note and restrictive feature-control note
within the target note bounds.
This checks declared image metadata, not whether the configured manager was
completely enumerated or whether the selected kernel and interpreter implement
the required runtime contract.
Each entry supplies consumer, package_archive_sha256 and a nonempty inputs array.
The consumer must be a direct regular-file payload from that exact package
archive, with unchanged bytes in the completed generation.
Each input supplies path and presence, either required or optional.
The consumer and every input lookup must remain in immutable storage, including
intermediate aliases; protected mutable storage is not accepted for these inputs.
Optional inputs may be absent only at a final leaf with immutable parents.
Alias lookup observes the target kernel symlink traversal limit.
Exceeding that limit rejects required and optional protected inputs alike.
The report records input hashes and lookup observations without claiming
complete dependency coverage, authenticated approval or runtime enforcement.
This extension preserves the existing configuration and executable restrictions.
Boundaries must be disjoint existing physical directories.
All objects outside them remain in the immutable partition.
The check requires exact filesystem reconciliation, retains every seed object's
metadata, rejects crossing hardlinks, and requires declared state to be covered.
Package state-requirements format 2 permits an optional directory_mapping
for directory requirements. The path field remains the fallback.
The checker selects a mapping only when the completed filesystem contains it;
a present mapping must resolve to a directory. Dangling links, cycles and
non-directory targets fail. Mapping parents must be physical directories.
Format 1 does not accept the new field.
Reports retain the declared fallback, mapping, selected path and resolved path.
The selected path still needs an explicit writable boundary; the declaration
neither classifies content nor grants authority.
Installation policy alias operations may create the package-declared mapping
at an absent, unowned destination using the existing validation rules.
Ordinary writable storage may contain executable files and package configuration,
including ordinary configuration aliases.
Package configuration membership alone does not require immutable storage.
Explicitly declared protected consumers and their inputs must remain immutable
throughout lookup, including intermediate directories and aliases.
Protected state requirements likewise cannot traverse ordinary storage.
An optional directory mapping must have a safe lookup path even when absent,
so creating it later cannot redirect protected state through ordinary storage.
The report does not install seeds, construct attachments, authenticate policy
or authorize runtime access.
This is a construction check for the selected storage layout, including ordinary
application storage; it does not implement live package transactions or assign
trust to applications installed there.
With image, construct an immutable base image and separate seed images using
complete mtree inputs, then mount each read-only and compare all observations.
The complete source is checked before and after the image set; each image
retains its independent filesystem readback. Successful image receipts are
written only after the final source check. Progress diagnostics go to stderr.
Seed roots retain their original metadata; empty base attachment slots have
UID/GID zero, mode 0755 and no flags, with the original timestamps.
The aggregate receipt binds policy, projections and individual image receipts.
Before reporting success, independently re-read the retained aggregate and child
receipts and verify every image and mtree digest against the selected inputs.
Write verification.json with the explicit scope retained-storage-image-consistency.
This is transport consistency, not authentication or a second filesystem readback.
These images are unsigned construction outputs; seed installation, attachment
activation are not implemented.
With image-boot-archive, export the unchanged immutable /boot namespace under
base/boot.tar and bind it to base/receipt.json.
Boot-check and boot-stage accept this immutable base receipt; mutable seed
receipts cannot be used as boot roots.
The aggregate image set and seed images must be retained for installation.
.It Fl -installation-policy Ar file
Optional explicit JSON installation configuration for construct only.
Format 1 retains replacement-only behavior.
Format 2 requires an operation field on every entry: create or replace.
Format 3 also accepts alias entries, described below.
Format 4 adds final-phase relocate entries.
Format 5 additionally accepts replace-template with the same source, hash,
metadata and exact owning archive fields as replace.
The destination must be declared by its owning package in the
5bsd.installation-templates annotation, a JSON string with format 1 and a paths
array of canonical absolute filenames.
Each declared template must be a nonexecutable regular package payload,
without file flags, and must not be a pkg configuration file.
Debug and manual subpackages must not inherit declarations for unowned files.
This permits installation-specific immutable policy without making it mutable
package configuration or authorizing runtime writes.
All existing source hashing, parent, hardlink, generated-output conflict and
root-builder checks still apply.
The entries object maps canonical candidate paths to source, sha256, size,
uid, gid, mode and replaces fields.
Source names an explicit canonical regular file outside the candidate.
Replaces lists the exact owning package archive SHA256.
The constructor checks these bindings and source bytes against the declared
package object graph before extraction.
The construction VM runner performs the same check before launching a guest.
These checks do not attest a completed filesystem; installation still reconciles
the real candidate and rechecks source bytes.
A new package archive needs a new explicit binding even when its configuration
payload is unchanged.
Before updating a binding, compare the owning package, file content and security
metadata with the previously selected input; changed templates require review
and recomposition of the intended configuration.
Destinations must be unaliased regular package-declared configuration files,
with existing declared directory ancestors and no file flags.
For format-2 create entries, the destination must instead be absent from the
observed tree and package payloads, and replaces must be an empty array.
Parent directories must already be declared; creation never makes ancestors.
Neither operation may overlap generated inputs or declared generator outputs.
The constructor checks module and library plans for destination conflicts
before installing configuration.
Configuration metadata must be root-owned, nonexecutable and not writable by
group or others. File flags are zero.
The trusted root builder stages and verifies all input bytes before
installing files, reconciles the complete tree, and binds the policy and
configuration metadata into its receipt and generated manifest.
Original package database configuration baselines remain unchanged.
For a format-3 alias entry, the fields are operation (alias), target,
package and requirement. The destination must match exactly one named state
requirement of the selected package and must be absent from the candidate,
package inventory and generated outputs. Its parents must already be directories.
Target must be a canonical absolute candidate path, other than root or the
destination itself. Candidate-only resolution rejects cycles and traversal
through nondirectories; an existing target must match the requirement type.
A missing target is permitted for state created at boot.
The generated link is root-owned, mode 0777, with zero file flags.
Aliases do not provision storage, authorize writers or approve runtime mounts.
For a format-4 relocate entry, the fields are operation (relocate), target,
package and requirement. The source path must match one package-declared file
state requirement. After all generators and database construction finish, the
source must be a regular nonexecutable file without flags or other hardlinks.
The target must be absent and unowned; both paths need physical existing parents.
Targets cannot collide with other installation entries or relocation targets.
The constructor moves the file preserving its bytes and metadata, replaces its
original path with an explicitly initialized root-owned alias, syncs the affected
file and directories, then reconciles the entire candidate again.
The receipt records the selected policy, original observations, moves and generated
records. Storage classification and writable-boundary validation still apply.
The retained verifier reconstructs the prior manifest from package and generation
inputs, reverses the declared moves in its filesystem observation, and checks
predecessor continuity, original content and metadata, exact generated records,
and the resulting filesystem digest. Parent observations permit only directory
timestamp changes. This verifies consistency, not policy approval or boot trust.
A failed construction must be discarded; no successful generated manifest
is emitted. This option neither approves policy nor grants runtime writers.
.It Fl -library-policy Ar file
Optional explicit format-1 JSON library-search policy for construct only.
Fields are format, program, directories and output.
Select a packaged strict-mode ldconfig executable, an ordered list of canonical
absolute directories and a new hints output path.
Directories and ancestors must be verified package-declared directories owned
by root without group or other write permission.
The constructor invokes ldconfig with -S, binds directory metadata and verifies
the exact ELF hints contents before accepting a receipt.
Outputs are root:wheel mode 0444.
The shipped library-policy.json example selects /lib and /usr/lib and generates
/boot/ld-elf.so.hints for protected BIP execution. Pass it explicitly with this
option when constructing that system profile. Approved additional search
directories are policy inputs, not discovered from a running host or mutable
ports cache. Ordinary execution retains its separate /var/run hints cache.
This trusted-builder input does not grant runtime storage authority or
authenticate packages and policy.
.It Fl -module-policy Ar file
Optional explicit format-1 JSON module policy for construct only.
Selects a packaged generator, module directories, package and basename patterns,
required or optional selections, and fixed output metadata.
An example is installed in
.Pa /usr/share/examples/bsd-build/module-policy.json .
Only matching package payloads become generator inputs.
Existing output requires explicit replacement intent and must match its owning
package payload; an unexplained file cannot be adopted.
The constructor records input selection, policy and output hashes and verifies
actual jailed execution before accepting generated state.
The policy is bounded to 64 groups and 1024 selected inputs, with command size
limits enforced before execution.
Supplying this input does not authenticate it or authorize module loading.
.It Fl -certificate-policy Ar file
Optional explicit certificate policy for construct only.
The format-1 JSON policy selects package payloads by package name and path
prefix,
additional verified files, explicit role overrides, a packaged generator, and
output paths and metadata.
An example is installed in
.Pa /usr/share/examples/bsd-build/certificate-policy.json .
The constructor derives a bounded output plan in a temporary jail, requires
planning to leave the candidate unchanged, and checks actual outputs against
that plan before recording a receipt.
Output directories must already exist and be empty; the bundle must be absent.
Supplying this input does not authenticate it or authorize a trust change.
.It Fl -extract-tar Ar executable
For construct, materialize the selected package payloads into an empty root
before running the declared generators.
The generated-input manifest must declare explicit root directory metadata;
see root-layout.json. It may also declare otherwise unowned directories with
explicit metadata, provenance and empty replacement lists.
Package-owned objects cannot be replaced, and files or symlinks cannot be added
through this extraction layout. All parents must be declared directories.
Directory declarations do not grant runtime write access or select writable mounts.
The root must have a root-owned mode-0700 parent in the trusted builder.
Archive hashes are checked before and after extraction.
The selected tar ignores account names during extraction; final ownership is
resolved from the verified candidate account files, followed by full filesystem
reconciliation.
Existing roots containing objects are refused.
Failure may retain a partial private tree, which must not be used as a generation.
This option does not authenticate inputs or authorize installation or activation.
.It Fl -database-pkg Ar executable
Explicit static pkg executable for optional fresh database construction.
Construct requires all five database options together.
The executable hash must match the format-3 database contract.
.It Fl -database-sqlite Ar executable
Explicit SQLite executable used for read-only verification of the result.
.It Fl -database-contract Ar file
Format-3 contract selecting engine/schema hashes, installation epoch and
package identity/control policy.
The reviewed profile requires schema 1000 and populated archive solver digests.
Selected package versions must exactly match this contract.
Recorded dependency build versions are not exact version pins; dependency
origins and the complete selected archive set remain checked.
.It Fl -database-policy Ar file
Explicit output pathname, uid, gid, mode and flags_set for the generated database.
The destination must be absent beneath an already verified directory.
An example is installed in
.Pa /usr/share/examples/bsd-build/database-output-policy.json .
.It Fl -database-osversion Ar integer
Positive 32-bit OSVERSION of the selected target build.
Database construction uses a separate restricted jail, with local-only archive
registration and scripts, triggers, plugins and repository updates disabled.
After verification, the database is installed without following symlinks or
replacing existing files, and is included in full filesystem reconciliation
and generated-file receipts.
These inputs and receipts are not authenticated by this offline action.
.It Fl -construction-timeout Ar seconds
Per-generator execution timeout for construct, from 1 through 86400 seconds.
The default is 300 seconds.
.It Fl -root Ar directory
Existing alternate root for verify, reconcile or construct, or a new
destination directory for deploy.
Deploy refuses an existing directory.
For deploy, its parent must be root-owned with mode 0700.
This keeps the candidate inaccessible to other local users even when package
extraction restores public modes on directories inside it.
Boot-environment deployment uses the private deployment work directory.
Secureboot-test requires an existing staged root with the selected built kernel.
.It Fl -qemu Ar file
QEMU executable for secureboot-test; default /usr/local/bin/qemu-system-x86_64.
.It Fl -qemu-data Ar directory
Firmware directory for secureboot-test; default /usr/local/share/qemu.
.It Fl -uefi-vars-tool Ar file
virt-fw-vars executable for secureboot-test; default /usr/local/bin/virt-fw-vars.
.It Fl -test-generation-key Ar path
Host-only disposable generation signing key for secureboot-test.
Requires
.Fl -test-generation-certificate
and one explicit test case independent of secondary firmware db trust.
The second-db-cert, second-dbx-cert and second-dbx-hash cases are rejected
in this mode; they test firmware-backed manifest trust.
Do not supply production signing keys to disposable qualification runs.
The firmware-signed-manifest case signs the manifest with the disposable EFI
signer and requires rejection as an untrusted manifest signer.
The revoked-generation-manifest case adds the generation certificate to dbx
while leaving the EFI signer trusted, and requires certificate revocation rejection.
Both require this explicit generation identity and case-specific loader evidence.
A generic loader failure does not satisfy either case.
.It Fl -test-generation-certificate Ar path
Public certificate for the generation test key.
The frontend rebuilds the test loader with this TRUST_ANCHORS input and
WITHOUT_LOADER_EFI_TRUST_DB=yes, and supplies the pair to the runner.
The path must contain only letters, digits, slash, dot, underscore or dash
because it is passed through the make trust-anchor list.
Firmware enrollment and EFI signing still use runner-generated disposable keys.
A planned or compiled test is not proof of firmware enforcement.
.It Fl -test-case Ar name
Select one explicit secureboot-test runner case, such as
.Dq unsigned-loader .
The runner validates the case name and its required inputs.
Without this option, the full default suite and complete-suite evidence are required.
With it, the independent verifier checks the selected case; this is partial
qualification and must not be reported as a complete-suite pass.
No host installation or reboot is performed.
.It Fl -public-key Ar file
Trusted code-signing public key for verify, deploy, bsdinstall, or media.
.It Fl -repository Ar directory
Completed combined generation under /usr/obj/usr/src/repo for deploy,
bsdinstall, or media.
A latest symlink is resolved once to select a specific generation.
.It Fl -pkg-public-key Ar file
Trusted package repository public key for deploy, bsdinstall, or media.
Public keys copied for catalogue verification, deployment, installer preparation
or media must contain one PUBLIC KEY or RSA PUBLIC KEY PEM block only.
They are parsed and emitted as canonical public-key bytes; combined public/private
PEM files, extra objects and malformed keys are rejected before copying.
.It Fl -bootenv Ar name
Create a new recursive boot-environment clone for deploy instead of --root.
The clone remains inactive.
Existing names are not reused or destroyed.
.It Fl -clone-from Ar BE|BE@snapshot
Select the source for a new boot environment.
Defaults to the active boot
environment.
A fully qualified dataset beneath the selected boot-environment
root is also accepted.
Only the new clone is made writable.
The helper checks the source dataset GUID before creation and again before
making the candidate writable.
An explicit source snapshot also has its GUID recorded and rechecked.
Dataset identity replies must contain unique expected fields and a valid
nonzero 64-bit GUID; malformed replies stop deployment.
The candidate dataset GUID and clone origin are retained and checked before
making it writable, before and after mounting, and before temporary devfs
and boot-environment cleanup unmount.
Temporary devfs mount identity and topology are recorded and rechecked separately.
If identity cannot be established or changes, cleanup retains the mounts for
operator diagnosis.
An observed replacement aborts without automatic deletion or unmount by name.
These checks do not serialize concurrent ZFS namespace changes.
An observed identity change aborts deployment and preserves any candidate.
These consistency checks do not serialize concurrent ZFS administration.
.It Fl -be-root Ar dataset
Select an explicit ZFS boot-environment parent dataset, for example
zroot/ROOT.
Requires both --bootenv and --clone-from.
This also permits creating a candidate in a separate test pool.
.It Fl -config Ar file
Read a literal key=value configuration file.
Command-line options override
file settings regardless of their position.
Unknown and duplicate keys fail.
Blank lines and whole-line comments beginning with # are accepted.
Values are literal: do not add shell quotes, expansions, or inline comments.
The file is never sourced as shell code.
.It Fl -arch Ar native|amd64|arm64|riscv64
Target architecture; native defaults to the current machine.
The make pairs are amd64/amd64, arm64/aarch64, and riscv/riscv64.
Host signing tools remain native to the build host.
.It Fl -jobs Ar count|auto
Parallel compilation jobs.
The default auto uses all logical CPUs.
.It Fl -src-conf Ar file
World option configuration passed as SRCCONF; defaults to /etc/src.conf.
Use /dev/null to disable that configuration file.
.It Fl -make-conf Ar file
Make configuration passed as __MAKE_CONF; defaults to /etc/make.conf.
These two files are trusted make input, unlike the literal frontend config.
.It Fl -kernel-dir Ar directory
Directory containing the selected kernel configuration, passed as KERNCONFDIR.
.It Fl -pkg-key Ar file
Existing unencrypted RSA private key for both base and combined repository
catalogues.
It must be private and outside the publication repository.
This is independent of the Ed25519 executable-signing key.
.It Fl -candidate-publication
Create a complete package generation for explicit validation without advancing
latest.
Applies only to build/packages and preserves the standard repository location.
Package, dependency, signature and kernel/hardware checks still apply.
The completed generation can be selected explicitly for VM installation;
it does not constitute VM qualification or a protected production release.
The normal publication path still advances latest only after successful checks.
.It Fl -boot-media-makefs Ar file
Optional explicit makefs tool for boot-stage media construction. Requires EFI
signing and all four remaining media options below. The complete mtree builds a
UFS2 partition; the signed EFI artifact builds a FAT16 ESP. mkimg combines them
into media/disk.img. Tools and staged inputs are hashed before and after use.
Receipt success denotes construction only; readback and firmware/normal startup
qualification remain separate requirements.
.It Fl -boot-media-mkimg Ar file
Explicit mkimg executable for GPT disk assembly.
.It Fl -boot-efi-fallback Ar name
EFI fallback filename for the selected architecture, such as BOOTX64.EFI.
Only the BOOT<uppercase letters or digits>.EFI filename form is accepted.
The name must match the signed loader's PE machine and optional-header format;
staging rejects mismatches before media output is created. Supported machine
formats are IA32, X64, AArch64 and RISC-V64 EFI applications. Header recognition
does not qualify firmware or runtime support for those architectures.
.It Fl -boot-esp-mib Ar size
ESP capacity in MiB, from 16 through 1024. Insufficient capacity fails construction.
.It Fl -boot-free-percent Ar percent
Additional free space for the UFS2 boot partition, from 0 through 50 percent.
.It Fl -boot-efi-path Ar path
Optional logical /boot path of a regular inventory file to sign as an EFI loader.
Requires manifest signing and boot-efi-signer. Staging checks its size and digest,
keeps an unsigned copy, and emits efi/loader.efi with input/output/tool hashes.
Staging requires independent Authenticode verification under the selected
signing certificate before media creation. It does not establish firmware
acceptance, enrolled trust or generation bootability.
.It Fl -boot-efi-signer Ar file
Explicit uefisign executable. It receives the key path, not a key descriptor,
preserving its parent/sandboxed-parser key separation. Staging checks key identity
and metadata and the signer hash before and after signing.
.It Fl -boot-efi-signature-verifier Ar file
Required explicit osslsigncode executable supporting require-leaf-hash.
Staging verifies the image digest and signature with the selected public
certificate bundle and pins the first certificate as the required signer.
Timestamp trust and CRL checks are disabled for this offline artifact check;
firmware trust and revocation require separate qualification.
The receipt records the verifier, OpenSSL, certificate and signed-image hashes.
A failed check prevents media assembly and successful stage publication.
.It Fl -boot-efi-signing-key Ar file
Optional private key for EFI executable signing, separate from manifest signing.
Requires boot-stage EFI inputs and boot-efi-signing-certificate.
If neither separate EFI credential option is supplied, EFI signing continues to
use the manifest credentials.
The receipt records explicit-efi or manifest-signing selection and the EFI
certificate digest; choosing separate inputs does not prove enrolled trust.
.It Fl -boot-efi-signing-certificate Ar file
Public certificate-only PEM for the separate EFI signing key.
Both separate credential options must be supplied together.
The backend rejects mixed or private PEM before copying a certificate snapshot
into efi/certificate.pem, then supplies that snapshot to the signer.
The snapshot digest is checked after signing.
Cryptographic signature verification is required during staging; firmware
verification remains a separate qualification step.
.It Fl -boot-signing-key Ar file
Boot manifest paths are resolved as complete logical pathnames, counting
directory aliases against the same symlink traversal limit as file aliases.
Unresolvable paths are rejected before manifest signing.
.Pp
Optional private manifest signing key for boot-stage. Requires boot-generated,
boot-signing-certificate and boot-openssl. The generated manifest is signed,
verified against the certificate public key, and included with its detached
signature and certificate in makefs inputs. No private key is retained in the
stage. The original key is opened without following a symlink and supplied only
to the signing subprocess through standard input; no temporary key copy is made.
Boot inputs must not occupy /boot/manifest, /boot/manifest.sig or
/boot/manifest.certs as files, directories or aliases. These output-name
collisions are rejected before creating the stage or invoking the signer.
Signature correctness does not establish release authorization, enrolled
firmware trust, filesystem readback or boot qualification.
.It Fl -boot-signing-certificate Ar file
Certificate-only PEM chain whose first certificate corresponds to the explicit
manifest signing key. Combined key/certificate files and non-certificate material
are rejected before staging. Every certificate is parsed and canonical public PEM
is emitted; both input and emitted-certificate digests are recorded.
This parsing does not validate chain trust or release authorization.
.It Fl -boot-openssl Ar file
Explicit absolute OpenSSL executable for manifest signing and verification.
Its digest is checked before and after the operation and recorded in the receipt.
.It Fl -boot-root-path Ar path
Logical path in the boot inventory of the root image supplied by boot-image.
Required for boot media construction. Its size and digest must match the
verified image receipt before staging or signing begins. The selected path is
recorded in the stage receipt; no filename is assumed.
The receipt reread used for staging must match the digest returned by input
verification before output is created; replacing it between verification and
staging is rejected.
The archive copy pass also checks entry order and completeness against that
receipt before writing the filesystem manifest or signing generated inputs.
These consistency checks do not authenticate the receipt's author.
This binds the image bytes, but does not validate loader configuration semantics
or authorize the generation.
.It Fl -boot-verity-image Ar file
Explicit combined filesystem/hash-tree image for boot-check or boot-stage.
Requires --boot-verity-descriptor and a matching immutable-base image receipt.
The checker validates the boot archive, original filesystem digest, complete
retained tree and descriptor together. Results establish unsigned consistency,
not signer authentication, firmware boot or activation authorization.
.It Fl -boot-verity-descriptor Ar file
Explicit root descriptor paired with --boot-verity-image.
Both inputs must be regular nonsymlink files. The descriptor is read into one
bounded private buffer. Disk staging additionally requires an explicit verity
boot policy. It copies the checked artifacts at that policy's paths and includes
them with the generated contract in the staged signing inventory. Kernel
disk-root admission and boot qualification remain incomplete.
.It Fl -boot-policy Ar file
JSON startup contract for boot-check or boot-stage, required when signing a
staged manifest. Preload mode is {"version":1,"mode":"preloaded"}.
The backend renders /boot/boot-policy.lua, binds its bytes through generated-file
staging, and includes it in the loader manifest when signing is requested.
A conflicting generated or packaged object is refused.
.Pp
With both verity artifact inputs, boot-check and boot-stage accept mode="verity"
and the fields image, descriptor, cache_blocks and heap_bytes. The two paths
must be distinct canonical /boot paths containing only ASCII letters, digits,
underscore, slash, dot, plus or hyphen. Cache blocks must be a nonnegative integer;
heap bytes must be positive; both are bounded by 2^63-1. The renderer derives
descriptor_sha256 from checked artifacts, never a caller override, and represents
memory bounds as exact decimal strings for the loader.
.Pp
The offline result contains unsigned startup-contract text and its digest.
transport_paths_bound and startup_integration_verified remain false: artifact
consistency does not bind logical paths in completed boot media or qualify boot.
Staging derives the root-image binding from the combined verified image, rejects
conflicting generated/packaged/signing paths and requires any explicit root path
to match the policy. Its result sets staged_paths_bound=true, while
transport_paths_bound and startup_integration_verified remain false until actual
media readback and boot qualification. Required Secure Boot startup validates the
contract before loading configuration or executables; disk mode commits required
selection, checks the descriptor digest and publishes the retained descriptor
before boot. Kernel disk-root admission remains incomplete.
.It Fl -boot-generated Ar file
Optional JSON object of generated boot file bindings for boot-stage.
Each absolute /boot path maps to source (absolute regular input path) and
observation (type=file, uid, gid, mode, flags_set, mtime, mtime_nsec, size,
sha256). Parents must exist in the package-derived boot inventory; replacement
of packaged objects is refused. Bytes are copied and checked before recording
success. The completed inventory produces loader.manifest.unsigned alongside
the makefs inputs. Signing, filesystem readback and boot qualification remain
separate requirements.
.It Fl -package-tool Ar file
Explicit host pkg executable used to create packages and verify/publish the
combined repository, passed as PKG_CMD for build/packages.
Requires an absolute path to a readable regular executable, without shell or
make metacharacters or whitespace. The normal backend default applies if omitted.
This selects a trusted build tool; it does not authenticate the tool or replace
the separate target bootstrap archive and its expected digest.
.It Fl -application-selection Ar file
Explicit format-1 package archive selection for build/packages publication.
The JSON object contains format=1 and an archives array of absolute path and
SHA256 bindings, using the maintained package-selection schema.
Ordinary application names, versions and archive bytes are preserved.
The publisher validates them against the combined base/hardware selection before
catalogue signing; this input does not authorize replacing system packages.
The selection path must be a regular file without whitespace or make/shell
metacharacters. Configuration files use application_selection.
These are explicit trusted-builder inputs: hashes bind bytes but do not establish
repository authentication. Fetching/resolving remote application repositories
remains a separate step. This option neither installs packages nor activates an
upgrade. Repository-root (format-2) selections are not accepted by this interface.
.It Fl -pkg-package Ar file
Explicit target-compatible pkg bootstrap archive to include in the combined
publication before catalogue signing.
Applies to build/packages and requires
.Fl -pkg-package-sha256 .
The archive must name pkg, match the target ABI, and declare an OS version
no newer than the target.
A base repository already containing pkg rejects a second bootstrap input.
This supplies offline installation without fetching a package manager separately.
The archive path must be a readable regular file without whitespace or make/shell
metacharacters. Configuration files use pkg_package and pkg_package_sha256.
.It Fl -pkg-package-sha256 Ar digest
Expected SHA256 of the explicit bootstrap archive.
The publisher checks the copied bytes before reading its manifest and includes
those bytes in the signed catalogue.
The digest pins the publisher's selected input; upstream provenance remains the
publisher's responsibility.
.It Fl -hardware-profile Ar file
Hardware port selection for build/packages, passed as HARDWARE_PORT_LIST.
Defaults to the maintained hardware-ports file for the target architecture.
The profile and ports checkout are checked before compilation.
.It Fl -distfiles Ar directory
Absolute ports download-cache directory for build/packages.
Defaults to distfiles beneath the selected ports checkout.
.It Fl -board Ar generic|rpi4|rpi5|rpi-zero2w
Board build defaults; generic leaves architecture and kernel unchanged.
Raspberry Pi profiles select arm64; Pi 4 and Zero 2 W default to GENERIC-RPI,
while Pi 5 selects GENERIC for an experimental UEFI path.
An explicit kernel overrides the default.
This selects compilation inputs only: board firmware assembly and signed disk
image integration are not yet exposed by this frontend.
Non-generic boards are therefore rejected by media.
These profiles do not establish hardware qualification or Pi 5 device support.
.It Fl -kernel Ar name
Kernel configuration; defaults to KERNCONF or GENERIC.
.It Fl -production
Build with WITH_MALLOC_PRODUCTION=yes.
Alias for --build-profile production.
Build and packages default to production; stage defaults to development.
.It Fl -build-profile Ar production|development|debug
Production enables production malloc.
Development explicitly enables debug malloc.
Debug also supplies DEBUG_FLAGS=-g for compiler debug information.
It does not disable optimization or select a different kernel configuration;
use --kernel and the configuration files for kernel diagnostics.
Build profiles are independent of executable-signing modes.
.It Fl -allow-debug-publication
Permit development/debug package publication for an explicit test only,
passing ALLOW_DEBUG_MALLOC=yes to maintained publication checks.
Rejected for production profiles and non-package actions.
Do not distribute these test generations as production releases.
.It Fl -incremental
Use WITHOUT_CLEAN=yes instead of the default clean build.
.It Fl -media-format Ar all|iso|memstick
Media artifacts to create; all (the default) creates both disc1.iso and
memstick.img.
Architecture selection applies to the existing target world,
kernel, and publication; media does not rebuild them.
.It Fl -output Ar directory
New output directory under /usr/obj for stage or media.
Defaults are unique directories under /usr/obj/usr/src/stages or
/usr/obj/usr/src/media, respectively.
.It Fl -signing Ar mode
Unsigned, development, or signed.
With no explicit mode, supplying a key selects signed; otherwise unsigned.
Unsigned needs no signing tools and retains development manifests.
Development creates and retains a local Ed25519 key on demand.
Signed requires an existing key and never generates a replacement identity.
.It Fl -key Ar file
Private signing key for signed mode.
Only its public key enters the image.
.It Fl -development-key Ar file
Persistent development key location, outside the stage and repository.
The default is /usr/obj/usr/src/signing-keys/development.pem.
.It Fl -relaxed-signatures
Sign the stage but omit the bundle signature-enforcement marker for testing.
This does not disable rejection of recognized invalid executable signatures.
.It Fl -build-backend Ar bsd-make
Select the build adapter.
Currently only bsd-make is implemented; unknown
backends are rejected.
.It Fl -format Ar text|json
Output format for plan.
JSON emits one request record followed by one record
per step, including its argument vector.
.It Fl -publish-repository Ar directory
Final combined publication destination; defaults to /usr/obj/usr/src/repo.
Base and hardware build staging remain under /usr/obj.
.It Fl -ports Ar directory
Custom ports checkout for packages.
Defaults to PORTSDIR or /usr/obj/5BSD-ports.
.El
.Sh DEPLOYMENT
Deploy verifies the signed repository and fetches authenticated packages
before
creating a target.
It installs base, the selected kernel and matching hardware
using pkg inside the target chroot, then verifies package checksums and signed
system bundles with native capsign.
Deploy requires --generated with the explicit generated-input manifest described
under Offline generation reconciliation.
It snapshots this input with the publication and validates its metadata and exact
package replacement declarations before creating the target.
After removing temporary installation inputs and unmounting devfs, it reconciles
every candidate object against the fetched package inventory and that manifest.
Reconciliation occurs while an inactive boot environment is still mounted.
The deployment report records the candidate dataset, GUID and origin, checked
before and after reconciliation, or the destination path for a directory target.
Observed identity replacement aborts before writing this report.
These checks do not serialize other ZFS writers or authorize activation.
A PASS reconciliation report does not establish successful unmount or boot.
Unexplained objects, modified payloads or mismatched metadata fail deployment;
the candidate and reconciliation.json remain available for diagnosis.
No scan automatically generates accepted exceptions, including for configuration
or the package database.
Private signing keys are not copied into the target.
Deploy is trusted-builder preparation, not a live BIP updater.
The current delegated BIP management interface excludes clone/receive and pool
operations; it does not authorize the conventional bectl path used here.
Authenticated activation, rollback selection and retirement require a separate
protected update implementation and qualification.
For boot-environment deployment, package state and system software must be
private to the root clone.
Separately mounted or symlinked etc, usr/local,
var/db/pkg, and Capabilities/System paths are rejected pending an explicit
migration design.
Shared home/data storage is not migrated.
The new dataset
identity, snapshot origin, and writable mount are checked before installation.
Root privileges and a native-architecture host are required.
Package scripts run inside the chroot using the host kernel.
Work logs and failed destinations are retained.
An unmount failure never causes
recursive cleanup of a mounted boot environment.
The command reports scope packages-bundles-and-filesystem only after successful
package and bundle verification, filesystem reconciliation, and unmount.
The report records the input hashes and package authentication method.
The deployment backend loads hardware validation and stage verification from
its own source tree, keeping validation helpers consistent with that backend.
A stage-verification failure retains the candidate for diagnosis and prevents
a successful deployment report.
The generated manifest remains a trusted builder input; this workflow does not
verify generator execution provenance, authenticate the completed generation,
apply BIP writer policy or provide a root-resistant live updater.
This path requires signed package and executable inputs; unsigned deployment
support and full VM deployment validation remain outstanding.
.Sh CONFIGURATION
The sample file is /usr/share/examples/bsd-build/bsd-build.conf.sample.
Every long option except config has a corresponding configuration key;
replace hyphens with underscores (for example clone_from or pkg_public_key).
Boolean values are yes or no.
Options remain subject to action-specific
validation even when supplied by the configuration file.
Boolean values are yes or no.
Signing values match --signing.
No file is loaded implicitly.
.Sh IMPLEMENTATION NOTES
Signing tools are compiled natively in the run directory before signing work;
target binaries are never used as host tools.
A native C compiler and host crypto, UCL and MD libraries are required.
The source tree contains the maintained host-tool recipe and stage helpers.
Executable signing is separate from repository signing and rollback policy.
Private keys must remain outside staged images and published repositories.
Fresh signing stages must have an empty code trust catalogue.
Stage verification requires exactly the requested code key in that catalogue;
missing, substituted and additional entries are rejected.
This workflow currently supports one system code signer per stage.
Protecting release keys from compromised build-host root requires external
key custody and an independently authorized signing process.
Before building, the frontend checks existing key permissions and resolves
existing ancestors of future key and staging paths without creating them.
The stage signing helper repeats the key checks when it uses the key.
.Pp
OS permissions must permit writing the object root and stage.
The tool reports permission failures instead of relocating output to a home
directory.
Inherited make output-directory overrides are cleared.
.Pp
Complete hardware publication currently requires a native target and an
installed hardware profile.
Foreign targets are rejected before compilation; stage remains available
for cross-compiling a private world/kernel image.
.Sh MACHINE-READABLE PLANS
The plan-only option
.Fl -format Cm json
emits newline-delimited JSON records with schema org.5bsd.build-plan.v1.
The first record describes the selected request and backend identifiers.
The verification_scope field is packages-bundles-and-filesystem for deploy,
offline-filesystem-reconciliation for reconcile, trusted-builder-construction
for construct, and system-bundles for other
ordinary workflows.
Secureboot-test runs the native EFI staging allocation regression before its
VM cases; that regression does not itself qualify firmware or boot.
Secureboot-test uses uefi-and-loader and records boot_verification as
qemu-qualification-requested.
Its system_image_verification field records the same requested qualification
for the authenticated preload; root_protection_verification remains
not-performed until the mandatory runtime boundary is implemented and tested.
Other workflows record boot_verification as not-performed.
All currently record root_protection_verification as not-performed.
These fields describe the workflow's verification coverage, not an observed
firmware state or a completed verification result.
Production malloc and signed bundles do not qualify verified boot or
protection against compromised root.
Subsequent records contain a sequence number, step name and exact argv array.
No commands are executed and no output directories are created by a plan.
This describes the current adapter invocation, not a completed artifact or
permission to execute installation steps.
The default format remains text.
.Sh EXAMPLES
.Bd -literal -offset indent
build-bsd plan stage --signing development
build-bsd stage --signing development
build-bsd plan build --config /secure/bsd-build.conf
build-bsd build --incremental --jobs 8 --kernel GENERIC-NODEBUG
build-bsd packages --signing signed --key /secure/release.pem
build-bsd status /usr/obj/usr/src/build-runs/RUN
.Ed
.Ss Kernel module hints boundary
For media, build-bsd builds a host-native kldxref through the maintained
bootstrap Makefile in the run's native-tools directory and passes its absolute
path as HARDWARE_KLDXREF to release staging.
The backend requires an explicit regular executable and does not search PATH.
It records the generator and output hashes in a MODULE_HINTS_GENERATED log record
and fails if the generator changes during execution.
Direct release callers must supply HARDWARE_KLDXREF explicitly.
This records trusted-builder provenance, not authenticated runtime authority.
.Pp
Normal world and bootstrap builds include the matching
.Xr kldxref 8 .
It propagates detected module/record and hints-file errors instead of silently
accepting incomplete indexes.
Construction VM qualification requires explicit kldxref and real module inputs
and checks failures without root privileges.
It additionally stages seven synthetic ELF metadata objects built by the
maintained construction Makefile, checking PNP bounds, string-read errors,
fatal-path cleanup and legitimate absence of a metadata section.
The generator also provides an explicit-file mode with a fixed output path,
qualified by twenty additional VM checks.
It does not discover unselected files.
Construct's explicit module policy derives the file list from package inventory
and binds the generator, inputs and any replaced package output to their hashes.
Its VM fixture verifies actual jailed replacement and malformed-input rejection.
Directory filename discovery is not an authenticated module inventory.
Complete parser validation, authenticated policy and package provenance,
complete module-inventory coverage and generation sealing remain required
before a protected generation can be approved.
.Pp
The current BIP kernel refuses runtime module loading and unloading, including
capability-gated requests.
A generated module index does not establish that required drivers or service
facilities are available at boot.
Hardened qualification must establish that those facilities are built into the
selected kernel or authenticated and preloaded, then exercise them during normal
startup.
Package presence and index-generation success alone do not prove this coverage.
.Ss Certificate construction boundary
The maintained world and package builds include
.Xr certctl 8 .
Construct can derive certificate outputs from an explicit
.Fl -certificate-policy
input, with no default certificate discovery or legacy blacklist inputs.
The policy, resolved input selection, generator payload, output plan and
measured
result are recorded separately.
Generic construction commands allow at most 4096 arguments and 128 KiB of
encoded arguments including terminators; individual arguments remain bounded
at 4096 bytes.
.Pp
The construction VM checks the production compiler and actual jail execution
using every certificate payload in its explicit source archive, plus malformed
input rejection and an explicit trust-role change.
The archive bytes and selected executable dependencies are repackaged into a
synthetic fixture; this is not complete package installation or a signed
release.
Unprivileged utility regressions run separately.
Input authentication, complete code-loading closure, generation sealing and
protected runtime updates remain required.
.Sh SEE ALSO
.Xr build 7 ,
.Xr capsign 8 ,
.Sh PUBLICATION DESTINATION
.Fl -publish-repository Ar directory
(configuration key
.Va publish_repository )
selects the final combined repository for a packages/build operation.
The default remains
.Pa /usr/obj/usr/src/repo .
A persistent destination such as
.Pa /var/db/5bsd/repositories/base
keeps published generations separate from disposable build output.
Base and hardware staging remain under
.Pa /usr/obj .
Only successful complete publication advances the destination's per-ABI
.Pa latest
pointer.
Creating or writing the destination requires normal filesystem
permissions; the tool does not relocate it on permission failure.

.Sh INSTALLED BOOT OBSERVATION
.Cm boot-observe
requires
.Fl -boot-observation-policy Ar file
and accepts
.Fl -output Ar directory .
The explicit JSON policy supplies hash-bound QEMU, firmware code, enrolled
firmware variables, boot media and data disks, plus CPU/memory limits,
observation duration and an optional loopback SSH forwarding port.
Policy format 2 additionally requires an explicit SSH probe: hash-bound client,
known-hosts file and private identity, guest account, remote command and expected
stdout digest. The probe uses strict host-key checking and bounded retries,
retaining each attempt. It disables automatic client-certificate discovery and
certificate algorithms, records the optional adjacent public-key file, and rejects
input paths containing SSH quoting or expansion tokens.
A requested probe must match before OBSERVED is possible;
a clean guest shutdown alone otherwise yields SSH_PROBE_FAILED.
This records one command observation, not complete SSH service qualification.
The current runner targets amd64 Q35 with firmware SMM enabled.
It boots the supplied media unchanged and read-only; firmware variables and
data disks are copied privately before use.
Networking is disabled unless explicitly requested, in which case restricted
user networking exposes only the requested loopback forwarding rule.
.Pp
This action collects serial output, QMP lifecycle events and input hashes.
The explicitly selected QEMU data directory must contain only physical
directories and regular files. Its names, sizes and file hashes are recorded
before launch and checked again after exit; changes invalidate the observation.
This records content identity, not publisher authentication or the complete
host execution environment.
It does not replace init or rc, inject a kernel command line, or infer a
successful login, SSH session or security qualification from boot output.
OBSERVED requires zero exit status and QMP evidence of guest poweroff; it is
a diagnostic result, not an acceptance PASS.
External termination or missing poweroff evidence is a harness failure.
Fatal serial markers are checked after exit as well as during observation.
A timeout records forced termination and exits 124; detected guest failures,
input changes and collector failures exit unsuccessfully.
See
.Pa /usr/src/tools/test/boot-observe/README.md
for the policy schema and limitations.

.Sh CONFIGURATION COMPOSITION
The
.Cm compose-config
action applies explicit top-level changes to an exact UCL base document.
Use
.Fl -configuration-base ,
.Fl -configuration-changes ,
.Fl -configuration-parser ,
.Fl -configuration-parser-sha256
and a fresh
.Fl -output
directory under /usr/obj.
The parser is the maintained bsd-ucl-json helper; its SHA256 is an explicit input.
The changes document contains format 1, base_sha256, replace, add and remove.
Replacement entries contain expected and value; removal entries contain the
expected prior value. Additions must name absent fields. Untouched fields are
preserved; nested objects and arrays are replaced whole. An empty grant is not
filled from defaults. Changes to the base require review and a new hash binding.
.Pp
The output contains configuration.json and receipt.json. This action does not
authenticate the base or validate service-specific semantics. Validate the
result with its consumer before including it as an explicit installation-policy
source in a new authenticated generation. It never edits the running system.

.Sh CAPSULE STATIC VALIDATION
The installed native helper
.Pa /usr/libexec/bsd-build/bsd-capsule-validate
shares Capsule's production configuration parser and effective-policy report.
It is statically linked and performs validation only; it neither starts a daemon
nor provisions storage. Select it explicitly with its measured SHA256.
For native build hosts, the maintained capimage/host-tools.mk target
.Cm bsd-capsule-validate
builds the same helper under the selected /usr/obj working directory.
It validates syntax and static constraints, not storage identities or bootability.
.Pp
For compose-config,
.Fl -capsule-validator Ar executable
and
.Fl -capsule-validator-sha256 Ar digest
select a matching Capsule executable with validation-only support.
They are optional for compose-config. Image storage bindings have been removed.
The builder invokes it with -n -f on the composed configuration before writing
the receipt. A validation failure leaves diagnostics and candidate configuration,
with no success receipt. The executable and output hashes must remain unchanged.
This validates syntax and Capsule's static constraints; it does not authenticate
policy, check live storage identity, grant runtime authority or activate boot.
The selected executable requires its matching runtime dependencies in the builder.

.Sh OFFLINE STATE STORAGE
.Cm state-storage
requires
.Fl -state-plan Ar FILE
and accepts a new
.Fl -output Ar DIRECTORY
under /usr/obj.
It executes only in an exclusive offline trusted builder with root privileges.
The builder may be a separate trusted virtual machine; root privileges are
required inside that builder, not necessarily on the machine hosting it.
The guest requires the matching /usr/src helper tree, Python 3.12, and the
explicitly selected native tools. Copying the frontend alone is insufficient.
Observe tool hashes and pool/dataset identities in the builder that executes
the plan. Export the pool and stop the builder before attaching its backing
disk to another guest; never attach the same writable disk concurrently.
The format-1 JSON plan names an operation, timeout in seconds, and explicit tools.
Each tool has path and sha256 fields; no host default tool is selected.
An allocate plan supplies dataset, pool_guid and parent_guid, with zfs, zpool
and mount tools. It creates a new empty child and records its GUID for subsequent
immutable policy construction.
An allocate plan may include an execution object with exec and setuid keys,
each containing the string on or off. Both default to off when the object is
omitted; setuid=on requires exec=on. This permits owner-selected ordinary
application storage without assigning integrity-management authority.
Device files remain disabled. The selected execution policy is recorded in the
allocation receipt and checked against dataset properties and mount options.
An initialize plan supplies a canonical source directory, observation and
allocation file references (each with path and sha256), plus the tar tool.
It verifies the recorded identity, offline properties and empty state before
copying the final reconciled seed. Execution policy is inherited from the allocation
receipt and cannot be overridden by the initialize plan. Legacy receipts without
that field retain exec=off,setuid=off. Existing data is not overwritten.
The verified allocation GUID is retained before temporary mountpoint setup.
If setup fails, cleanup restores the offline mountpoint only when a fresh GUID
check still identifies that allocation; it must not modify a replacement dataset.
Failure to query identity prevents safe cleanup and requires builder recovery.
Failure diagnostics record cleanup as not-attempted, completed,
refused-identity-mismatch or failed, with a separate cleanup error when applicable.
A cleanup failure preserves the initiating exception and never publishes a
successful provisioning receipt.
After the final pool sync, both operations recheck dataset identity, type,
mountpoint, mount state, canmount, devices, readonly and execution properties
before publishing a successful receipt.
Tool hashes are rechecked after these final operations.
A mismatch retains the dataset and failure diagnostics without a success receipt.
These are exclusive-builder observations, not protection against concurrent root.
Receipts remain unsigned construction evidence; this action does not sign,
activate, reboot or authorize runtime access.

.Sh INTEGRITY POLICY SCOPE
An explicitly ordinary mutable storage boundary may contain package
configuration and executable content.
Package membership does not grant protected runtime authority.
Declared protected consumer inputs must remain immutable, including aliases;
cross-boundary hardlinks remain prohibited.
For declared protected ELF consumers, construct and reconcile also require the
PT_INTERP reference to resolve to a reconciled immutable file through immutable
lookup components and aliases.
The report records the interpreter digest.
For BIP consumers it also rereads the interpreter without following filesystem
symlinks, checks its reconciled digest, and requires bounded ELF program headers
matching the explicit target class, byte order and machine.
No BIP feature mark is required on the interpreter itself.
This does not qualify kernel interpreter substitution, loader behavior or
transitive libraries.
Storage partition validation does not establish complete runtime dependency
coverage, authenticated boot, or protection against compromised root.

.Ss Storage policy capacity
Capsule and its shared native validator allocate storage tables for the selected
policy, bounded by 16 MiB of expanded entries per table and a 1 MiB input file.
The validator checks these limits before image construction; there is no fixed
eight-dataset layout limit. Static validation does not qualify descriptor or
mount acquisition in the running system.

.Sh CAPSULE VALIDATOR REPORT
The selected Capsule validator must support
.Fl n Fl j Fl f Ar configuration .
The maintained composition helper requires its versioned effective
service-manager report and bind that report to the validator and configuration
hashes.  A validator without this interface is rejected.
Recording the manager selection does not establish immutable dependency coverage
or runtime enforcement.

BIP format-3 image layouts and storage bindings are retired.
Old BIP layouts are rejected; their protection is not silently discarded.

.Ss Streaming verification read failures
The verifier preserves failed reads and premature EOF for known-size inputs;
finalization cannot turn these into success by retrying I/O. The native
verified-read-failure-test covers this behavior.
The kboot and OFW input callbacks also propagate allocation, mapping and read
failures as errors rather than EOF; readin-failure-test checks these cases with
injected operations. These native tests do not establish platform boot support. Generic backward replay and
unknown-length EOF handling require separate qualification.

.Ss Loader Lua read errors
The maintained lua-file-read-test checks byte counts, overflow, character reads
and persistent read errors in the loader Lua file wrapper. Required-verification
streams hash delivered bytes and finalize at close before normal script loading
returns; lua-stream-verify-test covers native mutation cases. Neither test executes
the Lua parser or firmware, and arbitrary consumers using data before close
remain outside this qualification.
The lua-config-io-test additionally exercises actual config.lua under a native
Lua interpreter, checking close-before-parse behavior and I/O failure handling;
its loader and file-I/O boundaries are injected.

.Ss EFI configuration verification
When firmware requires verification, the early EFI configuration reader checks
the retained buffer before parsing it and obtains size/type from the opened file.
The native efi-config-buffer-test covers changed bytes and error paths; it does
not qualify all loader script inputs or demonstrate firmware execution.

.Ss Boot observation serial processing
The maintained boot-observe runner scans appended serial bytes incrementally.
It retains fatal markers across polls and log replacement, handles split markers,
and bounds each scan to the log size observed at its start.
This reduces repeated I/O during long diagnostic runs.
It does not turn serial output or SSH port forwarding into service qualification.

.Ss Disk-backed integrity implementation status
The internal verity tree component has host-only construction and read-verification
tests and explicit checked-artifact integration with boot-stage.
This does not establish authenticated installed-root handoff or kernel admission.
No command or option currently qualifies this as a bootable hardened disk system.
The internal range verifier reuses one authenticated leaf hash block within
a request using bounded scratch space; every data block is still hashed.
Native tests and kernel-object compilation do not establish runtime performance.
An optional caller-bounded authenticated hash cache also has component tests.
Its provider concurrency and lifetime integration remain incomplete; no command
enables it as an installed-root backend.
The explicit verity_crypto_test kernel module tests OpenCrypto scatter/gather
SHA-256 requests; compile/link checks do not establish runtime correctness or
performance.
It is not installed, loaded or selected by normal build commands.
Its maintained instructions are in tools/test/construction/verity_crypto/README.
An accelerated verifier backend remains unimplemented.
An internal bounded builder can emit the combined filesystem and hash tree,
and boot-stage accepts explicit checked combined-image and descriptor inputs.
That input binding does not establish verified boot.

The loader has a bounded root-descriptor read path that verifies its private
buffer before copying it to load memory.
Kernel admission and full boot qualification remain incomplete.

Existing preload image results do not establish disk-backed system qualification.
Random-access streaming verifier contexts retain digests for completed pages and the bytes
of its partial final page. Backward reads compare the touched pages before
returning bytes; the whole-stream digest is still authenticated at close.
The native verified-replay regression covers altered full and partial replays,
restoration, page crossings, allocation failure and bounded replay I/O.
The non-seeking Lua stdio wrapper and streaming raw-file loader select an
enforced sequential restriction before reading. It allocates no replay table and rejects backward verified seeks;
whole-stream authentication at close remains mandatory.
Failed loads discard their verifier context and replay table without further
I/O or a verification success claim. File descriptors remain caller-owned.
For random-access contexts this adds one page hash per completed first-pass page and digest storage
proportional to file size. Loader memory and boot-time performance remain
unqualified. Authenticated consumption through every kernel, module and
root-descriptor load path remains an integrated qualification requirement.

.Ss Release trust rotation qualification
The maintained loader-trust-rotation-test builds native verifier profiles with
old-only, overlapping and new-only release anchors and tests real signatures,
foreign signer roles and explicit revocation.
It records public-anchor and build-artifact hashes.
These profiles do not attest an arbitrary EFI loader supplied to boot-stage.
The maintained EFI signer hashes nonempty sections in file-offset order;
uefi-digest-test and the staging signer regression check that calculation.
The existing boot-efi-signer input selects the signer executable and staging
records its digest. Signature-presence inspection is not cryptographic verification;
EFI signature and firmware verification remain explicitly separate results.
Removing an old anchor from a new loader does not revoke older signed loaders;
firmware enrollment/revocation, protected boot selection and rollback/recovery
remain separate integrated qualification requirements.

.Ss Explicit generation-trust loader build
The
.Cm loader-build
operation requires
.Fl loader-anchors Ar public-certificates.pem
and builds an unsigned EFI Lua loader using compiled generation trust anchors.
The PEM file may contain multiple public certificates for overlap during rotation;
private-key or mixed PEM inputs are rejected.
This operation currently supports native amd64 only and uses the requested
.Fl jobs
count (all logical CPUs by default).
.Pp
The output is a new directory below /usr/obj selected with
.Fl output .
It contains anchors.pem, inputs.json, build.log, an isolated obj tree and
loader-build.json recording the resulting executable and trust-header hashes.
The existing stand efi-loader target is used with firmware-db fallback for
manifest trust disabled and both make configuration files set to /dev/null.
Custom make configuration files and incremental builds are rejected for
this fixed trust profile. This does not change conventional loader builds.
.Pp
For example:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
build-bsd loader-build --loader-anchors /path/to/release-anchors.pem \
    --output /usr/obj/usr/src/loaders/release-candidate
.Ed
.Pp
This compiles software in a trusted builder; it does not independently attest
the source or toolchain. The receipt is not a release signature or proof of
firmware execution. Boot-stage requires the selected receipt and its SHA256
to bind the staged loader bytes. No firmware enrollment, signing, installation
or activation is performed by loader-build. Private signing keys are not inputs to this operation.

.Ss Binding an EFI staging input to its loader build
EFI signing through boot-stage requires
.Fl boot-loader-build Ar loader-build.json
and
.Fl boot-loader-build-sha256 Ar digest
in addition to the existing EFI inventory path and signing inputs.
Select the digest from the trusted builder output; it must not be treated as
independent source attestation merely because it accompanies a receipt.
.Pp
Staging verifies the pinned record, its supported compiled-trust profile, and
its loader size/hash against the staged inventory before invoking either signer.
It retains the exact selected record at efi/loader-build.json and records that
binding in stage.json. Missing, altered or mismatched inputs fail staging.
Paths named inside the receipt are not followed.
.Pp
This check binds artifacts to a selected build record. It does not establish
release-certificate authorization, independently attest compilation, or prove
firmware execution. Those results
remain separate and must not be inferred from staged_artifact_bound.

.Ss Paired native manifest verifier
The loader-build operation also builds loader-manifest-verify through its
maintained Makefile, with the same explicit public anchors and RSA/SHA256/SHA384
profile as the EFI loader. Both generated trust-header PEM payloads must match
the snapshotted public input. The build receipt records the native verifier's
path, executable digest and generated trust-header digest.
.Pp
The native tool accepts one manifest.sig path and uses the loader's actual
libsecureboot and BearSSL sources. It does not consult the system CA store or
import firmware trust. Its result concerns compiled generation anchors only;
it does not check firmware revocation, and certificate validity follows the
loader library's current default (expiry enforcement disabled).
EFI staging invokes the explicitly selected companion after checking its digest
against the pinned build record. Building it alone is not proof that a particular
staged generation is authorized.

.Ss Checking compiled generation trust during staging
EFI boot-stage additionally requires
.Fl boot-manifest-verifier Ar executable .
Its digest must match the companion verifier in the selected loader build
record. The path is explicitly selected; paths inside a receipt are never
executed implicitly.
.Pp
After generating the manifest signature, staging executes a checked private
snapshot of the verifier against the staged manifest, signature and certificates.
It checks their hashes before and after verification. Only an affirmative result
permits subsequent EFI signing and media publication. Rejection leaves no
successful stage.json. The result records compiled_generation_trust_verified;
firmware revocation, certificate expiry enforcement and firmware execution remain
separate unchecked properties. This verifies a signature under the selected
compiled anchors, not organizational approval or independent build attestation.

.Ss EFI signing payload preservation
The boot-stage backend checks that signing preserves every byte of the selected
unsigned loader except its PE checksum and certificate-directory fields.
The certificate table must be appended and cover exactly the remaining output.
This construction profile rejects pre-signed loader inputs.
The streaming comparison uses bounded buffers and adds no runtime I/O checks.
A successful transformation records executable_payload_preserved=true;
it does not establish cryptographic signature validity or firmware acceptance.

.Ss Independent EFI signature verification
EFI boot-stage requires boot-efi-signature-verifier, an explicitly selected
osslsigncode executable. After signing and payload preservation checks, staging
verifies Authenticode using the selected certificate and its exact DER leaf hash.
The signature_verified receipt field becomes true only after this check.
Firmware trust, boot success and revocation remain unverified by this operation.
The tools are trusted builder inputs, not executables chosen by a build receipt.

.Ss Root transition artifact construction
A verity-mode boot-policy JSON object may contain a transition object with
exactly these fields: version (1), transport_fs (ufs or zfs), transport_device,
provider, workers, cache_blocks, window_bytes, and buffer_bytes.
All selections and resource bounds are explicit inputs.
The image pathname comes from the outer boot policy; the descriptor digest comes
from checked generation artifacts.
Staging generates /boot/root-transition.bin and includes it in the manifest.
The bounded versioned record contains data only, with no mount options or scripts.
Transport and image strings must fit 1024-byte NUL-terminated fields, and the
provider name must fit 64 bytes.
Workers are a positive 32-bit count; cache entries are nonnegative, and window
and buffer sizes are positive signed-64-bit-range integers.
These encoding checks do not establish target MAXCPU, maxphys, block alignment
or sufficient provider allocation budget.
.Pp
The loader supports authenticated transition selection and retained preload
checks. Staging itself is construction evidence: its receipt reports
loader_consumed=false, kernel_admission_enabled=false and
target_resource_limits_verified=false. It does not enable disk-root boot.
The hardened kernel continues to require its existing immutable preloaded root.

.Ss Retained root transition handoff
When a transition is selected, the generated Lua contract also binds its path
and SHA256. The loader authenticates its exact bytes before switching to the
verified image and checks that its image path and descriptor digest match the
selection. It retains that private record and preloads it with the descriptor.
Final kernel handoff rejects missing, duplicate, changed or unselected transition
records. An older loader without the extended selection command cannot boot this
contract; a matching newly built loader is required. Staging requires root_transition_version=1 in the pinned build receipt for
this contract. Missing or unsupported declarations fail before either signer.
Kernel transition consumption remains unfinished.
No staging result implies firmware execution or completed disk-root admission.

.Ss Loader transition compatibility
The maintained loader-build profile records root_transition_version=1 in its
inputs and successful build receipt. Staging binds that declaration to the pinned
receipt and the selected loader bytes. A record without this declaration remains
usable only for contracts that do not require the transition artifact.
This is a trusted-builder compatibility statement, not independent source or
compiler attestation, firmware execution evidence, or activation authorization.

.Sh QUALIFICATION STATUS
BIP has been dropped. Its storage, session-policy, principal and provider-fixture
options have been removed from this frontend. The legacy Secure Boot harness
still requires separation from retired BIP fixtures; its historical results do
not qualify the current source tree. Independent Secure Boot and veriexec
remain supported development objectives, not completed qualification claims.

.Sh FIRMWARE QUALIFICATION
.Cm secureboot-test Fl test-case Cm firmware
runs only the independent amd64 UEFI firmware suite.
It requires explicit
.Fl test-firmware-code
and
.Fl test-firmware-vars
paths for the secure OVMF code and setup-mode variable template.
No staged operating-system root is required or accepted.
The existing
.Fl qemu , Fl qemu-data , Fl uefi-vars-tool
options select VM tools.
.Fl test-openssl , Fl test-uefisign , Fl test-makefs , Fl test-mkimg
can override native host tool paths.
The suite generates disposable keys in its private output directory, copies
all variable stores, and runs one 2048 MiB VM at a time.
It checks setup-mode execution, trusted execution, and rejection of unsigned,
untrusted-signer and modified images.
It also tests certificate revocation through an offline-generated dbx store:
the revoked signer must fail while a second trusted signer continues to work.
This proves boot-time revocation enforcement, not authenticated variable updates.
The probe uses the maintained EFI startup code and build libraries; preparatory
loader compilation is not loader execution qualification.
This suite does not qualify authenticated key rotation, recovery, the operating
system loader, veriexec, system startup or protection against root.

The suite audits exported variable stores before starting guests.
Only the intended dbx certificate append may differ; both enrolled certificates
must remain in db and the databases must retain authenticated-write attributes.

The authenticated-update client also tests a KEK-signed db replacement,
unauthorized updates and replay rejection through firmware SetVariable.
The replacement signer must be rejected before the update and accepted on a
subsequent boot; the retired signer must then be rejected.
Only public payloads and signatures are copied to the VM; private keys stay
in the host test output directory.
This does not qualify PK/KEK rotation, recovery or OS runtime update tooling.

A subsequent fresh guest replays the original db update against the persisted
store and must reject it. A newly signed update with a later timestamp must
succeed, and replaying that newer update must also be rejected.

.Ss Retired BIP loader fixtures
The protected dynamic-loader fixtures and their build inputs have been removed.
The legacy combined Secure Boot runner rejects BIP kernel qualification; it
still contains other retired dependencies and is not an operational OS test.
Use the independent firmware test case for its documented firmware-only scope.
This does not qualify the complete loader, kernel and installed-system chain.

.Ss Bazel build qualification
.Cm bazel-test
runs maintained native amd64 qualification and explicitly selected
cross-target kernel artifact qualification.
Bazel 9 or later is the only supported Bazel, with Bzlmod and rules_cc;
Bazel 5 and the WORKSPACE mode are no longer supported.
.Pp
Native iconv and fstyp default-path qualification uses the normal format-5
.Cm bazel-test
artifact/test selection plus the explicit-input preparer
.Pa tools/build/bazel/iconv-runtime-fixture.py .
The preparer requires a completed qualification and a versioned JSON plan naming
native archives, artifact destinations, kernel, boot inputs and fixture helpers.
It stages a disposable guest fixture under /usr/obj;
it does not install on the host or publish an OS image.
The maintained daemon-runtime QEMU harness supplies the disk-backed guest and
resource bounds.
SDK Python/loader bootstrap inputs remain separately identified.
See
.Pa tools/build/bazel/ICONV-RUNTIME-QUALIFICATION.md
for input schema, retained independent comparisons and actual runtime evidence.
The follow-up
.Pa tools/build/bazel/MAKEFS-QUALIFICATION.md
records native makefs default/reduced builds, complete helper installation,
and all 22 original fstyp cases including guest-only md/newfs and cleanup.
The standard base group file is an explicit fixture input;
root-only requirements and original test assertions are retained.
The complete makefs suite inventory and disposable guest runner are described in
.Pa tools/build/bazel/MAKEFS-RUNTIME-QUALIFICATION.md .
Typed ZFS selection retains the original conditional suite installation.
The maintained harness supports a separate private sparse scratch disk with
explicit sampled host allocation/free-space bounds; it stages no world tools
when used independently of its ZFS bootstrap mode.
Original ATF requirements, timeouts, cleanup and source skips are preserved.
The maintained permissions test accepts either capitalization of the configured
shell's permission-denied diagnostic; mode and nonzero-exit checks remain intact.
The explicit fixture plan may supply checker_profile.timeout_scale, an integer
from 1 through 20 with default 1.
The checker records both original ATF deadlines and uniformly scaled effective
body/cleanup deadlines; it permits no per-case exceptions.
Scaled emulation results remain distinct from original-timeout qualification.
Outer adapter/VM budgets are separate recorded harness inputs.
The complete maintained makefs fixture suite has executed all 63 identities
under the explicit scale-5 profile: 60 pass, three retain source skips, and
per-case/final cleanup and normal shutdown pass.
This does not qualify corrected-fixture execution under original deadlines.
This fixture does not qualify normal Capsule startup, SSH,
complete pkgbase images or controlled performance.
.Pp
.Fl bazel Ar path
selects the executable; the default is
.Pa /usr/local/bin/bazel ,
where the bazel9 package installs it.
Each qualification tool runs
.Ql bazel --version
before creating evidence output and refuses a version older than 9.
The declared pilot also requires
.Fl bazel-modules Ar file ,
a JSON file naming its offline module inputs:
.Dq repository_cache
(a content-addressed repository cache whose entries must match their
SHA-256 names),
.Dq distdirs
(an array of directories of regular files) and optionally
.Dq java_home
(the Bazel server JDK; omitted, the launcher's built-in default is used).
The module versions and the
.Pa MODULE.bazel.lock
are pinned in
.Pa tools/build/bazel
and used with
.Fl -lockfile_mode=error
and downloads disabled; the repository cache is copied into the private
output before Bazel runs, because Bazel writes to its repository cache.
The lockfile cannot be regenerated offline
.Po
.Ql bazel mod deps
evaluates every module extension, and rules_python's needs a Python 3.11
toolchain
.Pc ;
.Pa tools/build/bazel/README.md
describes refreshing the pins when network access is allowed.
The action also requires a matching development world SDK in the standard
object tree.
.Fl jobs
selects parallelism (all logical CPUs by default);
.Fl output
selects a fresh directory below /usr/obj.
It builds fresh Make reference libraries and compares Bazel artifacts,
including versioned exports and generated-input invalidation.
It also builds the declared Bazel targets of the OpenTelemetry
components
.Po
.Pa lib/libotelexport ,
.Pa cddl/lib/libdtotel ,
.Pa cddl/usr.sbin/bsdinstruments ,
.Pa usr.sbin/hwstat ,
.Pa usr.sbin/otelconf ,
.Pa usr.sbin/otelpfd ,
.Pa usr.sbin/otelauditd
and their test programs
.Pc
and compares their compile and link flags, archive members, symbols
and program ELF metadata with independent Make builds of the same sources.
These programs are not executed by this action.
The same components are also ordinary maintained native targets, selected
through format-5
.Fl -bazel-inputs
like any other component:
the native graph provides the Protocol Buffers closure
.Po
.Pa lib/libabsl ,
.Pa lib/protobuf ,
.Pa lib/libnghttp2 ,
.Pa usr.bin/protoc
and the
.Pa libexec/protoc-gen-upb*
plugins
.Pc ,
generates the OTLP bindings with uninstrumented execution-platform
.Dq :bootstrap
variants of protoc and the plugins, and builds libdtrace,
.Xr dtrace 1
and the OpenTelemetry programs with their installation payloads.
DTrace provider generation in the native graph uses
.Dq //cddl/usr.sbin/dtrace:bootstrap
and its
.Dq bootstrap_runtime
libraries, so the instrumented native libraries never need
.Xr dtrace 1
in their own configuration.
The separate libdtrace and
.Xr dtrace 1
probe
.Pa tools/build/bazel/dtrace-probe.py
and the Protocol Buffers, Abseil and nghttp2 probe
.Pa tools/build/bazel/protobuf-probe.py
are not part of this action; they take the same
.Fl -bazel
and
.Fl -bzlmod-inputs
arguments and are described in
.Pa tools/build/bazel/README.md .
The action also builds and stages a Make-only libmd installation reference
under its private output directory, preserving the selected world profile,
source layout, debug splitting and METALOG package metadata.
This does not establish Bazel installation parity.
The action does not launch a VM,
qualify performance, install packages or select Bazel for production builds.
Make remains the production backend.
Custom make configurations, signing and incremental reuse are not supported
by this qualification action.

.Ss Native Bazel qualification inputs
With
.Fl bazel-inputs Ar file ,
.Cm bazel-test
selects native libmd graph, test and stage-construction qualification using an
explicitly prepared workspace.
The file is JSON with integer
.Dq format
1 and these required path fields:
.Dq plan ,
.Dq test-plan ,
.Dq install-plan ,
.Dq test-runtime ,
.Dq library-install-plan ,
.Dq compression-tools ,
.Dq directory-policy ,
.Dq install-tools ,
.Dq toolchain-receipt ,
.Dq java-home ,
.Dq repository-cache ,
and
.Dq output-user-root .
.Dq distdirs
is an array of absolute directory paths.
All paths must be absolute; workspace, evidence and Bazel output roots belong
under /usr/obj.
The tool verifies prepared toolchain hashes, regenerates the native graph,
runs Bazel tests with the resolved module lockfile and downloads disabled,
and checks test results against the selected test inventory.
It records commands, input hashes and CPU, memory and block-I/O counters.
The staged paths, metadata, payload bytes and link identity are verified
against the hash-bound existing Make installation reference and selected
Bazel outputs.
This invocation does not rebuild the Make reference, bootstrap a fresh SDK,
qualify sandbox isolation or produce complete system packages.
The default pilot remains available by omitting
.Fl bazel-inputs ;
.Fl bazel-modules
applies only to it.
Both paths require Bazel 9 or later and neither selects Bazel as the
production backend.

.Pp
Input format 2 prepares a fresh workspace before running the same native
qualification and installation-parity gates.
Its required file fields are
.Dq profile ,
.Dq execution-tools ,
.Dq test-runtime ,
.Dq compression-tools ,
.Dq install-tools ,
and
.Dq module-lock .
Its directory fields are
.Dq reference ,
.Dq java-home ,
and
.Dq repository-cache ;
.Dq distdirs
is a nonempty array of directory paths.
The execution-tools file names the generator executables and SDK inspection
tools; tool receipts and runtime configuration use their maintained schemas.
Build-time generators must use tools compatible with the execution host.
For example, the mkimg test fixture generator requires an explicitly selected
.Dq jot
entry in
.Dq additional_execution_tools .
The staging helper fingerprints that executable and records its runtime
closure; it does not search the host PATH as a fallback.
The bc/dc source generators similarly require an explicitly selected
.Dq dirname
entry in
.Dq additional_execution_tools .
These bootstrap tools do not replace the Bazel-built programs under test.
The reference directory contains the verified Make installation and METALOG.
The selected module lock is copied explicitly; repository downloads remain
disabled during qualification.
.Pp
The requested fresh output directory contains newly exported source and SDK
snapshots, test and installation policies, a new Bazel output root, preparation
logs, generated format-1 inputs, and the qualification receipt.
Input overlap is rejected before preparation.
Source/configuration hashes are rechecked before success is recorded.
This mode reuses the selected SDK, static execution tools and Make reference;
it does not claim to bootstrap those inputs.

.Sh MAINTAINED BAZEL SOURCE GRAPH
Input format 5 to
.Cm bazel-test
.Fl bazel-inputs
builds maintained BUILD targets directly from the selected source tree.
It does not extract Make component metadata or generate component BUILD files.
Required fields are format (5), toolchain-workspace, toolchain-receipt,
java-home, repository-cache, distdirs, targets and tests.
Paths must be absolute; targets must be a nonempty array of explicit source labels.
The tests array lists selected test or test_suite targets and may be empty for
build-only work.
Every listed label must be included in targets.
The frontend expands suites using a configured Bazel query with the same build
settings, records test-inventory.json and binds its hash in the receipt.
Every expanded test must have exactly one successful Bazel action summary;
missing, additional, duplicate, skipped or failed action summaries are rejected.
Action success does not establish that every test case passed.
The frontend retains test logs and XML under test-reports, binds their hashes,
and records passing, skipped, expected-failure and failed case counts separately.
Counts cover all recorded attempts, including retries.
On failure, available test logs and XML are retained under failure-test-reports.
FAILED.json binds that diagnostic manifest and records observed test labels,
missing or malformed case evidence, and event-stream parsing errors.
These diagnostics do not establish a complete configured test inventory;
the invocation remains failed even if some recorded cases passed.
Report-capture errors are recorded without replacing the original failure.
The native ATF adapter reports expected failures and skips as XML skipped cases
with explicit reasons; they are not counted as passing cases.
Callers without XML reporting still require an actual pass.
Crashes, cleanup failures, malformed results and unsupported expected
exit, signal or timeout outcomes still fail the action.
A nonempty tests array that expands to no tests is also rejected.
Cached passing results are accepted and remain identified in Bazel's logs.
The source MODULE.bazel and lockfile define the dependency graph; the verified
prepared SDK is supplied as the bsd_sdk module override.
The Fuchsia runtime source view is a local repository declared by MODULE.bazel.
It resolves its source root from the checkout's exported source-marker label
and uses maintained native BUILD metadata without changing imported BUILD files.
It requires no separate source checkout path or prebuilt FIDL library.
This source view does not by itself establish invalidation, reproducibility,
installation or runtime qualification.
Invocation logs and receipts remain beneath the selected /usr/obj output.
Persistent Bazel state and verified SDK snapshots reside under
/usr/obj/usr/src/bazel-state, keyed by the canonical source checkout path.
The frontend serializes invocations using that state; Bazel owns configuration
and input invalidation. Evidence output must not overlap build state.
The Bazel server exits after 300 idle seconds. SDK snapshots are copied once per
content identity and verified before reuse; altered snapshots are rejected.
The SDK receipt's source, host dependency and snapshot bindings must all match.
This mode still uses a prepared SDK and does not establish bootstrap, installation,
package parity, publication, full-system or performance qualification.
.Pp
Native FIDL test installation uses the tests and googletest boolean build settings;
GoogleTest-disabled installation omits its runtime test, suite entry and notice.
Plain program test adapters execute the same binaries consumed by installation
rules, preserving default runfiles and process exit status without recompilation.
They do not enforce kernel identity, privilege or Kyua metadata prerequisites.
.Pp
Native FIDL compilation declares its schema compiler in execution configuration.
Optional runtime_libraries are explicit execution artifacts used to construct
its library search path; they do not select host-installed fallback libraries.
Binding generation declares the Python modules, templates and exact source/header
output inventory. Generated headers propagate through normal CcInfo dependencies.
These rules do not establish full FIDL runtime or cross-build qualification.
.Pp
Native shell generators may declare an ordered list of additional source
artifacts after their primary input; these are build dependencies and command
arguments, not paths discovered by the generator.
Generator PATH tools remain explicit SDK or source targets.
The shell build groups each generator's declared outputs in its own working
directory and rejects diagnostics from generators whose successful contract
has no stderr output, including otherwise hidden shell-pipeline failures.
.Pp
Format-5 runs capture locally available regular-file outputs from the default,
metadata and directory_metadata Bazel output groups before releasing the
frontend build lock.
The artifacts/manifest.json file records target/configuration identities,
original names and modes, content hashes and private stored copies.
The receipt includes the manifest hash.
Use these copies for later comparisons: bazel-bin and bazel-out symlinks still
refer to mutable shared build state.
Capture rejects missing files, unsupported remote URIs and mismatches with
Bazel's reported SHA-256 digest.
It does not capture test runfiles, directory layout or other output groups,
and does not establish installation or package parity.
.Pp
For qualification only, the
.Cm //tools/build/bazel/config:experimental_dtrace_section_archives
boolean selects section-associated static DTrace objects in libcapsulert and
libchannel, libservice and libfidl when DTrace is enabled.
It defaults to false.
Live tracing, complete package validation, and resource gates remain open;
this is not a production backend selection.
.Pp
The native ksu installation setting
.Dv //tools/build/bazel/config:enable_suid_k5su
is a boolean, defaulting to false.
True selects mode 4555 and the schg file flag, matching Make's
.Dv ENABLE_SUID_K5SU
installation contract; false selects mode 0555 without that flag.
This selects archive metadata and does not install or execute ksu.
.Pp
The optional build-settings object maps source target labels to JSON boolean,
integer or string values for declared Bazel settings.
Native command-line flags and composite values are not accepted in this object.
The boolean //tools/build/bazel/config:kernel_zstdio defaults to true for
native amd64 GENERIC configuration.
It explicitly overrides ZSTDIO through the native config parser and is also
available to native conditional source selection.
False removes that option; it does not select a different installed kernel.
Both states have been checked for generated option headers and native uzip
source selection, Make artifact/debug parity, and staged installation metadata.
The observed option change recompiles only the affected uzip translation unit.
Decompression runtime, resource/performance and whole-kernel qualification
remain separate.
The boolean //tools/build/bazel/config:veriexec_debug defaults to true and
preserves the core module's VERIFIED_EXEC_DEBUG compiler definition;
false corresponds to Make WITHOUT_VERIEXEC_DEBUG.
Current core sources do not consume that definition, so both settings produce
identical artifacts. Both variants have Make artifact and staged metadata
comparisons; live enforcement remains unqualified.
.Pp
The boolean //tools/build/bazel/config:werror defaults to true, matching the
maintained MK_WERROR default for amd64 kernel and module compilation.
False omits -Werror while retaining the kernel warning flags.
The qualification targets cover fourteen converted GENERIC amd64 Netgraph
modules and one two-object built-in kernel group; the complete kernel is not
qualified by those checks.
For example, //tools/build/bazel/config:split_manpages set to true selects
separate manual-page package ownership; false is the maintained default.
The boolean //sbin/growfs:gfsdbg defaults to false and selects the maintained
growfs GFSDBG developer variant, adding debug.c and FS_DEBUG.
Its binary symbols and linkage have been compared with Make GFSDBG=1;
runtime and variant installation qualification remain separate.
The boolean //sbin/mksnap_ffs:nosuid defaults to false; true selects
Make's NOSUID installation mode 0554 instead of 4554.
Archive metadata qualification does not establish installed setuid behavior.
The label setting //usr.bin/getaddrinfo:socket_header defaults to
//sys/sys:socket.h and selects the declared header used by the socket-table
generator, corresponding to Make's SYS_SOCKET_H override.
Supply a Bazel target label as its string value, not an ambient filesystem path.
An alternate header must describe the ABI used to compile the consumer.
.Pp
The boolean //tools/build/bazel/config:tests defaults to true.
The BSDNotify daemon_install_archive target covers only the daemon package.
Its install_archive target also includes the maintained test package when the
tests setting is enabled.
Native broker, transport, policy and dispatcher tests are separately selectable
targets; session runtime requires a matching kernel.
The BSDSysctl observability test accepts an ATF binary configuration value;
its Bazel runner supplies the built daemon while installed tests retain the
normal system path by default.
The contract inspects instrumentation and the ELF DOF section; it does not
establish live probe firing.
.Pp
Tests that require regular fixture files can set materialize_suite in their
maintained Bazel rule to copy the declared suite into private test storage.
This preserves file-loader checks that reject symlinks in ordinary Bazel runfiles.
.Pp
The sysctl, ed, pkill, daemon, timeout, pwait, col, column, hexdump, xargs, sysdecode, cap_grp, cap_pwd, cap_net, cap_sysctl, cap_dns, cap_netdb, cap_mkdb, ident, limits, asa, csplit, locale, lastcomm, getconf, procstat, libshmring, libauditcmp, liblogcmp, libtimecmp, libpowercmp, libnotify, libsysctlcmp, libnetworkcmp, libtracecmp, libcryptocmp, libcryptodesc, libdevicecmp, liboes, libbsdfilesystem, BSDLog, BSDAudit, BSDTime, BSDPower, BSDPowerctl, BSDNotify, BSDMemory, BSDSysctl, BSDVM, BSDNetwork, BSDTrace, BSDCrypto, BSDExtension, BSDDevice, BSDNamespace, BSDFilesystem, sysctlcmpctl, sysextctl, tracectl, tzfsctl, newfs_msdos, fsck_msdosfs, tunefs, swapon, reboot, savecore and pr installation
candidates use it to include or exclude test payloads and their package-owned
directories,
corresponding to MK_TESTS.
Explicit test targets remain available. Other components are not yet covered
by this setting.
.Pp
The boolean //tools/build/bazel/config:pf defaults to true.
It selects PF header inclusion in the native sysdecode ioctl generator
and the matching library compilation definition, corresponding to MK_PF.
Both PF selections have generator output comparison evidence.
The default library build, maintained test and installation match have been
checked; this does not establish whole-system qualification.
.Pp
The boolean //sbin/shutdown:nosuid selects mode 0554 instead of 4554 for
the shutdown and poweroff installation records, matching Make's NOSUID setting.
The default mode has artifact parity evidence; the NOSUID variant and lifecycle
runtime behavior require separate qualification.
.Pp
The targets //lib/geom:plugins and //lib/geom:installation_archives select
all configured GEOM class plugins and their individual installation archives.
The boolean //tools/build/bazel/config:openssl controls GELI inclusion and the
matching frontend executable alias together.
These aggregates do not produce a merged filesystem or pkgbase package.
.Pp
The string //tools/build/bazel/config:geom_class_dir defaults to /lib/geom
and selects the native GEOM frontend's compiled plugin search directory.
It must be an absolute path without control characters.
The frontend and NOP plugin use this setting for loader configuration and
plugin/debug staging respectively.
Default frontend and NOP plugin build/installation have Make comparison evidence;
Staging at /usr/lib has artifact comparison evidence.
The label //tools/build/bazel/config:geom_directory_policy selects a supplemental
mtree file for custom directories, relative to the installation root.
It defaults to an empty file. Set it to a declared source label in build-settings.
Missing directory policy and conflicting ownership or modes are rejected.
Explicit-policy staging at /opt/5bsd/geom has artifact comparison evidence.
Custom-directory execution and rescue static-class support remain unqualified.
.Pp
The boolean //tools/build/bazel/config:ofed defaults to true.
It selects native libpcap RDMA capture sources and the ibverbs, bnxtre and mlx5
library dependencies, corresponding to MK_OFED rather than MK_OFED_EXTRA.
Enabled and disabled profiles have static/shared symbol, linkage and installation
comparison evidence and bounded offline filter/savefile runtime tests.
Live capture, RDMA hardware and performance qualification remain pending.
This does not configure every OFED consumer or kernel driver.
Native libpcap also uses the pf and inet6_support settings.
.Pp
The boolean //tools/build/bazel/config:inet_support defaults to true.
The native jail and jls commands select INET with this setting and INET6 with
inet6_support; these are compile-time feature selections.
Only the dual-stack default has jail comparison evidence so far.
.Pp
The boolean //tools/build/bazel/config:jail defaults to true and controls
JAIL support and the libjail dependency in the native sysctl candidate.
Enabled and disabled sysctl builds have Make installation and read-only
comparison evidence, including rejection of jail targeting when disabled.
This is not yet a global replacement for every MK_JAIL consumer.
.Pp
The boolean //tools/build/bazel/config:dtrace defaults to true and controls
DTrace instrumentation in migrated consumers, including Casper, cap_fileargs,
libutil, libpam, and libfetch.
False selects ordinary compilation without provider-generation/link inputs.
This setting does not yet configure the kernel or every base component.
Native setkey selects its INET and INET6 definitions through inet_support and
inet6_support. The IPv6-disabled command has artifact parity and native libipsec
policy-test evidence; this does not qualify kernel PF_KEY operations or a complete
IPv6-disabled system.
.Pp
The boolean //tools/build/bazel/config:inet6_support defaults to true and
controls the native libutil and libfetch IPv6 compilation paths.
It does not configure every base component or the kernel.
Only the enabled libutil and libfetch profiles have been qualified so far.
The boolean //tools/build/bazel/config:openssl defaults to true; libfetch
selects native libssl/libcrypto when enabled and native libmd when disabled.
The libfetch disabled-TLS profile remains unqualified.
The boolean //tools/build/bazel/config:audit defaults to true and controls
native id audit-session reporting support (the MK_AUDIT equivalent).
It does not enable auditing in the running kernel.
.Pp
The boolean //tools/build/bazel/config:ps_check_keywords defaults to false
and enables native ps keyword-table diagnostics (PS_CHECK_KEYWORDS).
This diagnostic profile is separate from normal process-query qualification.
.Pp
The boolean //tools/build/bazel/config:nls defaults to true and controls
native libc message-catalog installation (MK_NLS), independently of locales.
.Pp
The boolean //tools/build/bazel/config:locales defaults to true and
controls optional native LC_CTYPE data and aliases (MK_LOCALES).
C.UTF-8 remains installed when this option is false.
.Pp
The boolean //tools/build/bazel/config:ls_colors defaults to true and
controls native ls color support (MK_LS_COLORS), including terminal-library linkage.
.Pp
The boolean //tools/build/bazel/config:sort_threads defaults to false and
controls native sort threading definitions, pthread linkage and manual text.
Both sort thread settings have selected installation and runtime qualification;
parallel performance and exhaustive option coverage remain unqualified.
The current source requires CASPER in src.opts.mk.
Native libcasper and cap_fileargs installation archives and normal
rtsol/rtsold/savecore binary and installation targets reject casper=false;
a headers-only archive is not a supported system installation profile.
The boolean //tools/build/bazel/config:casper defaults to true and controls
Casper linkage and WITH_CASPER compilation in native head, tail, wc, and paste.
This setting does not yet configure every Casper consumer; the disabled
profile remains unqualified for these utilities.
The boolean //tools/build/bazel/config:cddl defaults to true and controls
the native libprocstat ZFS source and compilation definitions.
It does not yet configure all CDDL components; only the enabled profile
has been qualified for libprocstat.
The boolean //tools/build/bazel/config:ctf defaults to false, matching
MK_CTF, and controls libproc CTF support.
It becomes effective only when cddl and dtrace are also enabled.
It does not enable object CTF conversion throughout world or kernel.
Bazel validates each setting against its declared type.
.Pp
The initial maintained graph covers compilation of libbz2, bzip2 and bzip2recover.
Native zlib static/shared targets and upstream example tests are also available.
The shared test uses an explicit test-only loader path to the candidate library.
The native zlib installation inventory matches Make for ten entries, including
headers, pkg-config, manuals, links and debuglink CRC. Static archive membership
and external symbol names/types also match. The default three zlib pkgbase
archives match independently built Make package metadata and file membership;
payload extraction and links are verified. Package installation, split-manual
packaging and cross-platform qualification remain open. External consumers of
extracted zlib packages pass upstream static/shared examples with loader selection
checked. Native BSD zopen/zdopen tests also pass, including rewind and descriptor
ownership, and agree with external consumers of Make libraries.
The native gzip candidate uses those compression libraries and declared DTrace
header/object generation. Bounded four-format runtime checks and a maintained
zstd EOF regression pass for Make and Bazel. All ten installed gzip ATF cases
pass against each selected binary with private candidate libraries; the test
harness and unchanged shell utilities come from Make staging. Native test
registration and package qualification remain pending. The native non-test gzip
installation inventory matches Make for all 24 entries, including script/manual
bytes, hardlink relationships and debuglink CRC; its stripped payload also passes
all ten cases. Gzip and md5 direct shared dependencies now match Make after
removing redundant transitive entries. The corrected digest passes bounded
matching-kernel VM checks; its full ATF suite remains open. Split-manual
configuration remains unqualified.
The amd64 liblzma/xz candidates build with declared BSD configuration and native
libmd dependencies; bounded ABI and CRC64/SHA256 roundtrip comparisons pass.
Its native 20-entry file installation inventory matches Make, including fifteen
public headers, pkg-config, archive membership/symbols and debuglink CRC. Parent
directory metadata, installed consumers and complete xz packages remain open.
Native xzdec and lzmainfo targets and installation manifests for all three xz
commands are available. Their 21 file entries match Make, including twelve
hardlink relationships, manual bytes and debuglink CRCs. Bounded stripped-binary
roundtrip, truncation and information-output comparisons pass; full upstream
tests remain open. The four default xz package archives match independent Make
package metadata, directory/file membership and shared-library requirements.
Extraction and all nine installed command names pass bounded runtime checks;
package installation, split-manual packaging and performance remain unqualified.
The amd64 zstd library and CLI candidates also build natively, with bounded
Make ABI and compression/decompression comparisons. Native private-library and
command installation inventories match Make for fifteen entries; the aggregate
archive retains both compilation groups and matches external symbol coverage.
Both Make and Bazel now install the required zstd_errors.h header. Four default
zstd packages match independent Make metadata and file membership. Native
static/shared tests and external installed-header consumers pass bounded
roundtrip/truncation checks; all four command aliases pass. Package installation,
full upstream tests, configuration variants and performance remain unqualified.
Native libbsdxml targets compile the five maintained sources and generate BSD
public headers with declared transformations. Headers and shared export names/types
match Make. Bounded static/shared parser tests pass with candidate linkage checked.
Its seven-entry component installation inventory also matches Make; external
consumers of the actual archive and stripped library pass. Complete runtime-family
packaging and upstream test-suite qualification remain open.
The initial native OpenSSL buildinfo target derives compiler identity from the
resolved toolchain and matches fresh Make build-information declarations for
amd64. The maintained amd64_sources inventory resolves 949 C and 38 assembly
inputs without Make evaluation; its source membership matches the selected Make
reference. Native libcrypto compilation now preserves the ordinary archive,
PIC shared objects, assembly and per-file flags. Archive membership, external
symbol records, shared exports and required libraries match Make for the selected
amd64 profile. Two native static/shared API tests pass, with external Make and
Bazel consumers also checked. A newly staged SDK must provide bsd_openssl_pic;
older SDKs are rejected. The typed openssl_ktls setting is available, but only its
default enabled profile is qualified. Native targets also cover four engines and
the legacy and FIPS providers; their exports and runtime dependencies match Make.
A native legacy-provider/engine loading test passes. The maintained seven-case
FIPS suite passes against both Make and Bazel modules using an external OpenSSL
driver with checked library selection. FIPS remains a non-validated integration;
hardware engine operation is not qualified. The openssl_fips boolean controls
provider inclusion in the aggregate. Native libssl and the OpenSSL CLI now build;
libssl archive/export inventories and both runtime dependency lists match Make.
Static/shared TLS 1.2 and 1.3 tests pass for data exchange and peer-verification
rejection. All eight maintained provider cases pass through the native CLI and
through the Make reference, with each build's own libraries/modules selected.
The ATF harness remains external. The native non-test OpenSSL installation
archive contains 6,548 matching file/link entries, including public headers,
manual aliases, configuration and separate debug files. All nine debug links
validate, and the stripped staged artifacts pass the provider and installed-header
TLS checks. Four native OpenSSL pkgbase packages now match independent Make
package metadata and file partitions, including configuration and directory
metadata. Extracted package payloads pass provider and installed-header TLS
checks. Package installation, dependency resolution, upgrades, full upstream
tests, network/KTLS/DTLS/QUIC coverage, other profiles and performance remain open.
The native libarchive candidate builds 123 C sources, ordinary/PIC archives and
DTrace provider objects. Its shared symbol inventory and dependencies match Make;
static symbol inventories match only after independently verified DTrace
object-path hashes are normalized. Static/shared compression and encrypted ZIP
memory roundtrip tests pass with native migrated dependencies selected.
Host libc/libthr/libsys remain runtime dependencies. The native installation
archive has 259 entries matching Make paths, metadata and nonbinary contents,
including 215 manual hardlinks and a validated debug link. An installed-header
consumer passes against the stripped staged library. Three native libarchive
pkgbase packages match independent Make metadata, file partitions, dependencies
and directory ownership. Extracted package payloads pass the same consumer test.
Package transactions, full upstream tests, alternate profiles, active tracing and performance remain
unqualified. The iconv setting requires a freshly staged SDK and applies only
to PIC compilation, matching Make.
The native cpio candidate matches Make version output and direct shared-library
dependencies. Both binaries pass 49 maintained tests with seven reported skips,
using an external Make-built harness and explicitly selected native libraries.
Native cpio test harness, installation/packages and performance remain open.
The native tar candidate includes archiveop DTrace generation and matches Make
version output and direct library dependencies. Both binaries contain DTrace
metadata and pass 73 maintained tests with six reported skips, using an external
Make-built harness and explicitly selected native libraries. Live probe activation,
native test harness, installation/packages, rescue/bootstrap and performance
remain unqualified.
Tar and cpio also provide native component installation archives. Each has five
entries matching Make component metadata, manual bytes and link relationships,
with validated separate debug links. Tar belongs to runtime and cpio to utilities;
these archives do not represent either complete package family.
Tar and cpio test harnesses now also build natively, with generated registries
matching Make byte-for-byte. Native harnesses pass the same 73 and 49 tests,
respectively, with six and seven skips, using an external qualification driver.
Both suites now also pass through native Bazel test targets with declared
program, harness, reference fixtures and migrated libraries in runfiles. Host
utilities and OS runtime remain dependencies; the suites are local and
noncacheable until those inputs are modeled. Test-package installation remains open.
Unzip also builds natively with component installation parity and a native Bazel
test suite: 28 cases pass with no skips. Version, direct dependencies and generated
test registry match Make; installation metadata, aliases, manuals and debug links
are verified. This does not qualify the complete utilities package or performance.
The libelf candidate builds 59 ordinary and three m4-generated C sources.
Its 62 archive members, static/dynamic symbol inventories and direct dependencies
match Make. Generated source content matches after normalizing only the origin
comment's source-path prefix; raw bytes differ. Native static/shared tests pass
ELF32/ELF64 creation, little/big-endian emitted-byte checks, section reopening
and truncated-header rejection. The same consumer passes against Make and native
libraries. Full API coverage, installation/packages and performance remain open.
Libelf installation now has 100 entries matching Make component metadata,
including 49 manuals, 45 hardlinks, public headers and a validated debug link.
This does not establish complete runtime-family package construction.
The native ar candidate includes declared lex/yacc generation and native
libarchive/libelf dependencies. Generated parser headers match Make; generated C
matches after only known path normalization in line directives. Both binaries
pass bounded archive editing/indexing, long-member, link-and-execute and MRI
script checks. Native test integration, installation/packages and performance
remain open.
Ar's native installation archive matches five Make entries, including executable
and manual hardlinks and separate debug metadata. Staged stripped ar/ranlib pass
ordinary/MRI archive indexing and link-and-execute checks. Complete toolchain
packaging and native test integration remain open.
The maintained ELF debug verifier reads bytes without rewriting the inspected
file. A subsequent audit of 15 staged native ELF/debug pairs passes its checks
and confirms unchanged hashes, modes, inode identities and link counts.
Ar now has a native Bazel functional test using declared executable/library
runfiles and direct process execution. Archive creation, listing, long names,
delete/reinsert/replace, ranlib invocation and MRI payload checks pass. Object
symbol-index/link validation remains separate external qualification evidence.
The internal libelftc candidate builds 14 source objects with native libelf
headers/dependencies and the maintained local version source. Its ordinary archive
members and 37 global symbol records match Make. No installed shared library is
introduced. Runtime consumers and performance remain unqualified.
Size and strings build in the normal Casper-enabled native profile, using a
separate internal libelftc PIE variant matching Make's library selection.
Their direct library dependencies match Make. Both Make and native startup checks
fail on the older host libc's missing cap_rights_intersect version; runtime is
not qualified. Bootstrap/NXB/disabled profiles and installation remain open.
Size and strings each have a three-entry component installation archive matching
Make metadata and manuals, with separate debug files verified without mutation.
The internal libelftc PIE archive also matches Make's 14 members and 37 global
symbol records. Matching-OS runtime qualification remains required.
C++filt builds natively with direct dependencies matching Make, but both CLI
startup checks fail on the older host libc's missing cap_rights_intersect symbol.
A separate native libelftc archive test passes bounded demangling, short-buffer
and string-table checks. CLI runtime, installation and performance remain open.
The target //lib/libmd:highlevel_sources provides the fourteen declared high-level
API source generators using the maintained libmd script and explicit SDK tools.
This target alone does not compile or qualify libmd.
The target //lib/libmd:compilation builds the declared amd64 C and assembly
groups in PIC and non-PIC variants with separate per-source settings.
It produces intermediate component archives, not an installable libmd library.
The experimental //lib/libmd:md aggregate uses an explicit rules_cc module
patch selecting PIC objects instead of duplicate PIC and non-PIC sets.
Its object coverage and global symbol inventory match the Make reference.
SDKs staged with the configured Python runtime enable the standard
ValidateStaticLibrary action, which rejects duplicate strong definitions.
Runtime, performance and installation remain unqualified;
this target is not a completed library migration.
The //lib/libmd/tests:tests suite provides fourteen maintained digest-vector
comparisons using drivers linked against the native aggregate static archive.
This does not qualify shared-library or installation behavior.
The //lib/libmd:md_shared target builds libmd.so.7 with a generated version map.
Its version map and defined export inventory match the Make reference,
and the configured Clang userland toolchain enables stack-protector-strong.
The //lib/libmd/tests:shared_tests suite compares all fourteen digest references
using the candidate shared library.
The //lib/libmd:md_install target supplies a stripped shared library with
a separate debug file and verified GNU debug-link CRC.
The //lib/libmd/tests:stripped_tests suite runs fourteen digest comparisons
against that stripped artifact.
The //lib/libnv:nv and //lib/libnv:nv_shared targets provide native libnv
compilation and versioned linkage as a Casper dependency.
Runtime and installation qualification for these targets remains open.
The //lib/libmd:manuals target generates and compresses all seven maintained
manuals with declared template, copyright and execution-tool inputs.
The //lib/libmd:install_manifest target declares the runtime library, header,
manual and link inventory; its default metadata matches a private Make stage.
The //lib/libmd/tests:install_manifest target supplies all 44 test-package
payloads, including shared drivers, debug files, references and ATF metadata.
Private staging passes the fourteen ATF tests against the candidate library;
complete package assembly and lifecycle parity remain open.
The //lib/libmd:component_archive target serializes both installation manifests
with declared source directory/account policy and explicit install-created
directories. It is a component archive, not a complete pkgbase package family.
The component_package_layout target audits libmd package membership.
Its component scope is rejected by complete-package manifest generation;
full runtime/tests family aggregation and external dependencies remain required.
Private BSD tar extraction preserves the declared payloads and links,
and the extracted ATF suite passes against the candidate shared library.
Fresh-root cold, disk-cache-restored and independent component archives
have been checked for identical bytes and intact extraction.
The restored archive also passes the fourteen ATF tests.
This does not qualify privileged package installation or system performance.
These checks do not establish installation, cross-target or performance parity.
Other architectures and final link/runtime/package qualification remain incomplete.
The six maintained bzip2 sample comparisons are Bazel test targets.
The versioned libbz2 shared target uses standard Bazel shared-library providers;
bzip2 and its sample tests select that artifact. Split-debug and stripped payload
targets are available for all three components; installation and package metadata
remain unfinished. Default ELF hardening and compilation modes are supplied by the
selected SDK toolchain, whose receipt must be refreshed when that policy changes.

.Pp
The target //usr.bin/bzip2recover:install_manifest declares the program and debug
payload destinations, ownership, modes and package tags from maintained BUILD
inputs.
The target //usr.bin/bzip2:install_manifest additionally declares the compressed
manual and program/manual hardlinks.
Building an installation manifest also builds its declared payloads; the manifest
output group selects metadata alone and the payload output group selects payloads.
The target //lib/libbz2:install_manifest declares the archive, shared library,
separate debug payload, header, development symlink and generated pkg-config file.
The pkg-config version comes from the declared upstream header.
These targets do not install files or create a package.
.Pp
Physical staging is not qualified for cache-safe package construction.
The current directory-artifact staging rule loses hardlink identity when restored
from Bazel's disk cache into a fresh output base.
A successful initial staging run does not establish package correctness.
The final package action must preserve the declared link and ownership metadata
in its serialized outputs and qualify cache restoration separately.
.Pp
A serialized installation archive candidate is available through
//tools/build/bazel/tests/install_archive:archive for mechanism qualification.
Its fixture covers hardlinks, symlinks, modes and explicit account identifiers
across disk-cache restoration and extraction.
It is not a pkgbase package and does not replace the existing staging consumers.
.Pp
The target //packages/bzip2:install_archive aggregates the maintained bzip2
component manifests with source mtree directory policy and source account files.
It declares the mtree execution tool and selects required directory ancestors.
This target produces an installation tar archive, not pkgbase subpackages.
.Pp
The target //packages/bzip2:package_layout partitions component payloads into
explicit base, debug, development and library packages, with an optional manual
package controlled by split_manpages.
It exposes declared dependency edges and validates package ownership boundaries.
It produces package-layout metadata, not installable pkg archives.
.Pp
The target //packages/bzip2:package_manifests generates UCL package manifests
using the declared layout and maintained UCL processor.
It requires a nonempty //tools/build/bazel/config:package_version value in
build-settings; missing versions are rejected during analysis.
Manifest generation does not create installable package archives.
.Pp
The candidate //packages/bzip2:packages creates native pkg archives from the
installation archive and declared manifests, using a target ELF for ABI discovery
and the target source header for OS version.
Physical staging remains inside the package action; serialized pkg files are the
cached outputs.
This target requires the package version setting and declared pkg/tar SDK tools.
It does not publish a repository or install packages on the host.
.Pp
The package_threads integer build setting controls compression concurrency;
zero selects pkg's automatic default and positive values set a thread limit.
Compilation parallelism is unaffected.
The package action emits index.json with exact package filenames and hashes.
Qualification consumers must use this index rather than scan persistent output
directories, which may retain files from other configurations.
Use frozen indexed snapshots for separate root-shell installation tests.
.Pp
The separate private-package-install-test.py helper requires an OS root shell
and confines package add/check/delete qualification to a fresh /usr/obj root.
It rejects package scripts and disables plugins and script execution.
A prepared helper or successful build does not establish installation qualification;
inspect its completed receipt separately.
Package cache, solver, installation and performance qualification remain separate.
.Pp
The package-cache-test.py qualification helper checks cold execution, disk-cache
restoration into a fresh output base, and independent rebuild byte identity using
the recorded frontend command inputs.
A restored package target may not materialize intermediate archive outputs.
The bzip2 cache check does not establish solver, installation, cross-host or
performance qualification.
.Pp
The native Bazel suite //tools/build/bazel/tests/pkg_layout:tests checks package
provider contents and rejects invalid ownership or dependency declarations.
Format 5 accepts either this suite label or individual test labels in targets
and tests; result accounting uses the expanded configured test inventory.
.Pp
The native //etc:account_databases target declares both master.passwd and shells
as inputs and passes the latter through pwd_mkdb's explicit -S option.
Ordinary pwd_mkdb invocations retain system-shell-list validation.
Generated database artifacts require their installation archive to restore
private-file modes; they are not an account-upgrade replacement policy.
.Pp
Native ATF targets support shell bodies and compiled test programs.
Each case is a separate Bazel test target with declared runtime libraries and
configuration files; compiled programs execute directly without a shell wrapper.
Only a successful process exit and an ATF passed result satisfy this adapter.
Generated Kyuafiles preserve explicitly declared per-program metadata as typed
properties, including required users and programs for TAP suites.
Installing a TAP suite does not establish Bazel execution of that suite.
Inherited locale variables are cleared and TZ is set to UTC, matching Kyua's
baseline isolation while allowing tests to select individual locale categories.
Locale files are explicit test inputs, mapped by locale name and category;
PATH_LOCALE points to these runfiles rather than the host locale installation.
Explicitly declared cleanup phases run after the body, including body failures;
a cleanup failure also fails the case.
When a body exits unsuccessfully, its ATF result text is included in the test
log when available; a passing result cannot override a failed process exit.
This does not provide complete Kyua metadata or timeout semantics, and external
termination may prevent cleanup.

Native SSH library targets accept typed build settings for ldns,
kerberos_support, mitkrb5, tcp_wrappers, usb and dtrace.
The native sshd-session target also accepts audit and blocklist_support
(default true), corresponding to MK_AUDIT and MK_BLOCKLIST_SUPPORT.
DTrace-enabled session builds generate provider headers and rewrite program
objects using the declared native DTrace tool and provider inputs.
The ssh_xauth_path setting defaults to /usr/local/bin/xauth;
ssh_askpass_path is empty by default, retaining the vendor's askpass default.
Nonempty helper paths must be absolute.
These settings generate declared compiler inputs; they do not change host SSH
configuration or establish client/server runtime qualification.
.Pp
.Sh NATIVE COMPONENT QUALIFICATION
Input format 4 to
.Cm bazel-test
.Fl bazel-inputs
qualifies selected native builds and tests without staging or packaging.
Required file fields are plan, toolchain-receipt, module-lock and profile.
Required directory fields are toolchain-workspace, java-home and repository-cache.
The distdirs field is a nonempty array of absolute paths.
An optional test-config file adds explicit test fixtures; configured ATF programs
are registered from the plan even when that field is absent.
At least one test must be present and the complete emitted test inventory must pass.
This mode supports components containing multiple configured programs.
Receipts report build/test scope only; they do not establish installation,
package, publication, bootstrap or complete-system qualification.
.Pp
Input format 3 to
.Cm bazel-test
.Fl bazel-inputs
qualifies selected component builds, native tests, physical staging and archive
creation from prepared inputs.
The maintained component-plan.py helper prepares configuration plans.
Its --jobs option bounds concurrent ordinary-source configuration queries and
defaults to the host logical CPU count; --jobs 1 exports serially.
This preparation setting is independent of the build-bsd compilation job limit.
It does not change the selected component coverage or qualify a native build.
Input formats 3 and 4 optionally accept additional-plans, a nonempty list of
absolute component-plan paths. The primary plan and additional plans retain
their original hashes and source bindings. All must use the same build
environment, profile, compiler identity and execution-tool selections.
Conflicting input hashes, duplicate component/program records, duplicate plan
paths and stale inputs are errors. This supports incremental graph expansion
without re-exporting unchanged components; it does not establish coverage of
unselected world or kernel components.
Input formats 3 and 4 optionally accept execution-log-format as json (the
compatibility default) or compact. Both retain the complete Bazel execution log;
the receipt binds its format, pathname, size and SHA256. Compact logs require
Bazel's upstream execution-log converter for JSON-based action audits. Conversion
can stream to standard output without materializing a large expanded JSON file.
This setting does not change test selection or replace runtime/case checks.
Configured syscall assembly generation uses the same maintained script and
per-output metadata as Make, with declared execution tools and output files.
Generated-source parity is a separate check from compilation and runtime tests.
Per-source TEXTUAL_SRCS metadata declares included C or assembly source fragments
as private compiler inputs.
A fragment is compiled separately only if it is also explicitly configured as a
translation unit; both uses participate in dependency and cache qualification.
Headers explicitly listed in SRCS remain declared inputs without producing objects.
Configuration groups use the effective bsd.confs.mk per-file destination, owner,
group, mode and package/config tags. The Make reference runs installconfig when
configuration groups contain payloads. Native configuration bytes must match the
independent Make reference in addition to the native manifest and METALOG.
Generated configuration is declared with CONF_GENERATED and per-output
CONF_GENERATOR, CONF_TEMPLATE, CONF_TOOLS, CONF_ARGS and optional
CONF_DEPENDENCIES fields shared with maintained Make recipes. An undeclared
object-directory file is not accepted as a source payload. Declared generated
outputs take precedence over stale object files. Template outputs are
namespaced by component in the native graph.
Native language catalogues follow bsd.nls.mk's configured NLS inventory and
NLSSRCFILES/NLSSRCDIR prebuild inputs. GENCAT is an explicit execution-tool
selection and must match the staged SDK tool. Ordered source concatenation and
catalogue generation are separate native actions with declared inputs.
File groups use effective bsd.files.mk per-file installation metadata, including
package tags and indirect destination variables. Generated payloads require a
declared generator; a preexisting object-directory file is not a source fallback.
Native file-group bytes must match the independent Make installation reference.
Library link selection follows the configured output inventory; an internal
library may retain a default shared-library name while building only archives.
Test framework library aliases and interfaces are exported from maintained Make
metadata; framework libraries are explicit native dependencies.
Dependency artifact and runtime selection preserve the declared source-component
identity rather than choosing a library by its basename alone.
Distinct source libraries claiming the same runtime SONAME are rejected.
Explicit name-based runtime selections must identify one selected component.
GROUP catalogues and member dependency lookups use source-component identities.
An unrelated duplicate name cannot replace an explicitly selected dependency.
Targets are emitted in source-component packages under
//components/<source-directory>:<target>.
Configured target basenames are preserved; duplicate identities within one
component are rejected. Dependency and runtime artifact references are qualified
by their source component. Installation manifests live with their components,
and the root native stage aggregates their qualified labels.
Program plain-library sequences preserve the final Make LDADD order and repeated
arguments through native linking contexts. Compound aliases require a declared
primary archive identity and transitive source mappings. Linker control groups
and compound searched aliases still require further support and are rejected.
Library search paths in program and shared-library links are translated only
when dependencies resolve to declared native inputs and the path matches their
recorded artifact directory or exported .OBJDIR. Unknown paths and indirect
search flags are rejected; source-tree object directories are not guessed. Configured PIC and PIE archive selections must match both the output
inventory and the recorded dependency artifact; unavailable variants remain errors.
Configured library lexer/parser generation uses grammar and output metadata from
maintained Make rules and explicitly staged lex, yacc and m4 tools.
Named parser outputs and their declared headers are supported. Traditional y.tab
mode, unmodeled auxiliary outputs and generated-program integration remain errors.
Configured library RPC generation uses schemas, modes, dependencies and tool
identities exported from maintained Make declarations. Generated headers are
declared compiler inputs. Unsupported RPC invocations, tool mismatches and output
collisions are errors; RPC programs require further generated-header integration.
Textual C and assembly inclusions are declared through maintained Make
TEXTUAL_SRCS metadata and become explicit per-source native inputs.
They do not become additional archive members.
The component emitter binds source labels to explicit metadata keys for compiler
flags, source order, archive variants and private textual inputs.
These keys remain independent of Bazel package-relative file paths.
Supplied mappings must identify every declared file exactly once; partial,
duplicate, extra and multi-file mappings are rejected.
Shared-only components with SHLIB_NAME and no LIB or PROG use their declared
SOBJS inventory and PIC compile contexts, linking the objects directly into the
shared output. Output names, SONAME and link driver come from maintained Make
metadata. Their installation and test-suite fixture coverage remain separate
qualification gates.
ATF suite module selection matches maintained SHLIBDIR to the test BINDIR.
Declared module artifacts and library dependencies are staged explicitly;
module test variants use a transitive, suite-relative ELF RPATH that is absent
from the installable variant. This keeps indirect dependencies in the declared
suite closure; strict loader artifact checks still apply. Destination collisions are errors.
Configured programs use native per-source compiler actions with effective Make
ordinary or PIE contexts, including source-specific C and C++ options.
Their declared textual fragments remain private to the corresponding source.
The resulting objects are linked directly in configured OBJS order; they are not
wrapped in an intermediate archive. Normal and test executables reuse those
objects. Test executables additionally search their declared sibling SONAME
symlinks using a test-only origin-relative runtime path. Native ATF suites stage
a real executable copy using the declared Python tool, so FreeBSD resolves the
loader origin to the suite directory. Installed executables
do not receive that test path. PROG_CXX selects the C++ link driver and CXXFLAGS;
other programs use the
C driver and CFLAGS, followed by configured LDFLAGS and declared dependencies.
Missing, duplicate or unsupported object contexts are rejected.
Extra per-source include roots are private compilation inputs; they are not
exported as headers for every library source or dependent consumer.
Library public compilation contexts are shared across their source actions.

Declared source aliases use origins shared with maintained Make recipes.
The native graph preserves alias names for compiler context and declares alias
outputs explicitly; origins outside the selected source tree are rejected.
Extra variant-only sources require explicit mappings to configured objects and
origins matching Make source lookup. Archive member coverage and order follow
the configured object lists; missing, extra or duplicate members are errors.
DTrace rewriting consumes ordinary variant objects; regular-only static objects
are appended afterward and remain absent from PIC archives.
Composition fixtures cover disabled probes and linked DOF sections.
The source-owned Casper candidate uses rules_cc compilation, private DTrace
object copies and separate regular/PIC inputs to cc_import.
Its shared-library link and PIC archive inventory have been compared with Make;
static consumption, runtime and active tracing are not yet qualified.
The cap_fileargs and digest candidates also build with capability support enabled.
Host digest runtime qualification currently fails at a Capsicum assertion for
both Make and Bazel artifacts with the selected SDK runtime; a matching-kernel
runtime check remains required for the full suite.
Subsequent matching-kernel QEMU smoke checks pass for both binaries, exercising
string hashing, Casper file access and command-name dispatch.
This does not qualify the complete digest suite or active DTrace.
The native digest installation manifest also matches the 49 default non-test
Make entries, including command/manual hardlinks and separate debug output.
This is component inventory parity, not a complete runtime pkgbase package.
Native static DTrace consumers retain provider-only imports with alwayslink.
The static digest passes bounded matching-kernel VM checks; live tracing and
installed static-library consumption remain unqualified.
Active tracing and reproducible DTrace output across sandbox paths remain
qualification requirements.
The component backend requires relative DTrace object identities and a supporting
execution tool. Older DTrace tools fail explicitly; there is no absolute-path
fallback. Complete component integration remains subject to qualification.
Component plan generation accepts an explicit format-1 tool-override file with
Make execution-tool names and absolute executable paths.
The configuration and executable hashes are bound into the plan; SDK selection
must match. Command fragments and unsupported Make variables are rejected.
Simple mixed linker GROUP members resolve against selected configured artifacts.
Native scripts preserve member order and archive group semantics; installed
scripts retain configured installation paths.
Runtime identity checks also traverse dependencies reached only through groups.
Generic native consumers and configured ATF tests share this traversal.
Installed linker-script aliases follow SHLIB_LDSCRIPT_LINKS and the maintained
INSTALL_LIBSYMLINK command, library ownership/mode and library package tags.
Duplicate, self-referencing or unsupported alias commands are rejected.
Native GROUP scripts are interface libraries with declared shared runtime members;
Bazel supplies test runtime search paths through its library metadata.


Library source membership follows the exported per-source compile contexts for
each archive variant. Explicit native variant inventories must cover every source;
missing, empty, repeated or unknown selections are errors.
Configured NOSSP PIC archives use distinct outputs and effective per-source flags
exported from the same Make definitions used by its suffix rules.
Missing NOSSP contexts and mismatched archive dependencies are errors.
Configured ATF programs get separate test-only runtime executables and Kyua runners.
The runners verify declared native library identity before executing test cases.
Configured runners preserve exported suite names, literal Kyua metadata and
literal test environment assignments. The clean runner computes PWD from its
physical working directory rather than inheriting the caller's PWD.
Exclusive programs also carry the Bazel exclusive tag to coordinate scheduling
across runners.
Default installed-library search paths are replaced by declared native artifacts;
custom library search paths, other loader overrides and custom Kyuafiles remain
unsupported and are rejected. Arbitrary Lua and shell expressions are not evaluated.
Kyua case timeouts and Bazel outer test deadlines remain distinct.
Runtime qualification currently covers the msun suite; policy translation fixtures
do not establish coverage for other base test suites.
Ordinary installable programs retain their configured runtime search paths.
The staged Clang toolchain uses a stable relative debug compilation directory to
avoid transient sandbox paths in DWARF.
Debugger source mapping and cross-workspace reproducibility require separate
qualification.
.Pp
Required file fields are plan, toolchain-receipt, module-lock, test-config,
directory-policy, compression-tools, install-tools, profile, pkg and flua.
Required directory fields are toolchain-workspace, java-home and repository-cache.
The component-policies and distdirs fields are nonempty arrays of absolute paths.
The package-component field is relative to the source root;
abi-file is relative to the staged filesystem root.
Every selected component must have exactly one reconciled installation policy.
The emitted native test inventory must match successful Bazel test results.
For native C/C++ tests configured with dynamic linkage, qualification additionally
checks that declared library SONAMEs resolve to the emitted native artifacts,
then executes the consumer with its configured arguments and without loader
environment overrides. Test-only build-tree runtime paths are not added to
installed library artifacts.
Native ATF BUILD targets may map declared fixture targets into relative suite
paths through data_paths. Absolute paths, empty or dot components, traversal,
duplicate destinations and file/directory collisions are rejected. Mapped files
and their runfiles remain declared dependencies. This permits tests to use a
native executable at their expected fixture path without a host lookup wrapper.
Native ATF targets may declare body_timeout_seconds and cleanup_timeout_seconds
as nonnegative integer phase deadlines. A zero deadline leaves that phase under
Bazel's outer timeout only. Cleanup defaults to 300 seconds; body deadlines must
be migrated explicitly from maintained ATF metadata. A timed-out phase fails the
test and its process group is terminated; body timeout still runs declared
cleanup with a separate deadline. Bazel's overall timeout must allow both phases
and startup overhead. Outer cancellation and deliberately detached descendants
are not covered by this process-group mechanism.
Native Kyua BUILD targets may also map declared executable labels to program
names with path_programs. These names precede the fixed /bin:/usr/bin ancillary
tool search path. Duplicate names, nonexecutables, and conflicting explicit PATH
settings are rejected; the runner checks candidate executability before Kyua.
Candidate runfiles are included. Host shell, ancillary tools, and system loader
libraries may still be used; this facility does not establish hermetic execution.
Native Kyua BUILD targets may declare fixture files under normalized relative
subdirectories. The adapter rejects absolute paths, traversal, duplicate
destinations and file/directory collisions. Fixture paths remain declared build
inputs; staging does not discover files from the host. Other system loader dependencies may remain
host supplied; this check does not establish a hermetic runtime environment.
Dynamic TAP/ATF runner qualification remains unsupported.
Native C tests may also select linkage static-in-shared to qualify static-only
direct dependencies inside a shared fixture.
The test source supplies int BSD_TEST_ENTRY(void); arguments are unsupported.
The emitted consumer calls that entry point and requires declared-library loader
verification.
This fixture does not count as another converted base-system component.
Configured SYMLINKS are derived from maintained Make installation metadata,
including literal targets, ownership, modes and package tags.
They must reconcile with the independent Make stage before native staging.
Multi-program export uses the effective PROGS inventory and preserves the
PROG_CXX selector for C++ recursive Make configuration queries.
It does not add PROGS_CXX to PROGS a second time.
Manual-page hardlink chains must terminate at a declared regular payload in the
same component manifest and have consistent inode ownership, mode and flags.
Cycles, absent targets and links through symlinks are rejected.
Native staging resolves chains independently of alias pathname order while
preserving declared link relationships in manifests.
Custom link command semantics and unsupported link classes are rejected.
PIC archive installation follows the configured INSTALL_PIC_ARCHIVE definition
state and Make output inventory, independently of MK_INSTALLLIB.
Supported shared-library file flags are preserved in installation metadata.
Unprivileged staging records these flags in METALOG without applying them to
private build-tree files.
Stage verification precedes the maintained Make package creation rules.
Output contains command logs, test events, stage parity, private package archives
and a receipt describing the verified scope.
This format does not publish, install, rebuild the SDK or select Bazel as the
production backend.
Package-manager installation, dependency solving and full-system qualification
remain separate requirements.

.Pp
Format-3 packaging also requires archive inspection before success is recorded.
It verifies archive ABI/version, complete member and payload coverage, extracted
bytes, file and directory modes, symlinks and hardlinks against the native stage.
The package manifest configuration-file inventory must exactly match staged
config tags; missing, extra, duplicate and malformed entries are rejected.
This checks configuration registration, not package upgrade or merge behavior.
Extraction uses a private output directory and does not install into a package
database or execute package scripts.
Archive owner/group names, header modes, types and link targets are verified
through the system libarchive interface before extraction.
File flags are compared through libarchive as semantic flag bits.
Private inspection extraction does not apply file flags.
Extracted ownership belongs to the invoking user; numeric ID mapping and
dependency-solver installation tests remain separate checks.

.Pp
The generic component test-config also supports suite native-c, format 1.
Each tests-array entry contains name, source, dependencies and cflags.
Sources are relative to the source tree; dependencies name selected libraries.
These additional consumer checks use native fully static cc_test targets.
Header inputs must already belong to the selected graph.
This adapter does not replace the component's own maintained tests.

.Pp
Native qualification retains JSON execution records in execution order, with
Bazel execution-log sorting disabled to avoid its end-of-build memory cost.
Log order is not a deterministic artifact comparison contract.
Expanded JSON can still consume substantial disk space; these diagnostics do
not establish build performance parity with Make.

.Pp
Format-3 and format-4 component qualification inputs may include
program-runtime-libraries, an ordered array of unique selected library names.
These libraries become explicit native dependencies of configured programs and
are included in configured ATF runtime identity checks, including dependencies
reachable through linker GROUP members.
For example, selecting c makes libc tests use the selected native libc rather
than relying on the prepared SDK's implicit C runtime.
The selection does not replace SDK startup objects or alter library builds.
Omitting the field retains the prepared SDK runtime behavior.
Explicit program runtimes currently require ordinary dynamic linkage;
static and suppressed-default-library link modes are rejected.
The component graph exporter accepts repeated --program-runtime-library options
for the same contract. No extra frontend command-line option is required.

.Pp
Configured ATF qualification also requires test-result-sqlite, the absolute path
to a SQLite command-line executable supporting read-only JSON queries.
This executable is included in the qualification input hashes.
Runtime-check results and command logs are stored beneath
runtime-checks/<test-identity>/ in the qualification output.
Nested test and artifact identities must be normalized workspace-relative paths.
ATF verification compares the suite's program basename separately from the
emitted binary artifact path and rejects duplicate runtime identities.
After Bazel and runtime checks succeed, the backend reconciles each ATF case
listing with its Kyua database, verifies program and native artifact identities,
and writes atf-results-receipt.json.
Missing, duplicate, failed, broken and unknown case results reject qualification.
Skipped and expected-failure cases remain distinct recorded outcomes;
all_cases_passed is false when either occurs.
The main receipt includes the case summary and the detailed receipt hash.

SQLite evidence inspection disables user startup commands with an explicit
empty initialization file and opens the Kyua databases read-only.

.Pp
The component exporter also accepts explicit LIBMD_GEN_SH, LIBMD_GEN_SED,
LIBMD_GEN_MV and LIBMD_GEN_RM executable selections in its tool-overrides file.
Digest helper generation uses the script, template, output arguments and header
mappings declared by the maintained Makefile; the selected execution tools must
match the staged SDK. A Makefile change requires regenerating affected plans and
SDK receipts before qualification.
.Pp
The native ncurses test
.Li //lib/ncurses/tests:api
exercises the native curses, tinfo, panel, menu and form shared libraries.
It initializes a screen using an explicit test terminal description and temporary
streams, then checks public APIs and object lifetimes.
This does not establish interactive terminal rendering or installed-system
compatibility.
The ncurses family installation archive supports the configured
.Li split_manpages
setting, corresponding to Make's
.Li WITH_MANSPLITPKG
option.
.Pp
The native candidate target
.Li //packages/ncurses:packages
aggregates the ncurses libraries, utilities and terminal data into configured
pkgbase subpackages using the maintained package template.
Building these candidate archives neither publishes a repository nor installs
an upgrade, and does not qualify the complete system packaging backend.

.Ss Secureboot header qualification
The native
.Li //lib/libsecureboot/tests:headers_test
target qualifies explicit trust-header generation with an empty-trust fixture.
It does not configure the installed system or establish secure-boot enforcement.
The native static libsecureboot target now requires an explicitly selected
.Li //tools/build/bazel/config:secureboot_policy
label pointing to a format-1 policy file.
The
.Li secureboot_trust_anchors
and
.Li secureboot_verification_certificates
labels in that configuration package select public certificate files or groups.
No usable policy or test root is selected by default.
Static/shared trust initialization and library installation metadata have been
qualified with explicitly selected test inputs.
System-wide signing, key rotation and enforcement remain incomplete.
.Pp
.Ss Version branch
The format-5 string setting
.Li //tools/build/bazel/config:version_branch
provides an explicit branch override for the native
.Li //bin/freebsd-version:version_script
target.
An empty value retains the maintained source default.
Use a version token containing only letters, digits, dot, underscore, plus or
hyphen.
The script and installation metadata have been compared with Make.
This selects userland and native kernel version-source generation; complete
kernel/image version consistency remains unqualified.
.Pp
.Ss Signed-file qualification
The native
.Li //lib/libsecureboot/tests:signed_rotation_test
target uses disposable in-memory RSA keys to exercise signed-file verification,
trust overlap, revocation precedence and malformed or missing metadata.
Test files stay beneath the Bazel test temporary directory.
This qualifies in-process verifier behavior, not firmware key rotation or system
image upgrade policy.
.Pp
.Ss Secureboot self-test qualification
The native
.Li //lib/libsecureboot/tests:selftest_clean ,
.Li selftest_bad256 ,
and
.Li selftest_bad384
targets exercise hash self-test result propagation with explicitly selected
trust and verification certificates.
The failure cases corrupt private expected hashes in test translation units
and require cached failure; production code has no injection hook.
These tests do not qualify firmware or system-wide enforcement.
.Pp
.Ss Native veriexec qualification
The native static
.Li //sbin/veriexec:veriexec
target uses the explicitly configured libsecureboot trust inputs.
.Li //sbin/veriexec/tests:tests
runs four maintained parser cases with the test's ioctl recorder.
.Li //sbin/veriexec:install_archive
packages the program and, when enabled, its tests.
Binary and installation metadata have been compared with Make.
These checks do not load a manifest or qualify kernel enforcement.
.Pp
.Ss Native BearSSL selection
The format-5 boolean build setting
.Li //tools/build/bazel/config:bearssl_ssl
selects the optional SSL source group.
It defaults to false, matching the library's opt-in
.Va MK_BEARSSL_SSL
setting.
Both default and SSL-enabled static/shared libraries and installation metadata
have been compared with Make.
The native
.Li //lib/libbearssl:crypto_test
target runs the unchanged upstream crypto suite with
.Li all
and requires
.Li bearssl_ssl=true .
The suite passes on the qualified amd64 host; Power8 cases are unavailable there.
TLS network behavior and other architectures remain unqualified.
These component builds do not qualify secure boot or veriexec enforcement.
.Pp
.Ss RPC binder build selection
The boolean //tools/build/bazel/config:rpcbind_warmstart_support defaults to
false, matching the maintained source default.
Set it through format-5 build-settings to compile rpcbind warm-start support.
The existing inet6_support and tcp_wrappers settings select IPv6 and TCP-wrapper
support and dependencies.
These compile-time selections do not start or configure the running RPC service.

.Ss Native libmagic database directory
The format-5 build setting
.Dq //tools/build/bazel/config:magic_path
selects the libmagic database directory, corresponding to Make's
.Va MAGICPATH .
Its default is
.Pa /usr/share/misc .
The native library, generated manual pages and database installation records
share this input.
Use a normalized absolute directory without control or quoting characters.
Default installation metadata has been compared with Make.
An alternate directory beneath the maintained
.Pa /usr/share
parent and split-manual packaging have also been checked.
The native file utility passes all 83 current upstream classification fixtures.
Arbitrary parent-directory policy, full runtime and system qualification remain
incomplete; selecting this setting does not establish a usable installed system.

.Ss Declared comparison-test environment
Native
.Va bsd_compare_test
rules may declare an
.Va environment
dictionary for inputs such as the timezone.
The default dictionary is empty.
Runner control variables and its locale/library-path settings cannot be
overridden through this attribute.
Calendar's native fixture suites declare UTC and cover 31 cases in both iconv
configurations; this does not qualify installed service behavior.

.Ss Comparison-test working files
Native
.Va bsd_compare_test
rules may declare
.Va working_files
as a mapping from single-file targets to normalized relative paths.
Subdirectories are supported; traversal, duplicate paths and file/directory
prefix collisions are rejected.
When present, the runner executes the tested program in a directory containing
those declared runfiles, allowing relative profile references without reading
from the host source checkout.
Captured output continues to use the test temporary directory.
The default mapping is empty and preserves the previous working directory.

.Ss Native device-tree generation
Native
.Va bsd_dtb
rules declare preprocessing and device-tree compilation as separate actions.
The preprocessor and native device-tree compiler use the execution configuration.
Source files, textual dependencies, appended sources, include directories,
compiler options and runtime libraries are explicit rule inputs.
Preprocessing disables implicit system include paths.
These targets and their consumer tests can be selected through existing
format-5 targets and tests.
Two board descriptions have been compared with the maintained Make workflow;
all current ARM overlay sources also pass generation and consumer comparisons.
This does not qualify complete board selection, installation, overlay application
or boot.

.Pp
The native dhclient parser test suite is
.Dq //sbin/dhclient/tests:tests .
Select it in both format-5 targets and tests to run the maintained plain test.
The target
.Dq //sbin/dhclient:install_archive
includes installed tests when the tests build setting is enabled.
Its installed PCP test requires root, VNET/netgraph, and an ISC DHCP server;
it is not executed by the host-safe parser suite.

.Pp
The native standalone Dummynet targets are
.Dq //sbin/dnctl:dnctl ,
.Dq //sbin/dnctl:install_archive ,
and
.Dq //sbin/dnctl/tests:tests .
The suite contains a flow-layout ABI test and three maintained dry-run command
tests. Full runtime qualification requires a kernel compatible with the built
userspace Capsicum ABI; successful artifact comparisons alone do not establish
that the shell tests ran.

.Pp
The native suite
.Dq //sbin/ping/tests:tests
runs the four maintained checksum cases without sending packets.
The target
.Dq //sbin/ping:install_archive
also packages the shell and pytest network tests when tests are enabled.
Those installed network tests are not executed by the checksum suite.

.Pp
Native init uses a declared static seed payload and toolchain assembly action.
Staged SDKs for raw assembly must include assembler tool inputs; regenerate
older snapshots with the maintained staging helper rather than modifying their
recorded files. The native init archive preserves its schg flag and ttys
configuration. Building this target does not execute init or qualify boot and
root-transition behavior.

.Pp
The native pfctl target
.Dq //sbin/pfctl:install_archive
packages the regression helper, shell tests and corpus when tests are enabled.
The Kyuafile retains the regression suite's jail and VNET requirements.
Successful archive qualification does not mean those privileged tests ran.

.Pp
The native target
.Dq //bin/zsh:tables
qualifies target signal and error table generation through declared compiler
and generator inputs. It does not build or install the shell and is not a
substitute for world or shell runtime qualification.

.Pp
The native targets
.Dq //bin/zsh:zsh
and
.Dq //bin/zsh:runtime_smoke
build the amd64 shell and exercise noninteractive behavior with declared
candidate libraries. Full shell installation, upstream interactive tests,
static-root configuration and cross builds require separate qualification.

.Pp
The target
.Dq //bin/zsh:install_archive
stages the complete shell component, including functions, manuals, configuration
and split debug information. Its installation inventory is qualified separately
from full upstream shell tests, system images and repository publication.

.Pp
The target
.Dq //bin/zsh:upstream_tests
runs the upstream suite as a local, noncached integration test. It uses host
commands, locales, account lookups and pseudo-terminals in addition to the
declared candidate and fixtures. Review upstream skips in the retained log;
a successful group does not imply every platform-dependent case executed.

.Pp
The typed setting
.Dq //tools/build/bazel/config:dynamic_root
currently controls zsh only. False selects its static, non-PIE build, matching
WITHOUT_DYNAMICROOT for that component. Other root-filesystem targets have not
yet adopted this setting; it does not qualify a complete static-root system.

.Pp
The native target
.Dq //bin/rmail:install_archive
uses the internal libsm PIE archive and preserves rmail's dynamic-link exception.
.Dq //lib/libsm:tests
runs selected unchanged upstream library tests. These checks do not qualify
mail handoff or delivery and do not invoke the host sendmail service.

.Pp
The targets
.Dq //bin/csh:generated ,
.Dq //bin/csh:csh
and
.Dq //bin/csh:runtime_smoke
qualify native generation, compilation and basic runtime behavior separately
from installation and interactive shell tests. csh also consumes dynamic_root
and nls_catalogs settings; its alternative configurations require separate
qualification. dynamic_root still does not control every root-filesystem target.

.Pp
.Dq //bin/csh:install_archive
stages csh/tcsh aliases, configuration, examples, manuals and native catalogs.
The typed examples setting currently controls csh examples only. Catalog
installation follows nls, while compiled lookup follows nls_catalogs; iconv
also affects catalog aliases and the maintained stub implementation.

.Pp
Static, NLS-disabled csh component parity has been checked independently of
full system qualification. Component BUILD-file presence does not establish
complete test catalogs, aggregate pkgbase installation or runtime coverage.

.Pp
.Dq //bin/cat/tests:tests
runs the maintained cat ATF cases; its scripts and fixtures are included by
.Dq //bin/cat:install_archive
when tests are enabled. Installation parity is separate from runtime success.
The current older-host/SDK Capsicum helper assertion prevents those cases from
reaching their bodies and remains a failed qualification gate.

.Pp
.Dq //bin/echo/tests:tests
selects the native echo executable through an ATF configuration input;
installed tests retain /bin/echo as their default. The test scripts and
Kyuafile are included by
.Dq //bin/echo:install_archive
when tests are enabled. Installation parity has been checked; the current
host/SDK Capsicum incompatibility prevents runtime qualification.

.Pp
.Dq //usr.bin/bsdcat:install_archive
includes its program, manual and debug data, plus the maintained ATF wrapper,
upstream harness, fixtures and Kyuafile when tests are enabled.
.Dq //usr.bin/bsdcat/tests:functional_test
runs the upstream cases against the declared candidate with its original
executable name. This local, noncacheable integration suite uses host shell
utilities; passing it does not qualify a complete native system runtime.

.Pp
.Dq //usr.bin/stdbuf:install_archive
and
.Dq //lib/libstdbuf:install_archive
stage the command and preload library independently.
.Dq //lib/libstdbuf:runtime_test
checks candidate library buffering through explicit preload runfiles.
It does not substitute for testing the installed command's absolute library
path in an isolated system runtime.

.Pp
The native installation targets
.Dq //usr.bin/xstr:install_archive ,
.Dq //usr.bin/revoke:install_archive
and
.Dq //usr.bin/tcopy:install_archive
preserve their maintained packages, manuals and debug files.
Artifact parity and private-file runtime comparisons do not qualify live
device revocation or physical tape operations.

.Pp
.Dq //usr.sbin/config:config
builds the kernel configuration tool with declared native lexer, parser and
embedded-template generators.
.Dq //usr.sbin/config:install_archive
includes the executable, debug file and both manuals.
GENERIC configuration output parity and warm cache reuse have been checked;
this does not establish a native Bazel kernel or module build.

.Pp
.Dq //usr.bin/xinstall:install_archive
stages install, its manual/debug data and the maintained ATF suite when tests
are enabled. The normal executable consumes the dtrace setting.
.Dq //usr.bin/xinstall:bootstrap_install
selects the maintained bootstrap code path without probes or copy_file_range.
This component target has not replaced the frontend's staged bootstrap tools;
its existence does not establish a self-hosted native build.

.Pp
.Dq //usr.sbin/nmtree:install_archive
stages native mtree/nmtree, manuals, debug data and maintained test fixtures.
.Dq //usr.sbin/nmtree/tests:stream_tests
runs twelve imported conversion/merge checks as native byte comparisons.
This candidate has not replaced the staged SDK tool. The maintained mtree
reports missing/extra entries on stdout while returning zero in the observed
cases; tree-verification callers must not rely solely on its exit status.

.Pp
Installation archive rules can declare mtree runtime libraries for the
execution platform. Their loader path is applied only to the mtree policy
subprocess. The xinstall archive now uses native mtree with explicit libraries;
its output matches the previous staged-tool archive. Other SDK consumers and
complete native bootstrap remain separate qualification work.
.Pp
.Dq //usr.bin/spdxtool:bootstrap_spdxtool
selects the native static, non-PIE tool corresponding to the Make
.Dv TOOLS_PREFIX
build mode.
It links native libpkgconf and libc and can be selected in format-5 targets.
Allocator policy remains an explicit build setting; static comparison must use
matching libc configurations.
This target does not automatically replace staged execution tools or establish
cross-host bootstrap compatibility.

.Pp
.Dq //lib/libkldelf:install_archive
and
.Dq //usr.sbin/kldxref:install_archive
stage the private ELF module reader and module-hints utility.
.Dq //usr.sbin/kldxref:bootstrap_kldxref
uses the native static library with linker-set registrations retained.
These candidates have amd64 copied-module comparison evidence; image/publication
helpers and full cross-architecture kernel packaging remain separate work.

.Pp
The six
.Dq //tools/test/construction:{native,bootstrap}_{pnp,module_inputs,kldxref}_test
labels select the maintained module-metadata audits with declared synthetic ELF
fixtures and native runtime libraries. Specify each desired label in both the
format-5 targets and tests lists. These tests run locally and are not cached:
the host shell and SDK Python loader remain execution dependencies. They never
load their synthetic fixtures into the kernel. This does not qualify a complete
kernel/module build or cross-architecture relocation behavior.

.Pp
Native kernel-interface generation candidates are
.Dq //sys/kern:bus_if ,
.Dq //sys/kern:clock_if ,
.Dq //sys/kern:cpufreq_if ,
.Dq //sys/kern:device_if ,
.Dq //sys/kern:linker_if ,
.Dq //sys/kern:msi_if ,
.Dq //sys/kern:pic_if ,
and
.Dq //sys/kern:serdev_if .
Each emits the maintained interface C source and header using declared native
generation tools. These targets do not compile a kernel or module. Complete
native kernel configuration, compilation, linking and packaging remain
separate qualification work.

.Pp
.Dq //sys/kern:vnode_if
emits the maintained vnode C source and all three generated headers.
.Dq //sys/kern:vnode_source
and
.Dq //sys/kern:vnode_headers
expose source and compilation-header dependencies for native kernel consumers.
Generation parity preserves the source-level SDT definitions and call sites;
compiled-kernel retention and live probes require separate qualification.

.Pp
Object-interface generation also covers every current device and architecture
.Pa *_if.m
source under sys, using targets named after the interface within its owning
Bazel package. For example,
.Dq //sys/dev/pci:pci_if
and
.Dq //sys/dev/usb:usb_if
emit their maintained C/header pairs. This qualifies text generation only,
not compilation or runtime support for the associated driver or architecture.

.Pp
.Dq //tools/build/bazel/tests/kernel_constants:constants_test
checks native object-based extraction of assembly constants and field offsets,
including compile-time layout assertions. The generation actions declare
execution tools and prerequisite objects and never execute target objects.
This test does not compile GENERIC's actual offset or assembly-constant objects;
configured kernel headers and full kernel builds remain separate work.

.Pp
.Dq //sys/amd64/conf:generic_config
and
.Dq //sys/amd64/conf:generic_debug_config
run native config with declared configuration inputs and capture complete
GENERIC output trees, including empty option headers. They do not execute the
generated Makefile or build a kernel. Other configurations require explicit
input declarations and separate qualification.

.Pp
.Dq //sys/kern:generic_offset
and
.Dq //sys/kern:generic_assym
compile actual amd64 GENERIC layout inputs with generated kernel options and
source headers. The assembly constants require a successful offset-layout
assertion compile. These targets do not link or boot a kernel and do not qualify
other configurations or architectures.

.Pp
GENERIC layout objects depend on individually extracted option headers.
Qualification confirms an unrelated MSGBUF_SIZE change causes no layout C
compilation, while KSTACK_PAGES recompiles genassym only. This is limited
invalidation evidence for layout targets; it is not a complete kernel build
or system performance result.

.Pp
.Dq //sys/kern:generic_config_object ,
.Dq //sys/kern:generic_env_object ,
and
.Dq //sys/kern:generic_hints_object
compile generated kernel data with CTF and retained DWARF.
The format-5 build-settings key
.Dq //tools/build/bazel/config:kernel_ctf_label
sets their CTF label explicitly. Its default matches ctfconvert when Make's
VERSION environment variable is unset. Changing this label reruns conversion
without recompiling these objects. Full kernel linking remains unqualified.

.Pp
.Dq //sys/kern:generic_vnode_if_object
compiles the generated vnode implementation with CTF, retained DWARF and
GENERIC SDT instrumentation. Object code, data and probe relocation records have
Make comparison evidence; a linked kernel and live probe attachment remain
separate qualification gates. Generated-source assertion filenames use a stable
compiler prefix map.

.Pp
The maintained kernel version generator supports explicit reproducible inputs
through newvers.sh's standalone
.Fl e
mode. The Make backend can select it with NEWVERS_ARGS=-e and supply PARAMFILE,
NEWVERS_COPYRIGHT, NEWVERS_COPYRIGHT_TEMPLATE, NEWVERS_IDENT,
NEWVERS_COMPILER_VERSION and NEWVERS_SOURCE_REVISION in the environment.
These identify the target param.h, source copyright and template, kernel identity,
compiler banner and source revision. An explicitly empty revision denotes an
unversioned source archive. This mode uses no host/VCS discovery or build counter;
its caller is responsible for supplying accurate provenance.
The shared renderer has direct Make/source/object comparison evidence.
Complete native kernel and module builds remain unqualified.

.Pp
.Dq //sys/kern:generic_version_source
now generates native GENERIC version C with declared text tools, the selected
C compiler identity, and the parsed identity from
.Dq //sys/amd64/conf:generic_identity .
The format-5 build-settings string
.Dq //tools/build/bazel/config:kernel_source_revision
must explicitly identify the source revision or archive; an empty value fails
analysis. This value is caller-supplied provenance, not automatic source
verification. The existing version_branch setting also applies.
.Dq //sys/kern:generic_vers_object
adds normal kernel compilation, CTF conversion and retained DWARF. Make object
comparison and limited invalidation checks pass; complete kernel/modules,
publication, runtime and system performance remain unqualified.

.Pp
.Dq //sys/fs/nullfs:generic_module_object_files
compiles the three nullfs module sources for amd64 GENERIC with KLD_MODULE,
KLD_TIED, CTF and retained DWARF. Object and relocation comparison uses Make's
WITH_REPRODUCIBLE_PATHS=yes and PREFIX_SYSDIR=sys profile. Default absolute
source-path strings and complete DWARF bytes are not claimed identical.
This object target does not link a module; separate module targets provide
linking, exports, debug splitting and installation metadata.

.Pp
The maintained Make module backend runs and checks nm before its symbol-policy
transforms, then checks the transform before editing ELF. The transforms consume
explicit text listings. Standalone module builds delete failed targets, matching
the source build's existing delete-on-error policy; a later build must not reuse
a partially transformed module. This backend correction does not qualify the
native Bazel module link and package path.

.Pp
.Dq //sys/modules/nullfs:generic_module ,
.Dq generic_full ,
and
.Dq generic_debug
produce the amd64 GENERIC module, full ELF and separate debug file using native
declared link/CTF/export actions. Code/data, relocations, export symbols and CTF
match the Make reference; build IDs and complete DWARF bytes differ.
Debuglink CRC and native kldxref metadata checks pass. No module loading is implied.
.Pp
.Dq //sys/modules/nullfs:install_manifest
records root/wheel ownership, 0444 modes and kernel/debug package tags.
The format-5 settings
.Dq //tools/build/bazel/config:kernel_directory
and
.Dq //tools/build/bazel/config:kernel_debug_directory
default to /boot/modules and /usr/lib/debug. An empty debug directory places
debug files beside the module. The boolean settings
.Dq //tools/build/bazel/config:kernel_ctf
and
.Dq //tools/build/bazel/config:kernel_debug
select kernel/module CTF conversion and separate debug-file generation for
these amd64 GENERIC targets. Either setting enables DWARF during compilation;
when both are disabled, Bazel's opt-mode debug flag is overridden. Their
defaults are disabled and match the selected Make profile with MK_CTF=no and
empty DEBUG_FLAGS. Another Make profile must supply matching values explicitly.
These settings do not infer arbitrary src.conf configuration. Setting changes
preserve compiled payloads.
Package assembly, complete module coverage and runtime qualification
remain open.

.Pp
.Dq //tools/test/construction:nullfs_artifacts_test
is a registered static module/debug audit. It passes with the rebuilt LLVM
objcopy and a freshly staged SDK: objcopy preserves CTF records using original
symbol identities across export localization, renaming and removal.
CTF parsing is deferred when symbol associations do not change.
Earlier SDKs still contain the corrupting transform and must be rebuilt;
Make/Bazel CTF dump equality alone does not establish correct associations.
Fuzzy name matching remains unsupported. This establishes static nullfs
artifact qualification, not complete module, publication or runtime qualification.

.Ss Native LLVM component settings
The format-5 build-settings keys
.Cm //tools/build/bazel/config:llvm_assertions
and
.Cm //tools/build/bazel/config:llvm_full_debuginfo
control assertion compilation and full debug information for native LLVM
components that declare these settings.
Their defaults are true and false, respectively, matching the maintained
LLVM defaults; reduced debug information uses line tables.
These settings do not establish that the entire compiler toolchain has been
converted or qualified.

.Pp
The native Clang component setting
.Cm //tools/build/bazel/config:clang_full
is true by default, matching
.Dv MK_CLANG_FULL .
Components that consume it define
.Dv CLANG_ENABLE_STATIC_ANALYZER .
This setting does not yet establish complete compiler feature-selection coverage.

.Pp
The native
.Cm //tools/build/bazel/config:lldb
setting defaults to true for the currently supported amd64 compiler components.
It selects LLDB-dependent component sources; it does not establish that the
complete debugger has been built or qualified.

.Ss Native Clang compiler defaults
The string settings
.Cm //tools/build/bazel/config:llvm_host_triple
and
.Cm //tools/build/bazel/config:llvm_default_target_triple
select the produced compiler's host and default output triples.
An empty value uses the selected C++ toolchain target triple.
Cross compilers may specify these independently.
.Cm //tools/build/bazel/config:llvm_default_sysroot
sets the produced compiler's default runtime sysroot; its default is empty.
It does not inherit the SDK used to compile Clang.
These inputs currently apply to the native Clang Driver and Frontend components
and LLVM TargetParser;
complete compiler and cross-compilation qualification remain outstanding.

.Pp
The boolean settings
.Cm //tools/build/bazel/config:clang_format
and
.Cm //tools/build/bazel/config:clang_extras
both default to false, matching the maintained source defaults.
The native Clang Format component includes its extra source when either is true.
The native LLVM Transforms component also selects four additional sources when
.Cm clang_extras
is true.
These settings do not yet establish complete optional-tool build or installation
coverage.

.Pp
The boolean setting
.Cm //tools/build/bazel/config:lld
defaults to true, matching the maintained source default.
It currently controls LLD-dependent source selection in the native installed
LLVM CodeGen and LTO components, together with
.Cm clang_extras .
This does not qualify the LLD executable or bootstrap library selection.

.Pp
In the native installed LLVM MC component, the disassembler entry source is
selected when any of
.Cm clang_extras ,
.Cm lld ,
or
.Cm lldb
is true. Bootstrap selection remains unqualified.

.Pp
The native LLVM Bitcode component includes its analyzer source when
.Cm clang_extras
is enabled. This does not establish bitcode tool runtime qualification.

.Pp
The installed LLVM Object component selects its optional entry source with
.Cm clang_extras .
The BinaryFormat component selects ELF.cpp when either
.Cm clang_extras
or
.Cm lldb
is enabled. Bootstrap selection remains unqualified.

.Pp
The installed LLVM DebugInfo component selects additional debug-format and
symbolization sources with
.Cm clang_extras .
This does not establish debugger execution or bootstrap library qualification.

.Pp
The native LLVM DWARFLinker component requires
.Cm clang_extras .
Its component qualification does not establish debug-information linking runtime
behavior or complete compiler linkage.

.Pp
The installed AMD64 LLVM Support component selects partitioning and archive-writing
sources with
.Cm lld ,
the solver source with
.Cm clang_full ,
and system utilities with
.Cm clang_extras .
This component is separate from the minimal TableGen bootstrap library.
Other architectures and complete runtime linkage remain unqualified.

.Pp
The installed LLVM ExecutionEngine component includes its world-stage bridges,
selects interpreter/MCJIT/runtime-loader sources when either
.Cm clang_extras
or
.Cm lldb
is enabled, and selects the remaining ORC/JITLink implementation with
.Cm clang_extras .
The COFF option table is generated by native TableGen.
Component compilation does not qualify JIT execution or remote process behavior.

.Pp
The native LLVM XRay, DWARFCFIChecker, DWP and MCA components require
.Cm clang_extras .
Shared target-machine support and Debuginfod sources are installed-world components.
Debuginfod uses the maintained LLVM configuration, which disables curl and httplib;
component compilation does not establish HTTP functionality.

.Pp
The native AMD64 LLVM X86 component uses the boolean
.Cm //tools/build/bazel/config:llvm_target_x86
setting, which defaults to true. Setting it to false makes the explicit X86
component incompatible; final compiler target registration remains a separate
integration requirement. Its installed-world source selection includes the
disassembler and adds the MCA extension when
.Cm clang_extras
is enabled. Cross-platform defaults and bootstrap selection remain unqualified.

.Pp
The native AMD64-hosted LLVM ARM component uses the boolean
.Cm //tools/build/bazel/config:llvm_target_arm ,
which defaults to true to match the current world target-all selection.
Disabling it makes the explicit ARM component incompatible. This selects a compiler
backend; it does not select the host architecture or qualify an ARM system build.
Aggregate target-all and cross-platform defaults remain unfinished.

.Pp
The native AMD64-hosted LLVM PowerPC component uses the boolean
.Cm //tools/build/bazel/config:llvm_target_powerpc ,
which defaults to true for the current world target-all selection. Disabling it
makes the explicit component incompatible. This selects a compiler backend,
not a PowerPC host or a qualified cross-system build. Aggregate target selection
and cross-platform defaults remain unfinished.

.Pp
The native AMD64-hosted LLVM AArch64 component uses the boolean
.Cm //tools/build/bazel/config:llvm_target_aarch64 ,
which defaults to true for current world target-all selection. Disabling it makes
the explicit component incompatible. Installed-world sources include its disassembler
and external symbolizer. This does not qualify an AArch64 host or cross-system build;
aggregate target selection and cross-platform defaults remain unfinished.

.Pp
The native AMD64-hosted LLVM RISC-V component uses the boolean
.Cm //tools/build/bazel/config:llvm_target_riscv ,
which defaults to true for current world target-all selection. Disabling it makes
the explicit component incompatible. Its MCA source is selected by
.Cm clang_extras .
This selects a compiler backend, not a qualified RISC-V host/system build.
Aggregate target selection and cross-platform defaults remain unfinished.

.Pp
The optional native AMD64-hosted LLVM BPF and LoongArch components use
.Cm //tools/build/bazel/config:llvm_target_bpf
and
.Cm //tools/build/bazel/config:llvm_target_loongarch .
Both booleans default to false, matching the maintained Make configuration.
Enable the corresponding setting when requesting that explicit backend component.
This does not qualify BPF execution, a LoongArch host, or complete compiler linkage.

.Pp
The installed LLVM Telemetry component is selected when either
.Cm clang_extras
or
.Cm lldb
is enabled. The optional Analysis C API entry source is selected by
.Cm clang_extras .
Aggregate source inventory qualification does not establish shared-library linkage
or runtime telemetry delivery.

.Pp
The native LLVM
.Cm //lib/clang/libllvm:pic_archive
and
.Cm //lib/clang/libllvm:llvm_shared
targets produce the selected installed-library PIC aggregate and
.Pa libprivatellvm.so.21
candidate. The shared target declares native C++, C++ ABI and math dependencies;
compression and terminal dependencies remain with compiler/tool executables.
Artifact linkage does not select Bazel as the production backend or establish
compiler runtime, installation, debug-package or pkgbase qualification.

.Pp
The native
.Cm //lib/clang/libclang:clang_shared
target links the selected Clang PIC aggregate with the native LLVM, C++, C++ ABI
and math shared libraries to produce
.Pa libprivateclang.so.21 .
This candidate does not establish compiler execution or complete system builds.

.Pp
The experimental
.Cm //usr.bin/clang/clang:clang
target builds the compiler driver with native Clang and LLVM libraries.
Backend registration follows the
.Cm llvm_target_*
settings; native initialization follows the Bazel platform CPU when enabled.
At least one backend must be selected.
This runtime qualification target uses Bazel runfile library paths and is not an
installation or package target. Cross-system builds and compiler installation
remain separate qualification requirements.

.Pp
The
.Cm //usr.bin/clang/clang:codegen_test
target executes the produced compiler on C and C++ fixtures for enabled backends
and checks the emitted ELF architecture. Select it in both the format-5 targets
and tests lists. It does not exercise system headers, linking or execution of
the generated objects.

.Pp
The experimental
.Cm //usr.bin/clang/clang:sdk_c_test
and
.Cm //usr.bin/clang/clang:sdk_cc_test
targets compile, statically link and execute probes using the candidate compiler
and the explicitly selected staged SDK. They do not establish independent
bootstrap or installed-system defaults.

.Pp
The
.Cm //usr.bin/clang/clang:clang_installed
target links the PIE driver without build-tree runtime search paths.
.Cm //usr.bin/clang/clang:install_archive
packages its stripped executable, debug file, manual, aliases and CC script.
The driver archive does not include the separately installed Clang resource
headers or compiler runtime libraries. Building it does not install or qualify
a complete toolchain or system.

.Pp
The
.Cm //lib/clang/headers:generated_headers
and
.Cm //lib/clang/headers:install_archive
targets generate and package the selected Clang resource headers.
The boolean
.Cm //tools/build/bazel/config:install_conflicting_clang_headers
defaults to false and enables the additional headers corresponding to Make's
.Cm INSTALL_CONFLICTING_CLANG_HEADERS
when true. This archive contains headers only; driver and runtime libraries are
separate targets. Set the boolean through format-5 build-settings.

.Pp
The
.Cm //lib/clang:compiler_install_archive
target combines the driver, resource headers and private LLVM/Clang shared
libraries, including debug files and development symlinks. It does not contain
system runtime libraries, CRT objects, linker or compiler runtime packages and
is not a complete sysroot. Building the archive does not install it on the host.

.Pp
The native ELF linker targets
.Cm //usr.bin/clang/lld:ld.lld
and
.Cm //usr.bin/clang/lld:lld_installed
provide a build-tree runtime candidate and a PIE installed candidate respectively.
.Cm //usr.bin/clang/lld:install_archive
contains its driver, debug file, manual and ld aliases.
The tests
.Cm //usr.bin/clang/clang:native_link_c_test
and
.Cm //usr.bin/clang/clang:native_link_cc_test
use native Clang and LLD together with the selected staged SDK.
They do not establish independent bootstrap or cross-system linker qualification.

.Pp
The tests
.Cm //usr.bin/clang/clang:native_lto_full_c_test ,
.Cm //usr.bin/clang/clang:native_lto_full_cc_test ,
.Cm //usr.bin/clang/clang:native_lto_thin_c_test
and
.Cm //usr.bin/clang/clang:native_lto_thin_cc_test
compile, link and execute static C/C++ probes with native Clang and LLD using
full LTO or ThinLTO. Select these labels in both targets and tests.
These single-translation-unit checks use the selected staged SDK; they do not
qualify inter-module optimization, ThinLTO caches or independent bootstrap.

.Pp
The native archiver targets
.Cm //usr.bin/clang/llvm-ar:llvm-ar
and
.Cm //usr.bin/clang/llvm-ar:llvm_ar_installed
provide build-tree runtime and installed candidates for the default shared LLVM
configuration on amd64 FreeBSD.
.Cm //usr.bin/clang/llvm-ar:install_archive
contains llvm-ar, its debug file, the llvm-ranlib binary hardlink and both distinct
manual pages. Static LLVM library linking and cross-host variants remain outside
this qualification. Building the archive does not install it on the host.

.Pp
The llvm-nm, llvm-cxxfilt, llvm-size and llvm-strings packages under usr.bin/clang
provide native targets named after the utility, an _installed variant, options,
source_inventory and install_archive. They currently qualify the default shared
LLVM configuration on native amd64 FreeBSD.
.Cm //lib/clang:utilities_test
uses native Clang output to exercise symbol reporting, demangling, section
reporting and string extraction. Select this label in both targets and tests.
It does not qualify exhaustive options, cross-host execution or bootstrap.

.Pp
The llvm-readobj and llvm-symbolizer packages also provide native runtime,
_installed, options, source_inventory and install_archive targets.
Their archives preserve the llvm-readelf and llvm-addr2line binary hardlinks and
each alias's distinct manual. The maintained
.Cm //lib/clang:utilities_test
covers ELF inspection, linked debug-line lookup and both argv0 dispatch modes.
Symbol-name lookup in relocatable objects is not qualified by this test.

.Pp
The llvm-objcopy package provides native runtime, _installed, source_inventory,
options and install_archive targets. Four native TableGen actions produce the
option tables. Its archive includes the llvm-strip binary hardlink.
.Cm //lib/clang:utilities_test
covers native debug splitting, strip dispatch, code-section preservation and
address-based resolution through the separate debug file. Installation helpers
still select their declared SDK objcopy/strip; this does not qualify an independent
native bootstrap.

.Pp
The llvm-objdump package provides native runtime, _installed, source_inventory,
options and install_archive targets, with native ObjdumpOpts/OtoolOpts generation.
.Cm //lib/clang:utilities_test
also checks AMD64 disassembly and code/debug-section reporting on native Clang
and LLD output. Other object formats and cross-architecture disassembly remain
outside this runtime qualification.

.Pp
The llvm-profdata and llvm-cov packages provide native runtime, _installed,
source_inventory and install_archive targets.
.Cm //lib/clang:profiles_test
runs a native Clang/LLD instrumented probe, merges its profile with native
llvm-profdata and reports coverage with native llvm-cov. Select it in targets
and tests. The probe compiles instrumentation separately and explicitly links
.Cm //lib/libclang_rt/profile:profile_archive
rather than selecting a profile runtime from the SDK.
The profile package also supplies source_inventory, install_manifest and
install_archive targets for native amd64, including its two profile headers.
Other system runtime and headers still use the declared SDK; this does not
establish independent compiler-runtime bootstrap or exhaustive gcov coverage.

.Pp
The clang-scan-deps and clang-format packages provide native runtime, _installed,
source_inventory and install_archive candidates; neither installs a manual.
Scanner also provides its generated options target. clang-format requires
clang_format or clang_extras in build-settings to include its library support.
Both have fresh Make binary/install parity evidence for the tested configuration;
the formatter reference uses a complete Make Clang library with formatting enabled.
These checks do not establish independent bootstrap.

.Pp
.Cm //lib/clang:clang_tools_test
checks per-file dependency scanning across a nested include change, explicit
LLVM formatting, formatting idempotence and dry-run validation. Enable
clang_format or clang_extras and select this label in targets and tests.
These checks do not qualify compilation-database/module/PCH variants or Bazel
cache invalidation.

.Pp
.Cm //usr.bin/clang/toolchain:install_archive
composes the native LLVM utility installations and their default-name aliases.
The format-5 build setting
.Cm //tools/build/bazel/config:llvm_binutils
(default true) controls eleven executable symlinks and eleven manual hardlinks.
False retains the LLVM-named utilities and omits their default-name aliases.
This archive is not a complete compiler, sysroot or package repository; building
it does not install it on the host.

.Pp
.Cm //lib/clang:compiler_tools_install_archive
combines the compiler driver, resource headers, private Clang/LLVM libraries,
LLD, LLVM utility installations/default-name aliases, dependency scanner and
profile-data utility. Formatting follows clang_format or clang_extras.
It excludes debugger/optional LLVM extras, system headers, CRT/runtime and
compiler-runtime packages. It is not a complete SDK or installed system.
The existing compiler_install_archive retains its narrower contents.

.Pp
.Cm //lib/csu/amd64:startup_objects
and
.Cm //lib/csu/amd64:install_archive
provide the ten native amd64 startup objects and their installation metadata.
.Cm //usr.bin/clang/clang:native_startup_c_test
and
.Cm //usr.bin/clang/clang:native_startup_cc_test
compile and run static probes with native startup objects, compiler and linker.
The probes retain SDK system headers and libraries. Broader startup cases are
covered by the maintained csu matrix below; other architectures, profiling
startup and independent bootstrap remain unqualified.

.Pp
.Cm //lib/csu/amd64:startup
supplies validated startup artifacts and
.Cm //lib/csu/amd64:startup_link_flags
supplies their compiler-driver response file.
Final rules_cc links declare both in additional_linker_inputs and pass
@$(location //lib/csu/amd64:startup_link_flags) in linkopts or user_link_flags.
Startup selection must be attached to the final link: CcInfo-only flags can be
pruned during dynamic_deps processing.
.Pp
The individual
.Cm //lib/csu/tests:static_* ,
.Cm dynamic_* ,
.Cm dynamicpie_* ,
.Cm dynamiclib_*
and errno case targets exercise the maintained startup test matrix.
Select explicit case labels in format-5 targets and tests. Mode test suites group
the ATF cases for direct Bazel use. All 36 cases passed in the tested native amd64
configuration, with final-link startup selection and runtime libraries checked.
The tests retain the prepared SDK compiler/system libraries and host rtld;
GCRT, other architectures and independent bootstrap remain separate
qualification work.

.Pp
.Cm //lib/csu/tests:install_archive
packages all maintained csu test programs, their helper, debug payloads and Kyua
registrations. Installation metadata, Kyuafile bytes, debug links and debug-payload
symbol records match a fresh Make reference for the qualified configuration.
The two errno programs using explicit -static omit separate debug files, matching
bsd.prog.mk. A separate extracted-archive Kyua run passed all 36 cases with
declared runtime libraries; this remains a diagnostic stage using the host
kernel/rtld and SDK Kyua. The archive does not itself qualify a complete installed system or
pkgbase repository. The bsd_kyuafile rule accepts an optional output path when
composing multiple registrations in one Bazel package.

.Pp
.Cm //lib/libclang_rt/include:install_archive
packages the maintained sanitizer interface headers.
.Cm //lib/libclang_rt/ubsan_minimal:install_archive
packages the native amd64 minimal UBSan runtime; static_archive and
source_inventory are also available.
.Cm //lib/libclang_rt/ubsan_minimal:runtime_test
checks clean, recoverable signed-overflow and aborting signed-overflow behavior
with compiler-generated instrumentation and the explicitly linked native archive.
The registered SDK compiler/system runtime remain in use. These checks do not
qualify other sanitizer runtimes, all handlers, other architectures or bootstrap.

.Pp
The lib/libclang_rt/asan-preinit, asan_static and stats_client packages provide
static_archive, source_inventory, install_manifest and install_archive targets
using the common native amd64 runtime.bzl policy. Their archive inventories,
symbol records and installation metadata match fresh Make references. This is
support-component qualification, not proof of working full ASan instrumentation
or statistics reporting; complete runtime consumers remain to be tested.

.Pp
The lib/libclang_rt/asan and asan_cxx packages provide native amd64 archive and
installation targets, including the maintained ASan ignorelist.
.Cm //lib/libclang_rt/asan:runtime_test
checks clean execution, heap overflow and use-after-free with real compiler
instrumentation and explicitly linked native archives. Compiler-rt uses -fpic
without the toolchain PIC variant's -DPIC, matching Make's static-runtime policy.
The C++ companion currently has archive comparison only; SDK system libraries,
host kernel/rtld and independent bootstrap remain qualification boundaries.

.Pp
The lib/libclang_rt/ubsan_standalone and ubsan_standalone_cxx packages provide
native amd64 archive and installation targets matching fresh Make references.
.Cm //lib/libclang_rt/ubsan_standalone:runtime_test
checks clean execution and a signed-overflow diagnostic/nonzero exit using real
compiler instrumentation and the explicitly linked native standalone archive.
C++ type checks and broader handler coverage remain unqualified; the prepared
SDK compiler/system libraries and host kernel/rtld remain in use.

.Pp
The lib/libclang_rt/safestack, stats, cfi and cfi_diag packages expose native
amd64 static_archive, source_inventory and install_archive targets. CFI includes
its maintained ignorelist. Build success for these targets does not establish
SafeStack thread behavior, statistics reporting or CFI enforcement; those need
instrumented runtime qualification. No new frontend input schema is required.

.Pp
The lib/libclang_rt/fuzzer, fuzzer_no_main and fuzzer_interceptors packages
provide native amd64 archive and installation targets. The main fuzzer package
also installs FuzzedDataProvider.h using the common runtime rule's declared
installed_headers mapping. These builds do not by themselves qualify fuzzing
execution, coverage, interceptors or corpus behavior.

.Pp
.Cm //lib/libclang_rt/fuzzer:runtime_test
and
.Cm //lib/libclang_rt/fuzzer:custom_main_test
run bounded instrumented campaigns with native libFuzzer and libc++ archives,
checking callback execution with supplied and custom main entry points.
These tests retain SDK CRT/system libraries and do not qualify optional sanitizer
crash hooks, ASan integration, disk corpus or subprocess behavior.

.Pp
The lib/libclang_rt/msan and msan_cxx packages expose native amd64 archive,
source-inventory and installation targets. The main runtime includes its
maintained ignorelist. Archive qualification does not establish uninitialized-read
detection, origin tracking, C++ allocation or thread/interceptor behavior.

.Pp
The lib/libclang_rt/tsan and tsan_cxx packages provide native amd64 archive,
source-inventory and installation targets. The main archive retains explicit
Make-compatible assembly placement through validated object_order metadata.
Archive/install comparisons do not qualify race detection, thread lifecycle,
synchronization or interceptor behavior.

.Pp
The lib/libclang_rt/xray, xray-basic, xray-fdr and xray-profiling packages expose
native amd64 archive and installation targets. Core includes the amd64 trampoline
and maintained public headers. These archive targets do not qualify runtime
patching, callback delivery or correctness of recorded tracing data.

.Pp
.Cm //lib/libclang_rt/dd:install_archive
packages the native amd64 deadlock-detector archive. Its source inventory, archive
members/symbols and installation metadata match a fresh Make reference. This does
not qualify lock-order inversion detection or runtime interceptor behavior.

.Pp
.Cm //lib/libclang_rt/asan_dynamic:runtime_shared
and its install_archive target provide the native amd64 dynamic ASan candidate,
including split debug data and package-tagged debug directories. Native shared
C++/math libraries are declared link-only inputs. Runtime startup ordering and
memory-error reporting require qualification separately from static ASan.

.Pp
.Cm //lib/libclang_rt/asan_dynamic:runtime_test
exercises clean, heap-overflow and use-after-free behavior with declared native
shared ASan/C++/math libraries and SDK system libraries. Its original startup failure was traced to FreeBSD TLS module discovery and
corrected. It and tls_runtime_test now pass for executables without and with
PT_TLS; the static ASan regression and rebuilt Make shared runtime also pass.
Thread/dlopen TLS lifecycle and C++ allocation still need qualification.

.Pp
The asan_dynamic thread_runtime_test and thread_tls_runtime_test targets check
worker creation/join, thread-specific-data destruction and memory-error detection
with and without executable TLS. The TLS variant also checks per-thread value
isolation. Dynamic library TLS loading and high-concurrency behavior remain
separate qualification work.

.Pp
.Cm //lib/libclang_rt:install_archive
composes the complete maintained amd64 compiler-runtime installation from 27
component manifests, including headers, ignorelists and shared-runtime debug data.
It is an installation artifact, not an independently bootstrapped toolchain or
a complete pkgbase transaction. Other architectures remain unqualified.

.Ss Native services database generation
.Cm //usr.sbin/services_mkdb:install_archive
includes the utility, manual and services configuration.
The format-5 build-settings boolean
.Cm //tools/build/bazel/config:servicesdb
mirrors MK_SERVICESDB and defaults to true.
When enabled, the archive also includes services.db generated from the declared
services source using the native execution-platform services_mkdb, loader and
libraries. Byte order is selected from the target configuration; unsupported
architectures fail selection instead of using the execution host's byte order.
The current utility target is qualified only for amd64.
Setting the boolean false omits database generation and installation while
retaining the utility and configuration.
This component archive does not establish installed NSS behavior or complete
pkgbase ownership/update handling for the generated database.

.Ss Native nscd configuration prefix
.Cm //usr.sbin/nscd:install_archive
includes the daemon, configuration and both manuals.
The format-5 build-settings string
.Cm //tools/build/bazel/config:nscd_prefix
mirrors nscd's Make PREFIX for its compiled configuration path.
It defaults to an empty string; a nonempty value must be an absolute path.
A declared header defines CONFIG_PATH as the prefix followed by /etc/nscd.conf.
As in maintained Make, this setting does not relocate the installed
/etc/nscd.conf payload or change the relative fallback nscd.conf lookup.
Default and /opt/5bsd configurations have artifact parity on amd64;
this does not establish live NSS/cache or daemon runtime behavior.

.Ss Native mptutil debug variant
.Cm //usr.sbin/mptutil:install_archive
supports the format-5 build-settings boolean
.Cm //tools/build/bazel/config:mptutil_debug
(default false), corresponding to defining DEBUG in maintained Make.
It enables additional controller commands and diagnostic code, not merely debug
symbols. Both variants have amd64 installation/artifact parity; no controller
operations were executed during qualification.

.Ss Native MPS and MPR utilities
.Cm //usr.sbin/mpsutil:install_archive
installs mpsutil and the mprutil executable/manual hardlinks.
The executable selects the controller interface from its invoked name.
The format-5 build-settings boolean
.Cm //tools/build/bazel/config:mpsutil_debug
(default false) matches Make's defined DEBUG variant.
USE_MPT_IOCTLS remains defined in both configurations.
Artifact and version/argument-dispatch checks cover both names and variants;
controller and firmware operations remain unqualified.

.Ss Native MFI and MRSAS utilities
.Cm //usr.sbin/mfiutil:install_archive
installs mfiutil and the mrsasutil executable/manual hardlinks.
The format-5 build-settings boolean
.Cm //tools/build/bazel/config:mfiutil_debug
(default false) matches Make's defined DEBUG variant, enabling additional
controller diagnostics. The legacy dump command resolves the opened device
and supports only the mfi driver diagnostic sysctl.
Controller operations require separate runtime qualification.

.Ss Native installer helper identity
The format-5 build-settings string
.Cm //tools/build/bazel/config:installer_osname
(default 5BSD) supplies the maintained installer OSNAME header used by
.Cm distextract , distfetch
and
.Cm partedit .
It must be nonempty and contain no control characters.
The native partedit target currently selects the x86 implementation on amd64;
this does not qualify an installation or disk-editing operation.

.Ss Native installer installation candidate
.Cm //usr.sbin/bsdinstall:install_archive
assembles the installer helper binaries, scripts, generated pkgbase script,
repository configuration and manuals.
Its installed test fixture mapping remains unfinished, so the target rejects
.Cm //tools/build/bazel/config:tests=true
instead of silently dropping the requested test subtree.
Artifact comparison does not qualify a disk installation or an upgrade.

.Ss Native book installation candidate
.Cm //share/doc/5bsd:install_archive
installs the native Markdown book as runtime-package content.
Its explicit chapter inventory rejects missing or unlisted source files and
is shared with native source ownership for installer test fixtures.
This target does not assemble the installer test package or run its checks.

.Pp
The intermediate
.Cm //usr.sbin/bsdinstall/tests:fixture_archive
target contains installer test scripts, Kyuafile and source fixtures.
It excludes the pending generated manual-page and sysctl inventories and is
not a complete installed test package.
The parent installer target continues to reject tests=true until those
inventories are also represented natively.

.Ss Cross-target Bazel kernel artifacts
Format-5 source graph inputs may select
.Dq target-kind
set to
.Dq kernel
and use
.Fl arch Ar arm64
or
.Fl arch Ar riscv64 .
The native amd64 SDK supplies execution tools; registered target C++ toolchains
select the target triple, freestanding ABI and declared source headers.
The host platform remains native, so config and layout-extraction programs
are built for the host. Target objects are never executed on that host.
This profile builds artifacts only and rejects runnable test selections.
The default target kind is userland and currently requires amd64.
Cross userland, complete kernels and module sets remain unqualified.

.Pp
The explicit ARM64 and RISC-V filemon module targets exercise relocatable
linking, CTF/export processing and the target shared-module link, with the
existing typed kernel CTF/debug settings and installation metadata.
These candidates do not qualify complete module sets or runtime loading.

.Pp
Explicit target labels also cover ARM64 and RISC-V backlight, firmware,
CUSE and cryptodev module candidates, using the same kernel profile, generated
options/interfaces, CTF/debug settings and file installation metadata.
Shared export-all and multi-object module linking are included in their
independent Make artifact comparisons. Runtime loading remains unqualified.

.Pp
The ARM64 and RISC-V64 kernel profiles also cover the nineteen VirtIO modules
selected by their GENERIC Make inventories, with explicit module and install
targets and target-generated options.
The PMEM module remains amd64-only, matching its NVDIMM dependency.
Independent comparisons cover CTF/debug disabled and enabled artifacts and file
installation records; matching-kernel module loading and device operation remain
unqualified.

.Pp
Explicit ARM64 and RISC-V64 filesystem candidates include the two ACL modules,
cd9660 and cd9660_iconv, fdescfs, nullfs, pseudofs, tmpfs and unionfs.
They use target-generated options and the existing kernel CTF/debug settings.
Independent Make comparisons cover installed artifacts and file metadata;
filesystem operations and matching-kernel module loading remain unqualified.

.Pp
ARM64 and RISC-V64 candidates also include mac_abac, mac_ddb, mac_do, mac_ntpd,
mac_pimd, mac_portacl, mac_priority, mac_test, mac_test_hooks and oes.
The normal kernel profile preserves generated MAC options and individual kernel,
mac-abac, oes and test package assignments.
Artifact/CTF/debug and file metadata comparisons do not qualify policy
enforcement, authorization, event delivery or matching-kernel module loading.

.Pp
Seventeen Netgraph module/install candidates are also available for ARM64 and
RISC-V64 using actual target KDB, NETGRAPH and network option headers.
The shared header graph selects options by Bazel target CPU and preserves the
qualified amd64 artifact outputs.
Cross artifact and metadata comparisons do not establish remaining Netgraph/core
module coverage, socket/packet behavior or matching-kernel runtime operation.

.Pp
ARM64 and RISC-V64 candidates also cover the eight cc TCP congestion-control
modules, bridgestp, if_disc, if_edsc, if_enc, if_gif, if_gre and if_me.
Target-generated network options and original source/dependency selections feed
the existing CTF/debug and installation pipeline.
Independent artifact/metadata comparisons do not qualify protocol behavior,
algorithm performance or matching-kernel module loading.

.Pp
Additional ARM64 and RISC-V64 candidates cover fifteen GEOM modules, alq, nmdm,
rc4 and the four accept filters, preserving target options and module export
policy.
GEOM UZIP's existing typed kernel_zstdio setting selects its actual ZSTD source;
independent comparisons cover both values and both CTF/debug modes.
These artifact/metadata checks do not qualify live storage/filter behavior,
cryptographic operation, module loading or resource performance.

.Pp
The kernel target profile also builds eighteen new Netgraph core/node module
and install candidates on amd64, ARM64 and RISC-V64.
Their shared native source inventory, actual target BPF options and registered
freestanding toolchains preserve Make's module exports and installation metadata.
Independent comparisons include both CTF/debug modes; remaining Bluetooth/MPPC
modules, runtime traffic/control behavior and complete kernels remain unqualified.

.Pp
The ng_mppc module and install candidate also covers amd64, ARM64 and RISC-V64
with GENERIC's existing compression/encryption defaults.
Independent comparisons cover plain and CTF/debug artifacts and install metadata.
Custom MPPC option/source-variable combinations, live traffic and module loading
remain unqualified; no new frontend option or security policy is introduced.

.Pp
Seven Bluetooth kernel module/install candidates also cover amd64, ARM64 and
RISC-V64, including USB transports and virtual HCI.
Target-generated USB/bus options and the original native USB device-ID generator
feed existing module, CTF/debug and installation rules.
Artifact/metadata comparisons and unchanged-profile cache reuse do not establish
live controller/socket/firmware behavior or complete kernels and module sets.

.Pp
Native mqueuefs, sem, sysvmsg, sysvsem and sysvshm module/install candidates cover
amd64, ARM64 and RISC-V64 with actual target option headers and original exports.
The maintained System V Makefiles also declare their missing vnode interface.
Artifact and metadata comparisons do not establish IPC runtime/authorization,
module loading or complete kernels and module sets.

.Pp
Native autofs, ext2fs, fusefs, msdosfs, msdosfs_iconv, p9fs, tarfs and ufs
module/install candidates cover amd64, ARM64 and RISC-V64 with real target options
and original exports.
Tarfs comparisons cover both values of the existing kernel_zstdio setting.
The maintained msdosfs_iconv Makefile also declares its missing vnode interface.
Artifact/metadata checks do not qualify live filesystem behavior, storage
performance or complete kernels/module sets.

.Pp
Native NFS client, common, server, locking and nfssvc module/install candidates
cover amd64, ARM64 and RISC-V64.
The format-5 build-settings boolean
.Dq //tools/build/bazel/config:kernel_nfs_root
selects both actual target NFS_ROOT configuration and the optional diskless
object group; GENERIC defaults true and comparisons cover both values.
Kernel NLM RPC C/headers retain their maintained source-controlled contract.
Artifact checks do not qualify NFS traffic, RPC authentication, lock recovery,
NFS-root boot or complete kernels/module sets.
.Pp
Native krpc, kgssapi and kgssapi_krb5 module/install candidates declare
amd64, ARM64 and RISC-V64 object groups.
Kernel RPC generation declares schemas, rpcgen, preprocessor and filter tools;
stdout generation preserves the maintained header-guard semantics before
applying the original kernel include filters.
KOBJ interfaces use the maintained generator.
Plain artifacts, installed CTF sections, generated inputs and file install
metadata compare with independent Make references on all three architectures.
Separate DWARF layout and KOBJ source-provenance comments differ.
These artifact checks do not establish live TLS/GSS/Kerberos authentication or
matching-kernel module operation.

.Pp
Native CARP, bridge, epair, lagg and VLAN module/install candidates cover
amd64, ARM64 and RISC-V64.
Declared option headers are extracted from the actual target GENERIC config;
artifact and file install-metadata comparisons cover plain and CTF/debug modes.
These checks do not qualify live networking, failover, alternate kernel options
or complete kernels; separate DWARF layout remains outside the artifact gate.

.Pp
Native libntp and ntptime candidates use the existing format-5 target list.
The source-owned build settings
.Li //usr.sbin/ntp:version_serial
and
.Li //usr.sbin/ntp:version_cset
select an explicit positive version counter and revision token;
defaults reproduce a fresh Make object tree.
A private action runs the maintained version script with declared tools.
Ordinary and PIE internal archives remain separate.
Default and OpenSSL-disabled artifact/install comparisons and help-only tests
are qualified; clock-control runtime, remaining NTP programs and complete
package aggregation remain open.

.Pp
Native OpenBSD PF and dummynet module/install candidates cover amd64, ARM64
and RISC-V64 through the existing format-5 kernel profile.
PF preserves its compatibility-header precedence, distinct object-group warning
and define policies, and maintained final link order.
Artifact and file metadata comparisons cover plain and CTF/debug modes; they do
not qualify live packet filtering, queues, synchronization, probe arguments or
complete kernels. Separate DWARF layout remains outside this artifact gate.

.Pp
Native ntpdc, ntpq and private AutoOpts candidates preserve ordinary/PIE object
variants, optional OpenSSL, per-program version actions and their Make paths.
Help/version and artifact/install comparisons are qualified; NTP protocol and
daemon runtime remain open.
Artifact comparison tooling can select captured outputs with
.Fl -qualification ,
.Fl -target
and
.Fl -output-suffix ;
it verifies the receipt's manifest, content hashes and containment and rejects
ambiguous selections.
Raw
.Fl -candidate
files carry no native qualification binding.
The regression target
.Li //tools/build/bazel:qualification_artifacts_test
uses the normal format-5 target/test lists.

.Pp
Native wlan, wlan_acl, wlan_amrr, wlan_ccmp, wlan_gcmp, wlan_rssadapt,
wlan_tkip, wlan_wep and wlan_xauth module/install candidates cover amd64,
ARM64 and RISC-V64 GENERIC in plain and CTF/debug modes.
Source ownership, per-file warnings, shared AES objects, generated option headers,
link order and file metadata have independent Make comparisons.
Radio/firmware, live wireless behavior, complete kernels and controlled performance
remain outside this artifact qualification.
.Pp
Native NTP libparse and libntpevent archive candidates preserve ordinary and PIE
objects, maintained event configuration and conditional OpenSSL sources.
The normal format-5 target lists and OpenSSL setting select these builds;
no frontend-specific replacement build is required.
Captured archive symbol/order comparisons and actual compile-option audits
cover both settings; remaining NTP program/runtime and package aggregation
gates remain open.

.Pp
Native nvme, nvd and ahci module/install candidates cover amd64, ARM64 and
RISC-V64 GENERIC in plain and CTF/debug modes, including ARM64's additional
AHCI source and ACPI/OFW interfaces. Source/option inputs, warnings, object order,
probe sections and installed file metadata have independent Make comparisons.
Live controller/disk I/O, recovery, full kernels and controlled performance remain
outside this artifact qualification.
.Pp
Native ntpdate, ntp-keygen and sntp candidates preserve their maintained version
ownership, optional OpenSSL, PIE and pkgbase installation metadata.
Default and OpenSSL-disabled format-5 profiles cover build/install contracts
and bounded usage/help/version exits through the normal test action.
Server queries, clock changes, key generation and daemon runtime are not
qualified by these tests.

.Pp
Native CAM core/peripheral module and install candidates cover amd64, ARM64
and RISC-V64 GENERIC in plain and CTF/debug modes. All thirty original C sources,
generated option inputs including opt_kdtrace.h, warnings, probe sections,
object order and installed file metadata have independent Make comparisons.
Live devices, transport/peripheral behavior, recovery, complete kernels and
controlled performance remain outside this artifact qualification.

.Pp
Native dttransport, hypertrace, dtaudit, dtmalloc, dtnfscl, profile, prototype
and sdt module/install candidates cover amd64, ARM64 and RISC-V64 GENERIC in
plain and CTF/debug modes. CDDL compatibility headers and forced-header order
are declared inputs; normal generic-helper behavior has an if_epair regression.
Artifacts, options, export/object order and file metadata have independent Make
comparisons. Live tracing/transport, core DTrace and remaining modules, complete
kernels and controlled performance remain outside this qualification.
.Pp
Native ntpd candidates retain the original time-discipline DTrace provider,
static anchor, generated version, refclock sources and package configuration.
Default, OpenSSL-disabled and DTrace-disabled format-5 profiles cover emitted
probe/site and build/install contracts plus early help/version exits.
These checks do not qualify a running time service, live probe firing, clock
changes or complete NTP package aggregation.
.Pp
Native NTP documentation and family installation archives use the maintained
format-5 target lists.
The typed
.Li //tools/build/bazel/config:html
boolean defaults true; false omits HTML/data files and their directories while
retaining all nine manuals.
The existing
.Li //tools/build/bazel/config:split_manpages
setting selects separate manual package tags.
These are entries in the native JSON
.Li build-settings
object; this frontend does not offer
.Fl -with-option
or
.Fl -without-option
CLI switches.
Independent Make references use those switches on the reference helper.
The combined installation archive is not a completed native package repository.

.Pp
Native core DTrace, FBT, kinst and dtraceall component profiles use genuine
GENERIC target-generated interfaces and assembly constants on amd64, ARM64 and
RISC-V64, retaining original object order and C-only CTF conversion.
The format-5 module and install targets qualify captured artifacts against
independent Make outputs; profiles without runtime test targets do not establish
module loading, probe firing or a complete native kernel build.

.Pp
Native ctladm and iscsid program/install targets use existing typed ISCSI
configuration and the normal format-5 qualification route.
Safe usage tests run before device or service operations; installed CTL port
tests retain their original root and kernel-module requirements and require a
disposable guest for runtime qualification.
These component checks do not establish working storage sessions or native
package publication.

.Pp
Native systrace profiles generate syscall headers and textual tracing C from
maintained master/configuration files using the original Lua generator API and
an explicitly declared execution-platform flua tool.
Native/Linux syscall tables, fasttrap and dtrace_test component qualifications
cover original build/install contracts, not live syscall tracing or execution
of the kernel test module.

.Pp
Native ctld targets declare the original C++ sources, yacc/lex generation and
installation metadata through normal format-5 inputs.
The usage test exits before kernel initialization; configuration-test mode
invokes the kernel and remains part of disposable-guest qualification.
Component builds do not establish a working CTL service or storage sessions.

.Pp
The native DTrace directory exposes per-architecture module, debug and install
manifest groups matching maintained directory membership and order.
Six plain/CTF component profiles cover fifteen amd64 modules and thirteen each
on ARM64 and RISC-V64; the parent system graph remains responsible for selecting
the directory under its CDDL/DTrace configuration.
Successful family builds and unchanged warm reuse do not establish whole-system
kernel packaging or runtime tracing.

.Pp
Native opensolaris compatibility-module profiles retain all maintained sources,
export-all behavior and the original complete OpenZFS header search order on
amd64, ARM64 and RISC-V64.
Artifact/install and physical-header comparisons qualify this component;
actual module loading and DTrace dependency resolution remain runtime gates.

.Pp
Native tcpdump uses the maintained OpenBSD sources and 5BSD compatibility glue.
The existing typed inet6_support setting and normal format-5 inputs select
program/install/offline-test targets.
Offline tests consume private PCAP files; they do not qualify live capture,
interface controls or privilege separation.

.Pp
Native virtiofsd program/install targets and the original daemon ATF cases use
normal format-5 qualification inputs.
The unchanged tests embed production sources and exercise private local sockets;
they do not establish a complete guest filesystem or execution of the installed
daemon binary.

.Pp
Native ACPI support profiles preserve maintained root/directory selection:
sixteen modules on amd64 and two on ARM64; RISC-V64 does not select this family.
Artifact, compiler-policy, interface/option and physical-header comparisons
qualify component build/install behavior, not hardware operation or fault
injection.

.Pp
The historical deferred bsdtrace native candidate is preserved in
.Pa tools/build/bazel/checkpoints/bsdtrace-20261101.patch
with resume evidence in
.Pa tools/build/bazel/BSDTRACE-CHECKPOINT.md .
The saved patch itself is not evidence of a passed qualification.
Its first attempt rejected a changed SDK tool after host upgrade; a fresh
snapshot passed hash verification, but the subsequent native build was
canceled for another requested upgrade checkpoint.
Revalidate all host-bound SDK and Make-reference inputs before resuming.

.Pp
The resumed native bsdtrace component now qualifies fourteen explicit GNU C17
translation units, nine installation records, ELF dependency/hardening metadata,
both installed debug CRCs and eleven original safe ATF cases.
All thirteen original cases are installed; the public-header compiler and
kernel-image default-path cases remain disposable-guest gates.
See
.Pa tools/build/bazel/BSDTRACE-QUALIFICATION.md
for immutable artifact bindings, source-header checks, retained failed attempts
and live hardware/runtime/performance limits.
The normal format-5 bazel-test action is used; Make remains production/default.

.Pp
Native PCI Ethernet profiles cover thirteen maintained amd64 selections and
ten each on ARM64 and RISC-V64, with plain and CTF installation comparisons,
original generated device IDs, option/header inputs and object order.
See
.Pa tools/build/bazel/PCI-ETHERNET-QUALIFICATION.md
for exact evidence and resource guards.
These are component build gates; hardware operation and full system builds
remain unqualified. The existing format-5 action is used.

.Pp
The native base-accounts component uses the existing format-5 action for
literal script/reference/manual/test installation and native guest helpers.
Its nine default component installation records match Make, and all eleven
original ATF cases pass at normal paths in a bounded disposable guest,
including the absolute pw/pwd_mkdb helper chain and shipped reference files.
A separate test-disabled, manpage-split profile matches all six component
installation records and four literal payloads against Make.
The maintained explicit-input fixture assembler and daemon-runtime VM harness
preserve original test bodies, unprivileged identities and cleanup.
See
.Pa tools/build/bazel/BASE-ACCOUNTS-QUALIFICATION.md
for bootstrap, configuration and system-level limits.
This component gate does not qualify normal startup, host account migration,
full pkgbase creation or system upgrades; Make remains production/default.

.Pp
Native MII-family profiles qualify miibus, mdio and miiproxy on amd64, ARM64
and RISC-V64, including generated interfaces, FDT source selection, plain/CTF
installation records, source/header inputs and original object order.
See
.Pa tools/build/bazel/MII-FAMILY-QUALIFICATION.md
for exact comparisons and retained reference failures.
The existing format-5 action is used; hardware operation and complete native
world/kernel/pkgbase builds remain open.

.Pp
Native iflib, etherswitch and toecore profiles qualify plain/CTF products on
amd64, ARM64 and RISC-V64, including generated interfaces, installation
metadata, source/header inputs and original object order.
All six iflib configurations retain Make's SDT section/symbol contracts.
See
.Pa tools/build/bazel/NETWORK-SUPPORT-QUALIFICATION.md
for immutable evidence and retained analysis failures.
Hardware operation, live probe firing and complete native system builds
remain open; Make remains production/default.

.Pp
Native mailwrapper uses existing typed format-5 settings plus MAILWRAPPER
selection to preserve MAIL, DMAGENT, SENDMAIL and OPENSSL dependencies,
configuration files, examples, aliases and package ownership.
All ten selected component configurations match 84 unique Make installation
records; four private unprivileged dispatch/error cases and corrected compiler
policy checks pass.
See
.Pa tools/build/bazel/MAILWRAPPER-QUALIFICATION.md
for immutable artifact bindings, debug/ELF checks and unqualified privileged
fallback, actual mail delivery and full-system gates.
No production mail configuration is installed or modified by qualification.

.Pp
Native dtracedctl accepts the typed format-5
.Va //tools/build/bazel/config:dtrace_optimized
boolean, default false, to select authentic full LTO.
Plain and optimized component builds qualify Make compiler/header/link inputs,
ELF contracts, installation and six pre-connect parser test methods each.
See
.Pa tools/build/bazel/DTRACEDCTL-QUALIFICATION.md
for captured linked bitcode and separate installed daemon/IPC runtime gates.
No host tracing or daemon connection is activated by these tests.

.Pp
Native PPP uses existing typed format-5 source settings plus
.Va pam_support , radius_support
and the seven component
.Va //usr.sbin/ppp:no_*
selectors to preserve actual Make source/library selections and installation
policy, including root:network setuid mode and private configuration files.
See
.Pa tools/build/bazel/PPP-QUALIFICATION.md
for three qualified build profiles, retained source-diagnostic differences,
pppctl usage tests and separate privileged/network/runtime gates.
Custom configuration-directory and experimental netgraph inputs remain open.
.Pp
Native USB network qualification uses existing format-5 kernel profiles,
module/install actions and target GENERIC option providers for nineteen
source-selected modules on amd64, ARM64 and RISC-V64.
All 114 plain/CTF component configurations match Make installation contracts;
plain modules are byte-identical and installed CTF sections/debug CRCs pass.
See
.Pa tools/build/bazel/USB-NETWORK-QUALIFICATION.md
for original compiler/link-input audits, retained analysis failures and separate
hardware, live tracing, alternate configuration and full-system gates.
No host module loading is performed by these checks.

.Pp
Existing format-5 native inputs qualify the corrected source-owned ACPICA,
evdev and autofs header providers without new command-line options.
The real configured kernel query retains version and COPYRIGHT dependencies.
See
.Pa tools/build/bazel/ROOT-HEADER-OWNERSHIP-QUALIFICATION.md
for seven affected userland build/install regressions and explicit bhyve
dependency-order, physical-section and runtime reconciliation gates.
Configured analysis and archive generation do not establish a complete
native system, runtime qualification or split-package ownership.
.Pp
The existing format-5 target/test API runs
.Va //tools/build/bazel:kernel_inventory_test
with declared native bmake test data.
The separate
.Pa tools/build/bazel/kernel_inventory.py
audit reads an explicitly configured Make kernel without building it.
See
.Pa tools/build/bazel/KERNEL-INVENTORY.md
for configuration freshness, final-link/post-processing preservation and
remaining native kernel closure gates.
Query success is not a kernel build or runtime qualification.
.Pp
The native ELF comparison helper now rejects a different ordered dynamic
library dependency vector for both programs and shared libraries.
Its standalone
.Pa tools/build/bazel/elf-needed-order-test.py
regression accepts explicit executable, shared-library, SONAME, inspector,
object-copy and work-directory inputs and checks private negative ELF copies.
This is an inspection regression, not a guest runtime test.
The normal format-5 qualification path remains responsible for capturing the
native build outputs; no library-order override is provided.
See
.Pa tools/build/bazel/ELF-NEEDED-ORDER-QUALIFICATION.md
for the default bhyve link correction, fresh Make reference, installation
checks, and remaining section, configuration and runtime gates.
.Pp
Native component qualification now includes cxgbetool and mlx5tool through the
existing format-5 target and test lists.
Their package manifests preserve the original driver-interface headers,
textually included register tables, manuals and package tags.
The pre-device argument tests run as an ordinary user and do not qualify NIC
operations or firmware changes.
See
.Pa tools/build/bazel/NIC-TOOLS-QUALIFICATION.md
for independent Make, immutable ELF, installation, object and input checks and
remaining hardware, configuration and cross-userland gates.
.Pp
The existing format-5 target API also qualifies seventy-one amd64 GENERIC core
kernel objects and an existing clock-conversion group.
Per-source compiler exceptions retain stack-protector and assembly semantics;
the CTF action converts a private copy and preserves assembly without C types.
See
.Pa tools/build/bazel/CORE-KERNEL-QUALIFICATION.md
for the fresh linked Make reference, captured objects, input hashes and
filename-related differences.
The profile runs no runtime tests and does not qualify a complete native
kernel link, installation, cross-kernel build or performance.
.Pp
The existing format-5 explicit SDK and target inputs also qualify twenty-four
amd64 GENERIC virtual-memory built-in objects after an execution-host upgrade.
Changed host execution dependencies require a new staged SDK receipt;
a previous successful receipt is not silently rebound.
See
.Pa tools/build/bazel/VM-KERNEL-QUALIFICATION.md
for actual compiler inputs, object and CTF comparisons and remaining complete
kernel, runtime, cross-build and controlled performance gates.
.Pp
The existing format-5 native target API also qualifies forty-three selected
amd64 GENERIC IPv4/TCP built-in objects with the selected staged SDK.
See
.Pa tools/build/bazel/IPV4-KERNEL-QUALIFICATION.md
for compiler/input, machine-code, CTF and filename/relocation comparisons.
This does not qualify a complete kernel, cross build, network runtime or
controlled performance result.
.Pp
The normal format-5 host-test API runs
.Va //tools/build/bazel:ctf_semantics_test .
The separate
.Pa tools/build/bazel/ctf-artifact-check.py
inspector accepts explicit captured target/output and Make object inputs.
It preserves raw CTF hashes and checks supported standalone type-layout/schema
metadata and ELF symbol associations without rewriting artifacts.
See
.Pa tools/build/bazel/CTF-SEMANTIC-CHECK.md
for supported formats, negative regressions and ABI/runtime limits.
Kernel artifact profiles retain their restriction against runnable host tests.
.Pp
The format-5 native target API also qualifies twenty-nine selected amd64 GENERIC
IPv6 built-in objects through the existing staged SDK and kernel settings.
See
.Pa tools/build/bazel/IPV6-KERNEL-QUALIFICATION.md
for actual compiler/input, code, CTF and symbolic relocation checks.
Complete kernel, cross-build, network runtime and performance gates
remain open.
.Pp
The existing format-5 native target API builds WPA private utility and crypto
libraries with distinct ordinary and PIE objects, and the passphrase program.
Typed build settings
.Va //tools/build/bazel/config:openssl ,
.Va //tools/build/bazel/config:wpa_supplicant_eapol ,
and
.Va //tools/build/bazel/config:inet6
select the maintained source configuration.
See
.Pa tools/build/bazel/WPA-PRIVATE-QUALIFICATION.md
for five profile builds, actual input and ELF comparisons, installation checks
and seven runtime input/loader methods per profile.
Remaining WPA programs, wireless services, cross builds, full packages and
controlled performance results are separate open gates.

.Pp
The existing format-5 target API also qualifies thirty-two selected amd64
GENERIC network built-in objects, reusing the maintained interface generators
and header providers.
See
.Pa tools/build/bazel/NETWORK-KERNEL-QUALIFICATION.md
for code/CTF, compiler input and symbolic relocation checks.
Complete kernel link, runtime, cross-build and performance gates remain open.
.Pp
The same format-5 WPA settings build the common library with distinct
ordinary and PIE objects and the hostapd control client.
See
.Pa tools/build/bazel/WPA-COMMON-CLIENT-QUALIFICATION.md
for four configuration profiles, immutable artifact and install comparisons,
and safe CLI and loader tests.
The client is assigned to the hostapd package.
Control requests, daemon and wireless behavior, cross builds and complete
system qualification remain separate open gates.

.Pp
The existing format-5 native target API also qualifies thirty-six selected
amd64 GENERIC MII built-ins, with existing device-ID and interface generators.
See
.Pa tools/build/bazel/MII-KERNEL-QUALIFICATION.md
for code/CTF, actual input and relocation checks, and remaining hardware,
complete kernel, cross-build and performance gates.
